window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__ = window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__ || {};
window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__["vol4/09_memory"] = {"key": "vol4/09_memory", "sections": [{"id": "sec-1", "level": 2, "title": "Purpose"}, {"id": "sec-2", "level": 2, "title": "Spatial Belief Persistence and Expiry"}, {"id": "sec-3", "level": 2, "title": "Estimating State from Dated Measurements"}, {"id": "sec-4", "level": 3, "title": "Multi-object tracking and data association"}, {"id": "sec-5", "level": 3, "title": "Applying a delayed observation"}, {"id": "sec-6", "level": 2, "title": "The Spatial Memory Handoff and State Invariants"}, {"id": "sec-7", "level": 2, "title": "State Epochs and Temporal Anchoring"}, {"id": "sec-8", "level": 2, "title": "Coordinate Transform Expiry and Localization Drift"}, {"id": "sec-9", "level": 3, "title": "Localization drift and map frames that jump"}, {"id": "sec-10", "level": 3, "title": "3D dynamic scene graphs"}, {"id": "sec-11", "level": 2, "title": "Spatial Belief Failure Modes and Map Corruption"}, {"id": "sec-12", "level": 2, "title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls"}], "html": "<h1 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h1\">Spatial Memory</h1>\n<h2 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h2\">Purpose</h2>\n<p><em>How long can a robot act on a remembered scene after it loses sight of it?</em></p>\n<p>A person hidden behind a storage rack can keep moving while the robot's map still shows an empty aisle. Forgetting the person loses a constraint on motion; preserving the last position indefinitely turns old evidence into false certainty. The engineer must estimate the scene from measurements that arrive at different times with different errors, retain that estimate through brief sensing gaps, and limit how long it can support a decision. That limit depends on the motion the sensors cannot observe, the error already present, and the clearance the task can tolerate. A stored estimate must therefore carry both its physical age and the conditions under which its uncertainty remains bounded. The permission path needs that information in time to refuse motion whose evidence has expired. Spatial memory connects sensing to the causal boundary by preserving what an observation supports without extending its authority beyond the physical margin.</p>\n<p>{{&lt; pagebreak &gt;}}</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Learning Objectives</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Apply a state estimator's predict and update steps to dated measurements, including late ones at their capture epoch, and test whether its reported covariance matches its actual error</li>\n<li>Track objects across frames without withholding occupancy evidence until their identity is confirmed</li>\n<li>Specify a belief record that carries evidence age, uncertainty growth, and consumer query budgets</li>\n<li>Compose transform chains at a common epoch, and trace how localization drift and loop closure move remembered map cells without renewing their evidence</li>\n<li>Select a spatial representation for a clearance query from the query it answers, the memory reads it costs, and the storage it needs</li>\n<li>Design spatial updates that preserve evidence age and distinguish missing observations from verified free space</li>\n<li>Set belief expiry from bounded uncertainty growth and the task's physical tolerance</li>\n<li>Diagnose frozen transforms, phantom tracks, and unobservable drift that pass freshness and innovation checks</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"sec-2\" class=\"cs-h2\">Spatial Belief Persistence and Expiry</h2>\n<p>On the warehouse mobile manipulator, intermittent observation becomes a hazard at every rack corner. As the robot pivots into a turn, structural uprights occlude the cross aisle from the navigation cameras, and whatever the vision backbone detected seconds earlier survives only in memory. A worker stepping around the corner can enter the corridor that memory still records as clear. Republishing the cached perception frame with a fresh software timestamp cannot replace the missing sensory evidence. The stopping budget assumes obstacles remain within declared clearances, and a person who arrives unseen violates that assumption. The open question is how long the remembered empty aisle may keep authorizing motion after the uprights hide it, and whether any amount of remembering can make the blind crossing safe.</p>\n<p>Every physical AI system acts on a world it observes only intermittently. Perception delivers dated, calibrated observations, yet sensor frames arrive tens of milliseconds apart, a rack upright or a gripper finger can occlude a target surface for seconds, and the controller must keep issuing commands throughout those blind intervals. <strong>Spatial memory</strong> carries physical state across these perceptual gaps. In a conventional computing system, a value in memory remains valid indefinitely because external reality does not alter the digital state. In an embodied system, memory retains an exact representation of the past while the physical world continues to move. A cached state becomes stale not because bits decay, but because unobserved reality drifts away from the recorded value. The central operational question of spatial memory is how long a stored state may continue to authorize machine motion. The answer is a physical deadline rather than an arbitrary software timeout, determined by the initial measurement error, the rate at which unobserved dynamics can expand that error, and the clearance margins permitted by the task. Computing and enforcing that deadline requires five capabilities: an estimator that fuses dated measurements into a calibrated state distribution, a memory record that couples every geometric value to its capture epoch and an error-growth law, coordinate transforms and map structures that preserve those timestamps across aggregation, an invalidation mechanism derived directly from the growth model, and an accounting of unobservable environmental drift.</p>\n<p>↩ Observations dated by capture epoch and carrying calibrated spatial covariances are established in @sec-perception-observation-contract.</p>\n<p>An embodied system cannot operate purely on instantaneous sensor frames, nor can it rely entirely on the implicit context cached inside a learned policy network. Instead, the architecture requires an explicit <strong>belief</strong>: an estimate of physical state projected forward from historical sensory evidence and paired with an explicit model of expanding uncertainty.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Belief State</strong>: @astrom1965optimal showed that a controller that cannot observe its state directly loses nothing by acting on the conditional distribution of that state given everything observed so far, the formalization encountered in @sec-training-privileged-distillation as a partially observable decision problem. However, an abstract mathematical distribution carries no native reference frame, evidence epoch, or error-growth model. A physical runtime requires all three to verify whether a clearance corridor remains valid during a sensor dropout.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Belief state</strong></em> is an estimate of a physical quantity carried forward from dated sensor evidence, expressed within an explicit reference frame, accompanied by the evidence epoch $\\EvidenceEpoch$ of the most recent constraining measurement and a declared error-growth law $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)$ that models uncertainty expansion in the absence of new data.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: A belief maps elapsed time into spatial uncertainty. The safety enforcement path permits motion only while $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)$ remains within the task's clearance envelope. Every belief therefore carries an invalidation horizon measured from $\\EvidenceEpoch$, beyond which the safety controller must reject any dependent trajectory.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: A sensor measurement captures evidence from a single discrete instant. A belief estimates state at later points in time through forward propagation, remaining valid only as long as its growth law accurately bounds physical drift.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Mistaking publication recency for evidence freshness. Re-evaluating or republishing a belief updates software message timestamps, but only a new physical sensor measurement advances the evidence epoch $\\EvidenceEpoch$.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Spatial memory is the second of four proposal-side stages in Part III, positioned between perception and intent. Because it resides on the untrusted proposal side of the proposal boundary, spatial memory can shape proposed trajectories but cannot certify its own freshness. The permission path must independently evaluate the age of the underlying evidence against a local monotonic clock.</p>\n<p>↩ The proposal boundary, above which learned components may only propose and below which the permission path alone admits commands, is established in @sec-boundary-machine-model.</p>\n<p>Learned policies maintain internal history through recurrent states or transformer attention buffers, but these mechanisms keep or drop tokens according to a capacity budget rather than physical persistence. A transformer's context window, and the key-value cache that stores it, holds a bounded number of tokens, so older tokens are evicted as new frames arrive (the streaming-window policies of @sec-memory-episodic). When an obstacle steps behind a pallet rack, the tokens that recorded it can fall out of the window while newly observed features take their place, even though that obstacle continues to obstruct the physical corridor. An internal neural context window is therefore an unreliable sole record of physical state. Spatial memory decouples persistence from policy attention, explicitly preserving the entity, its last observed geometry, and an uncertainty volume that expands for as long as it remains occluded.</p>\n<p>Externalizing physical state from the neural policy also cleanly decouples downstream consumers with divergent computational constraints. The motion planning policy queries a host-resident scene representation to extract a compact working set tailored to its candidate trajectory. In contrast, an isolated permission controller requires a strictly bounded local safety snapshot stored directly in its local memory to evaluate real-time clearance queries. This snapshot retains the raw evidence epochs, spatial uncertainties, geometric coverage bounds, and validity regions from the underlying beliefs. Replicating state across memory buses does not refresh its physical validity. The safety controller neither requires the complete global map nor waits for host queries to complete before deciding whether an ongoing motion remains admissible.</p>\n<p>Internal software consistency does not guarantee physical geometric validity. To prevent an actuator from executing commands based on obsolete assumptions, an embodied memory system must elevate state expiration to an explicit interface event. Every stored belief carries an invalidation horizon, the deadline at which accumulated uncertainty consumes the task's clearance margin (@sec-memory-invalidation-horizon). When that deadline passes, the belief immediately ceases to authorize motion. The permission path rejects any proposal that relies on the expired state and selects a fallback qualified for the remaining evidence, actuation authority, and clearance, such as a controlled stop.</p>\n<p>Every belief and its corresponding invalidation deadline originate in a state estimator. The perception front-end streams discrete, timestamped, coordinate-framed measurements annotated with calibrated sensor covariances. Transforming these intermittent observations into a continuous physical state, backed by uncertainty bounds that faithfully reflect unobserved drift, is the first job of spatial memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h2\">Estimating State from Dated Measurements</h2>\n<p>A single LiDAR return or stereo match localizes the rack-end upright at one isolated instant. A physical safety permission path, however, requires a continuous range estimate that stays current between sweeps, fuses observations across heterogeneous sensors, and produces an uncertainty metric whose statistical confidence matches physical reality. A recursive <strong>state estimator</strong> satisfies all three demands by alternating two complementary operations: a prediction step that projects the state forward through a dynamic motion model, and an update step that incorporates each newly arrived, dated measurement. The discrete Kalman filter [@kalman1960new] provides the classical formulation of this recursion, establishing the mathematical foundation for this chapter [@thrun2005probabilistic, ch. 3; @barshalom2001estimation]. Multi-object tracking and delayed-observation handling apply these two steps directly. The belief decay under occlusion examined in @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion corresponds to the prediction step executing without measurement updates, while the observability limits of @sec-memory-state-estimation-bounds define the state dimensions that no update stream can reconstruct. The mechanics are clearest on a scalar, the remaining distance $\\RemainingUprightDistance$ from the mobile base to the rack-end upright, so the two operations are worked through on it before the notation extends to full state vectors.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Kalman Filter</strong>: Stanley Schmidt's group at NASA Ames took up Kálmán's paper shortly after it appeared and, for circumlunar navigation studies, linearized the nonlinear measurement equations about the current estimate [@smith1962application], the reformulation now called the extended Kalman filter. MIT's Instrumentation Laboratory then carried the filter into Apollo's on-board navigation in a square-root form suited to a computer with a short word length [@mcgee1985discovery]. The recursion fit that computer because it keeps only the latest estimate and its covariance, never the measurement history, and the same property lets spatial memory hand a consumer one dated record instead of a log to replay.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ ONE-DIMENSIONAL KALMAN FILTER ON THE RACK-END DISTANCE\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-perception-state-estimation and the consistency checks of\n# │          @sec-memory-estimator-consistency.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Predict the remaining distance to the rack-end upright over one\n# │          LiDAR sweep, update it with the OS1-64, fuse an illustrative stereo\n# │          range, and show what an arrival-stamped stereo range does.\n# │ Show:    prior 20.0 mm -&gt; 20.6 mm after prediction; LiDAR gain 0.32,\n# │          posterior 17.0 mm; stereo sigma 3.8 mm at 1.10 m, gain 0.95,\n# │          posterior 3.7 mm; misdating bias 38.0 mm, about 10 posterior\n# │          standard deviations; expected NIS 6.25.\n# │ How:     scalar predict (P + (sigma_v dt)^2) and update (g = P / (P + R));\n# │          OS1-64 sweep period and worst-case precision from the registry;\n# │          the illustrative stereo rig of @sec-perception-error-clearance at\n# │          AISLE.d_clear; the arrival-stamp travel of @sec-perception-handoff\n# │          (t_exp/2 + t_readout + t_transport + t_dma at the drive limit)\n# │          as the misdating bias. All three inputs are re-derived here from\n# │          the registry or re-loaded with guards matching Ch8&#x27;s cells.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_latency, fmt_length, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass AisleKalman:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Scalar predict, update, two-sensor fusion, and a misdated update on the rack-end distance.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    FUSION = Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndRangeFusion  # category: illustrative (prior, speed disturbance, stereo rig; MLSysIM scenario)\n    sigma_prior = FUSION.prior_sigma  # category: illustrative (posterior from the previous sweep)\n    sigma_v = FUSION.speed_sigma  # category: illustrative (odometry speed error, e.g. wheel slip)\n    dt = (1 / RM.lidar.scan_rate).to(millisecond)  # category: datasheet (one 20 Hz sweep; LidarSweep.t_sweep in @sec-perception)\n    sigma_lidar = RM.lidar.range_precision_max  # category: datasheet (OS1 precision, worst case, 1 sigma)\n    bias_r = RM.lidar.range_accuracy  # category: datasheet (OS1 accuracy, a bias the filter cannot represent)\n    z = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative (range to the upright)\n    tau_recv = FUSION.age.result.arrival_offset.to(millisecond)  # category: derived (ArrivalStampOffset.tau_recv in @sec-perception-handoff)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    R = FUSION.result\n    bias_misdate = R.misdating_bias  # arrival-stamp travel at the drive limit\n    sigma_pred = R.predicted_sigma\n    g_lidar = R.first_gain\n    sigma_lidar_post = R.first_sigma\n    sigma_stereo = R.second_sensor_sigma\n    g_stereo = R.second_gain\n    sigma_fused = R.fused_sigma\n    bias_post = R.misdated_shift\n    bias_in_sigmas = R.misdated_shift_sigmas\n    nis_expected = R.nis_expected\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(dt.magnitude - 50.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Sweep period must match the perception chapter&#x27;s 50 ms: {dt}&quot;)\n    check(abs(tau_recv.magnitude - 26.6) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Arrival offset must match the perception chapter&#x27;s 26.6 ms: {tau_recv}&quot;)\n    check(abs(bias_misdate.magnitude - 39.9) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Arrival-stamp travel must match the perception chapter&#x27;s 39.9 mm: {bias_misdate}&quot;)\n    check(abs(sigma_pred.magnitude - 20.6) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected predicted sigma: {sigma_pred}&quot;)\n    check(abs(g_lidar - 0.32) &lt; 0.005, f&quot;Unexpected LiDAR gain: {g_lidar}&quot;)\n    check(abs(sigma_lidar_post.magnitude - 17.0) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected LiDAR posterior: {sigma_lidar_post}&quot;)\n    check(sigma_lidar_post &lt; min(sigma_pred, sigma_lidar), &quot;Posterior must be tighter than either input&quot;)\n    check(abs(sigma_stereo.magnitude - 3.8) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected stereo sigma: {sigma_stereo}&quot;)\n    check(abs(g_stereo - 0.95) &lt; 0.005, f&quot;Unexpected stereo gain: {g_stereo}&quot;)\n    check(abs(sigma_fused.magnitude - 3.7) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected fused sigma: {sigma_fused}&quot;)\n    check(abs(bias_post.magnitude - 38.0) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected misdating bias: {bias_post}&quot;)\n    check(9.5 &lt; bias_in_sigmas &lt; 11.0, f&quot;Prose says the bias is about ten posterior sigmas: {bias_in_sigmas}&quot;)\n    check(abs(nis_expected - 6.25) &lt; 0.05, f&quot;Unexpected expected NIS: {nis_expected}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    sigma_prior_str = fmt_length(sigma_prior, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    sigma_v_str = fmt(sigma_v.to(meter / second).magnitude, precision=1)\n    dt_str = fmt_latency(dt, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    sigma_w_str = fmt_length(R.process_sigma, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    sigma_pred_str = fmt_length(sigma_pred, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    sigma_lidar_str = fmt_length(sigma_lidar, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    g_lidar_str = fmt(g_lidar, precision=2)\n    sigma_lidar_post_str = fmt_length(sigma_lidar_post, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    z_str = fmt_length(z.to(meter), unit=meter, precision=2)\n    sigma_stereo_str = fmt_length(sigma_stereo, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    g_stereo_str = fmt(g_stereo, precision=2)\n    sigma_fused_str = fmt_length(sigma_fused, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    bias_misdate_str = fmt_length(bias_misdate.to(millimeter), unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    bias_r_str = fmt_length(bias_r, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    bias_post_str = fmt_length(bias_post, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    bias_in_sigmas_str = fmt(bias_in_sigmas, precision=1)\n    nis_expected_str = fmt(nis_expected, precision=2)\n]```\n\nThe **predict** step advances the state estimate across time without incorporating new external evidence. Between two successive LiDAR sweeps separated by $\\NominalFrameInterval$ = `[AisleKalman.dt_str]`, wheel odometry (@sec-memory-localization-slam) indicates that the mobile base closed the distance by $\\BaseOdometrySpeed\\,\\NominalFrameInterval$, yielding the prior prediction $\\RemainingDistanceEstimate^- = \\RemainingDistanceEstimate - \\BaseOdometrySpeed\\,\\NominalFrameInterval$. Odometry, however, carries inherent uncertainty. An illustrative speed disturbance of `[AisleKalman.sigma_v_str]` m/s (from minor wheel slip on polished concrete, for example) accumulates `[AisleKalman.sigma_w_str]` of standard deviation over that single sweep interval. Because independent errors combine in quadrature, $\\DistanceEstimateStandardDeviation^2_{-} = \\DistanceEstimateStandardDeviation^2 + \\OdometryNoiseStandardDeviation^2$, an initial `[AisleKalman.sigma_prior_str]` uncertainty expands to `[AisleKalman.sigma_pred_str]`. Without new measurement constraints, prediction inevitably inflates state uncertainty.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ IMU ERROR GROWTH WITHOUT A RANGE UPDATE</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: The IMU paragraph of @sec-perception-state-estimation.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Show from the BMI088 datasheet why white noise and constant offsets</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          grow differently under integration, and which one limits coasting.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    over 1 s: 0.014 deg from gyro noise vs 1 deg from the zero-rate</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          offset (19.2 mm at the rack end); 0.9 mm from accelerometer noise</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          vs 98 mm from the zero-g offset, about twice delta_loc.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     angle = N_g sqrt(T); heading = b_g T; position from white</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          acceleration noise = N_a sqrt(T^3 / 3); position from offset =</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          b_a T^2 / 2. BMI088 datasheet rev 1.9 (Jan 2024), Tables 4-5,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          typical values, from the registry (Sensors.IMU.Bosch_BMI088).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>import math from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_int, fmt_length, check</p>\n<p>AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class ImuErrorGrowth: \"\"\"Attitude and position error of the BMI088 integrated open loop.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>g0 = 1.0 * standard_gravity  # category: constant (standard gravity) imu = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator.imu  # category: datasheet (registry: Bosch BMI088) n_gyro = imu.gyro_noise_density.to(\"deg/s/Hz<strong>0.5\").magnitude  # category: datasheet (BMI088 gyro rate-noise density) b_gyro = imu.gyro_zero_rate_offset.to(\"deg/s\").magnitude  # category: datasheet (BMI088 typical zero-rate offset, offset cancellation off) n_acc = (imu.accel_noise_density / g0).to(\"Hz</strong>-0.5\").magnitude  # category: datasheet (BMI088 accel noise density X/Y, in g/sqrt(Hz)) b_acc = (imu.accel_zero_g_offset / g0).to(\"dimensionless\").magnitude  # category: datasheet (BMI088 typical zero-g offset, in g) t_coast = Scenarios.WarehouseImuOneSecondCoast.duration  # category: illustrative (interval without a range update) z = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative (rack-end distance) delta_loc = AISLE.delta_loc  # category: illustrative (along-travel localization allowance)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>T = t_coast.to(second).magnitude R = Scenarios.WarehouseImuOneSecondCoast.result ang_noise_deg = R.heading_noise.to(\"deg\").magnitude ang_bias_deg = R.heading_bias.to(\"deg\").magnitude lat_bias = R.lateral_bias pos_noise = R.position_noise pos_bias = R.position_bias bias_over_loc = R.bias_over_reference</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(ang_noise_deg - 0.014) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Unexpected gyro-noise angle: {ang_noise_deg}\") check(abs(lat_bias.magnitude - 19.2) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected lateral error from gyro offset: {lat_bias}\") check(abs(pos_noise.magnitude - 0.91) &lt; 0.01, f\"Unexpected position error from accel noise: {pos_noise}\") check(abs(pos_bias.magnitude - 98.1) &lt; 0.05, f\"Unexpected position error from accel offset: {pos_bias}\") check(1.8 &lt; bias_over_loc &lt; 2.1, f\"Prose says the offset term is about twice delta_loc: {bias_over_loc}\") check(pos_bias &gt; 50 * pos_noise, \"Prose says offsets, not white noise, limit coasting\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>n_gyro_str = fmt(n_gyro, precision=3) b_gyro_str = fmt(b_gyro, precision=0) n_acc_str = fmt_int(round(n_acc <em> 1e6)) b_acc_str = fmt_int(round(b_acc </em> 1e3)) t_coast_str = fmt(T, precision=0) ang_noise_str = fmt(ang_noise_deg, precision=3) ang_bias_str = fmt(ang_bias_deg, precision=0) z_str = fmt_length(z.to(meter), unit=meter, precision=2) lat_bias_str = fmt_length(lat_bias, unit=millimeter, precision=1) pos_noise_str = fmt_length(pos_noise, unit=millimeter, precision=1) pos_bias_str = fmt_length(pos_bias, unit=millimeter, precision=1) delta_loc_str = fmt_length(delta_loc.to(millimeter), unit=millimeter, precision=0) ]```</p>\n<p>The robot's inertial measurement unit (IMU) is the other source of high-rate data for the predict step, and its datasheet shows why dead reckoning cannot sustain an accurate estimate on its own. A sensor's noise density states how much random error it adds per square root of measurement bandwidth, so white noise of density $\\SensorNoiseDensity$ integrated for $\\InertialIntegrationTime$ seconds spreads by $\\SensorNoiseDensity\\sqrt{\\InertialIntegrationTime}$, while a fixed offset integrates in proportion to $\\InertialIntegrationTime$. The Bosch BMI088 gyroscope has a rate-noise density of <code>[ImuErrorGrowth.n_gyro_str]</code> °/s/$\\sqrt{\\text{Hz}}$ alongside an uncalibrated zero-rate bias of <code>[ImuErrorGrowth.b_gyro_str]</code> °/s. Its accelerometer has a noise density of <code>[ImuErrorGrowth.n_acc_str]</code> µg/$\\sqrt{\\text{Hz}}$ and an uncalibrated zero-g offset of <code>[ImuErrorGrowth.b_acc_str]</code> mg [@bosch2024bmi088]. Integrating these sensor signals accumulates error, and stochastic noise compounds along a different trajectory than deterministic bias. Broadband white noise integrates in variance, causing orientation uncertainty to grow with the square root of time. Across <code>[ImuErrorGrowth.t_coast_str]</code> s without external range correction, it accumulates only <code>[ImuErrorGrowth.ang_noise_str]</code>°. In contrast, a constant bias integrates linearly, producing heading drift proportional to time that reaches <code>[ImuErrorGrowth.ang_bias_str]</code>° over that same second. At <code>[ImuErrorGrowth.z_str]</code>, this angular error projects into <code>[ImuErrorGrowth.lat_bias_str]</code> of lateral displacement for the rack upright. Because acceleration undergoes double integration to give position, its bias scales quadratically with elapsed time ($\\tfrac{1}{2} \\AccelerometerBias \\InertialIntegrationTime^2$). The bias generates <code>[ImuErrorGrowth.pos_bias_str]</code> of drift after one second, about twice the <code>[ImuErrorGrowth.delta_loc_str]</code> localization allowance. Integrated accelerometer white noise contributes only <code>[ImuErrorGrowth.pos_noise_str]</code> (its position variance grows with the cube of time, a law derived in @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion). In this illustrative uncalibrated case, sensor offsets dominate broadband noise and limit how long the inertial system can coast uncorrected. Practical estimators therefore augment the state vector to estimate these biases online, an approach that succeeds only while the available measurements and executed motion constrain the relevant bias components (the observability condition of @sec-memory-state-estimation-bounds). The estimators also use the underlying noise densities to parameterize the process noise, the matrix generalization of the odometry variance $\\OdometryNoiseStandardDeviation^2$ that the general filter writes as $\\ProcessNoiseCovariance$. Because these offsets drift with temperature and vary across physical chips, operating parameters must be identified through in-situ stationary calibration on the physical platform rather than taken from static datasheet ratings.</p>\n<p>The <strong>update</strong> step incorporates a fresh scalar measurement $\\DistanceMeasurement$ characterized by variance $\\DepthStandardDeviation^2$. The updated posterior is a linear combination, $\\RemainingDistanceEstimate = (1-\\ScalarKalmanGain)\\,\\RemainingDistanceEstimate^- + \\ScalarKalmanGain\\,\\DistanceMeasurement$. Assuming the prior prediction error and measurement noise remain statistically independent, the posterior variance evaluates to $(1-\\ScalarKalmanGain)^2 \\DistanceEstimateStandardDeviation^2_{-} + \\ScalarKalmanGain^2 \\DepthStandardDeviation^2$. Minimizing this variance with respect to the blending weight $\\ScalarKalmanGain$ produces the optimal Kalman gain, which balances each information source inversely against its relative uncertainty: $$\\ScalarKalmanGain = \\frac{\\DistanceEstimateStandardDeviation^2_{-}}{\\DistanceEstimateStandardDeviation^2_{-} + \\DepthStandardDeviation^2}, \\qquad \\RemainingDistanceEstimate = \\RemainingDistanceEstimate^- + \\ScalarKalmanGain\\,(\\DistanceMeasurement - \\RemainingDistanceEstimate^-), \\qquad \\DistanceEstimateStandardDeviation^2 = (1 - \\ScalarKalmanGain)\\,\\DistanceEstimateStandardDeviation^2_{-}$$ The residual $\\DistanceMeasurement - \\RemainingDistanceEstimate^-$ constitutes the <strong>innovation</strong>, the new information supplied by the measurement that the forward projection did not capture. Given the Ouster OS1-64's worst-case datasheet range precision of <code>[AisleKalman.sigma_lidar_str]</code>, the computed gain is <code>[AisleKalman.g_lidar_str]</code>. The estimator shifts approximately one-third of the way toward the raw LiDAR reading, contracting the posterior standard deviation to <code>[AisleKalman.sigma_lidar_post_str]</code>, a modeled spread tighter than either input alone. Because published precision specifications typically reflect ideal calibration conditions rather than real-time operating modes, this sensor variance must be verified at the configured operational scan rate. The sensor's separate <code>[AisleKalman.bias_r_str]</code> systematic accuracy offset represents a structural bias that zero-mean Gaussian models cannot accommodate; offline calibration must eliminate this offset, or the downstream safety clearance margin must absorb it explicitly.</p>\n<p>Fusing a second sensor executes as a sequential update step. The illustrative stereo camera rig analyzed in @sec-perception-error-clearance achieves $\\DepthStandardDeviation =$ <code>[AisleKalman.sigma_stereo_str]</code> at the <code>[AisleKalman.z_str]</code> rack-end distance. Fusing this measurement yields a stereo gain of <code>[AisleKalman.g_stereo_str]</code>, shrinking the final fused standard deviation to <code>[AisleKalman.sigma_fused_str]</code>. For statistically independent measurements captured at the same physical instant, update ordering is commutative: each observation contributes an information increment, and their underlying precision matrices (inverse variances) sum directly.</p>\n<p>A misdated measurement breaks this estimation algebra silently. Suppose a stereo depth observation is tagged with its host arrival timestamp, which @sec-perception-handoff shows hides exposure, readout, and transport delay, rather than with its physical exposure epoch. The filter erroneously pairs this observation with a robot pose <code>[AisleKalman.bias_misdate_str]</code> further along the aisle than where the photons actually struck the sensor. That pose offset makes the measured range read systematically long by the same spatial displacement, causing the update to drag the state estimate <code>[AisleKalman.bias_post_str]</code> away from the physical obstacle while reporting the same deceptively tight <code>[AisleKalman.sigma_fused_str]</code> standard deviation. The resulting physical error spans approximately <code>[AisleKalman.bias_in_sigmas_str]</code> reported standard deviations in the hazardous direction, yet the filter's covariance update detects nothing amiss because algebraic variance reduction depends entirely on modeled sensor noise rather than measurement residuals. Corrupt timestamps are therefore far more dangerous than dropped sensor frames. Dropping the stereo frame instead would leave the posterior uncertainty at <code>[AisleKalman.sigma_lidar_post_str]</code>, imprecise but statistically honest.</p>\n<p>Set side by side on one range-error scale (@fig-08-kalman-predict-update), the stages read as a single story. Prediction widens the bar from <code>[AisleKalman.sigma_prior_str]</code> to <code>[AisleKalman.sigma_pred_str]</code>, each update narrows it below both of its inputs to a final <code>[AisleKalman.sigma_fused_str]</code>, and the misdated column reports that same tight spread while its center sits <code>[AisleKalman.bias_post_str]</code> from the true range, the only bar that does not contain the truth.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ SCALAR KALMAN PREDICT AND UPDATE (FIGURE)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @fig-08-kalman-predict-update in @sec-perception-state-estimation.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Show prediction widening and each update narrowing the estimate,\n# │          and a misdated update that stays narrow while biased.\n# │ Show:    sigma 20.0 -&gt; 20.6 -&gt; 17.0 -&gt; 3.7 mm; LiDAR and stereo input\n# │          spreads; the 38.0 mm misdating bias at the fused spread.\n# │ How:     Every value reads AisleKalman (no literals).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import viz\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import millimeter\n\nAK = AisleKalman\n_mm = lambda q: q.to(millimeter).magnitude\nPETROL = &quot;#007A87&quot;  # measured (theme-colors.tex)\nCRIMSON = &quot;#A51C30&quot;  # consequence (theme-colors.tex)\nINK, SOFT, PALE = &quot;#1A202C&quot;, &quot;#4A5568&quot;, &quot;#A0AEC0&quot;\n\nsteps = [\n    (&quot;prior&quot;, 0.0, _mm(AK.sigma_prior), AK.sigma_prior_str, None),\n    (&quot;predict&quot;, 0.0, _mm(AK.sigma_pred), AK.sigma_pred_str, None),\n    (&quot;LiDAR\\nupdate&quot;, 0.0, _mm(AK.sigma_lidar_post), AK.sigma_lidar_post_str, (_mm(AK.sigma_lidar), AK.sigma_lidar_str, AK.g_lidar_str)),\n    (&quot;stereo\\nupdate&quot;, 0.0, _mm(AK.sigma_fused), AK.sigma_fused_str, (_mm(AK.sigma_stereo), AK.sigma_stereo_str, AK.g_stereo_str)),\n]\n\nfig, ax, COLORS, plt = viz.setup_plot(figsize=(6.2, 3.4))\nax.grid(False)\nax.spines[&quot;top&quot;].set_visible(False)\nax.spines[&quot;right&quot;].set_visible(False)\nax.axhline(0, color=PALE, lw=0.8, ls=&quot;--&quot;, zorder=0)\n\nfor i, (name, mu, sig, lab, meas) in enumerate(steps):\n    ax.errorbar(i, mu, yerr=sig, fmt=&quot;o&quot;, color=PETROL, ms=3.5, capsize=4, lw=1.6, zorder=3)\n    # sigma labels centred over their own bar so none runs into the next column&#x27;s input bar\n    # a label that would meet this column&#x27;s taller input bar starts right of it instead\n    tall_input = meas is not None and meas[0] &gt; sig + 4.2\n    ax.text(i - 0.12 if tall_input else i, mu + sig + 1.2, f&quot;σ = {lab}&quot;, ha=&quot;left&quot; if tall_input else &quot;center&quot;, va=&quot;bottom&quot;, fontsize=9, color=INK)\n    if meas is not None:\n        s_m, s_lab, g_lab = meas\n        ax.errorbar(i - 0.42, 0.0, yerr=s_m, fmt=&quot;none&quot;, color=PALE, capsize=3, lw=1.2, zorder=2)\n        ax.text(i - 0.42, -s_m - 1.2, f&quot;input {s_lab}&quot;, ha=&quot;center&quot;, va=&quot;top&quot;, fontsize=9, color=SOFT)\n        # when the input and posterior bars end level, the gain label drops a line below the input label\n        gain_y = -sig - 1.2 - (5.5 if abs(s_m - sig) &lt; 5 else 0.0)\n        ax.text(i + 0.1, gain_y, f&quot;gain {g_lab}&quot;, ha=&quot;left&quot;, va=&quot;top&quot;, fontsize=9, color=SOFT)\n\n# Misdated stereo update: same reported spread, biased mean\nxm = len(steps) + 0.2\nb = _mm(AK.bias_post)\nax.errorbar(xm, b, yerr=_mm(AK.sigma_fused), fmt=&quot;s&quot;, color=CRIMSON, ms=3.5, capsize=4, lw=1.6, zorder=3)\nax.annotate(&quot;&quot;, xy=(xm + 0.3, b), xytext=(xm + 0.3, 0), arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;&lt;-&gt;&quot;, color=CRIMSON, lw=0.8))\nax.text(xm + 0.38, b / 2, f&quot;bias\\n{AK.bias_post_str}\\n≈ {AK.bias_in_sigmas_str} σ&quot;, ha=&quot;left&quot;, va=&quot;center&quot;, fontsize=9, color=CRIMSON)\nax.text(xm, b + _mm(AK.sigma_fused) + 1.2, f&quot;σ = {AK.sigma_fused_str}&quot;, ha=&quot;center&quot;, va=&quot;bottom&quot;, fontsize=9, color=INK)\nax.axvline(len(steps) - 0.4, color=PALE, lw=0.6)\n\nax.set_xticks(list(range(len(steps))) + [xm])\nax.set_xticklabels([s[0] for s in steps] + [&quot;misdated\\nstereo&quot;], fontsize=9)\nax.set_xlim(-0.7, xm + 1.1)\nax.set_ylabel(&quot;estimate minus true range (mm)&quot;, fontsize=9)\nax.tick_params(axis=&quot;y&quot;, labelsize=9)\nax.set_ylim(-_mm(AK.sigma_lidar) - 8, max(b + 12, _mm(AK.sigma_lidar) + 6))\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\nplt.close()\n]```\n:::\n\nThe recursion generalizes from a scalar distance to an $\\StateDimension$-dimensional state vector $\\StateEstimate$ with error covariance $\\EstimationErrorCovariance \\in \\mathbb{R}^{\\StateDimension \\times \\StateDimension}$. It parameterizes system dynamics through a state transition matrix $\\StateTransitionMatrix$, process noise covariance $\\ProcessNoiseCovariance$, an observation matrix $\\MeasurementMatrix$ projecting state space into measurement space, and measurement noise covariance $\\MeasurementNoiseCovariance$ (@eq-percep-kf-predict and @eq-percep-kf-update):\n$$\\PredictedStateEstimate = \\StateTransitionMatrix\\,\\PreviousUpdatedStateEstimate, \\qquad \\PredictedErrorCovariance = \\StateTransitionMatrix\\,\\PreviousUpdatedErrorCovariance\\StateTransitionMatrix^\\top + \\ProcessNoiseCovariance$$ {#eq-percep-kf-predict}\n$$\\MeasurementInnovation_k = \\MeasurementVector_k - \\MeasurementMatrix\\PredictedStateEstimate, \\quad \\InnovationCovariance_k = \\MeasurementMatrix\\PredictedErrorCovariance\\MeasurementMatrix^\\top + \\MeasurementNoiseCovariance, \\quad \\KalmanGain_{k} = \\PredictedErrorCovariance\\MeasurementMatrix^\\top\\InnovationCovariance_k^{-1}, \\quad \\UpdatedStateEstimate = \\PredictedStateEstimate + \\KalmanGain_{k}\\MeasurementInnovation_k, \\quad \\UpdatedErrorCovariance = (\\IdentityMatrix - \\KalmanGain_{k}\\MeasurementMatrix)\\,\\PredictedErrorCovariance$$ {#eq-percep-kf-update}\nIn this formulation, $\\MeasurementInnovation_k$ represents the innovation vector, $\\InnovationCovariance_k$ denotes innovation covariance, and $\\KalmanGain_{k}$ signifies the Kalman gain matrix (designated $\\KalmanGain_{k}$ to reserve $\\Glyph{\\mathbf{K}}$ for camera intrinsic calibration matrices). The preceding scalar analysis corresponds to the degenerate setting $\\StateTransitionMatrix = \\MeasurementMatrix = 1$, with the odometry displacement $-\\BaseOdometrySpeed\\,\\NominalFrameInterval$ entering as a known control input. @Eq-percep-kf-predict omits that input term for brevity, and the term shifts the mean without changing $\\EstimationErrorCovariance$. Under linear dynamics and additive Gaussian noise, this closed-form update yields the provably optimal minimum-variance unbiased estimate. If nonlinearities or non-Gaussian noise corrupt the process, the update functions merely as an empirical linear estimator whose output covariance requires experimental validation.\n\nPhysical robotic sensors are inherently nonlinear functions of platform state. A LiDAR range measurement to the rack upright evaluates to $\\UprightRange = \\|\\UprightPosition - \\BasePosition\\|$, while a camera records target bearings through the nonlinear pinhole projection detailed in @eq-percep-pinhole-projection. The **extended Kalman filter (EKF)** [@smith1962application] adapts @eq-percep-kf-update to nonlinear regimes by evaluating the measurement function $\\MeasurementFunction(\\PredictedStateEstimate)$ directly and substituting the linear projection $\\MeasurementMatrix$ with the measurement Jacobian $\\nabla_{\\EstimatorState} \\MeasurementFunction$ evaluated at the current state prediction. For a scalar range reading, this Jacobian with respect to platform base position reduces to the unit line-of-sight vector directed from the upright toward the base. This Taylor-series linearization is a first-order approximation. Substantial curvature across the prior distribution, common when estimating bearings to nearby obstacles or operating under broad initial uncertainty, can therefore cause the EKF to underestimate its true estimation error [@julier2004unscented]. This failure mode makes the empirical consistency evaluations of @sec-memory-estimator-consistency safety requirements rather than optional checks.\n\nThe **unscented Kalman filter (UKF)** developed by @julier1997new bypasses analytical Jacobians through deterministic sampling. For an $\\StateDimension$-dimensional state vector, the UKF instantiates $2\\StateDimension+1$ sigma points structured around the matrix square root of $\\EstimationErrorCovariance$, propagates each sample point through the true nonlinear observation function, and reconstructs the transformed posterior mean and covariance [@julier2004unscented]. This transformation captures higher-order curvature effects on the mean that the EKF truncates, requiring $2\\StateDimension+1$ functional evaluations per cycle. The UKF nevertheless approximates the posterior as a unimodal Gaussian, leaving it subject to identical qualification and consistency standards.\n\nBoth filtering frameworks, and the multi-object tracking and out-of-sequence replay built on them, depend on every sensor measurement being indexed by its true capture epoch. The misdated stereo benchmark illustrates the breakdown when this assumption fails. While reported uncertainty remained artificially compressed, true physical error drifted across ten standard deviations. A reported covariance is therefore an engineering hypothesis that must be systematically validated against real-world data.\n\n### Testing whether the covariance is honest {#sec-memory-estimator-consistency}\n\nCatching an overconfident covariance like the misdated stereo filter&#x27;s requires two complementary statistical tests: an offline qualification metric evaluated against ground truth before deployment, and an online runtime monitor evaluated directly against incoming measurement streams during operation. Both formulations use a zero-mean Gaussian error model with the correct nonsingular covariance. Whitening the error under these assumptions produces independent standard normal coordinates; their squared norm, rather than the error vector itself, follows a chi-squared distribution. Matching covariance alone does not establish Gaussian tails.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ NEES AND NIS CONSISTENCY NUMBERS</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-memory-estimator-consistency (moved from the perception</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          chapter's error-qualification cell with the estimator).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Give the acceptance band for mean NEES over a finite campaign and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the single-update NIS gates that one misdated update must exceed.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    mean-NEES band 0.65 to 1.43 (N = 50, scalar, 95 percent); NIS</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          gates 3.84 and 6.63, which one misdated update exceeds with</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          probability 0.63 and 0.39.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.RackEndMisdatedUpdateConsistency: chi-squared quantiles</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          for the band and gates; noncentral chi-squared survival for the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          misdated update's NIS (physics/statistics.py).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_int, fmt_percent, check</p>\n<p>class EstimatorConsistency: \"\"\"Acceptance bands and single-update gates for testing an estimator's covariance.\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>CONSISTENCY = Scenarios.RackEndMisdatedUpdateConsistency  # category: chosen (campaign size, levels; MLSysIM scenario) n_runs = CONSISTENCY.runs  # category: chosen (qualification runs with ground truth) n_dim = CONSISTENCY.dimension  # category: derived (scalar rack-end distance) conf = CONSISTENCY.confidence  # category: chosen (two-sided acceptance level) nis_expected = AisleKalman.nis_expected  # category: derived (misdated stereo update)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>R = CONSISTENCY.result nees_lo, nees_hi = R.band_low, R.band_high nis_95, nis_99 = R.gates p_catch_95, p_catch_99 = R.detection</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(nees_lo - 0.65) &lt; 0.005 and abs(nees_hi - 1.43) &lt; 0.005, f\"Unexpected NEES band: {nees_lo}, {nees_hi}\") check(abs(nis_95 - 3.84) &lt; 0.005 and abs(nis_99 - 6.63) &lt; 0.005, f\"Unexpected NIS gates: {nis_95}, {nis_99}\") check(0.55 &lt; p_catch_95 &lt; 0.7 and 0.35 &lt; p_catch_99 &lt; 0.5, f\"Unexpected single-update detection probabilities: {p_catch_95}, {p_catch_99}\") check(nis_expected &gt; nees_hi, \"Prose says the averaged NIS falls far outside the band\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">margin_misdated_update_gate_envelope.svg fig-alt hand-types 3.84, 6.63, 0.63, and 0.39</h1>\n<p>check(round(nis_95, 2) == 3.84 and round(nis_99, 2) == 6.63, \"Margin fig-alt gate labels are stale.\") check(round(p_catch_95, 2) == 0.63 and round(p_catch_99, 2) == 0.39, \"Margin fig-alt detection probabilities are stale.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>n_runs_str = fmt_int(n_runs) conf_pct_str = fmt_percent(conf, precision=0, style=\"prose\") nees_lo_str = fmt(nees_lo, precision=2) nees_hi_str = fmt(nees_hi, precision=2) nis_95_str = fmt(nis_95, precision=2) nis_99_str = fmt(nis_99, precision=2) nis_expected_str = fmt(nis_expected, precision=2) p_catch_95_str = fmt(p_catch_95, precision=2) p_catch_99_str = fmt(p_catch_99, precision=2) ]```</p>\n<p>A filter achieves statistical consistency when its reported covariance matches the dispersion of its actual state errors. When offline ground truth is accessible (a laser-tracker survey of the rack upright, say, with an optical motion-capture reference for the base pose), the normalized estimation error squared (NEES), $\\NeesStatistic_k = (\\EstimatorState_k - \\StateEstimate_k)^\\top \\EstimationErrorCovariance_k^{-1} (\\EstimatorState_k - \\StateEstimate_k)$, evaluates the system. Under that zero-mean Gaussian model with the correct nonsingular covariance, whitening the error of an $\\StateDimension$-dimensional state forms a vector of $\\StateDimension$ independent standard normal variates. Their sum of squares follows a chi-squared distribution with $\\StateDimension$ degrees of freedom and theoretical mean $\\StateDimension$. Evaluated across $\\TrialCount$ statistically independent experimental trials, the scaled sample mean $\\TrialCount\\MeanNeesStatistic$ follows a chi-squared distribution with $\\TrialCount \\StateDimension$ degrees of freedom [@barshalom2001estimation]. For the scalar rack-end distance over <code>[EstimatorConsistency.n_runs_str]</code> experimental trials, the two-sided <code>[EstimatorConsistency.conf_pct_str]</code> acceptance interval for sample mean NEES $\\MeanNeesStatistic$ spans from <code>[EstimatorConsistency.nees_lo_str]</code> to <code>[EstimatorConsistency.nees_hi_str]</code>. An empirical NEES above this band flags an overconfident filter, the safety-critical defect, because its reported uncertainty understates the error that clearance buffers must absorb. Conversely, an average falling below the lower bound indicates an underconfident filter whose inflated covariance unnecessarily throttles vehicle operating speed.</p>\n<p>While absolute ground truth remains unavailable during live deployment, the estimator can continuously audit its measurement innovations. The normalized innovation squared (NIS), $\\MeasurementInnovation_k^\\top\\InnovationCovariance_k^{-1}\\MeasurementInnovation_k$, follows a chi-squared distribution with $\\MeasurementDimension$ degrees of freedom for an $\\MeasurementDimension$-dimensional measurement vector under valid model assumptions, so its expected value is $\\MeasurementDimension$. The same statistic also serves as the multi-object data-association gate. A bias $\\InnovationBias$ in the innovation adds $\\InnovationBias^2/\\InnovationVariance$ to that expectation, where $\\InnovationVariance$ is the innovation variance. For the misdated stereo observation in @fig-08-kalman-predict-update, $\\InnovationBias$ is the <code>[AisleKalman.bias_misdate_str]</code> arrival-stamp travel, and the expected NIS rises from 1 to <code>[EstimatorConsistency.nis_expected_str]</code>. A single misdated update's NIS then follows a noncentral chi-squared distribution, the bias setting its offset and the sensor noise its spread. One update therefore breaches the 95 percent validation gate of <code>[EstimatorConsistency.nis_95_str]</code> with probability <code>[EstimatorConsistency.p_catch_95_str]</code>, and trips the 99 percent threshold of <code>[EstimatorConsistency.nis_99_str]</code> only <code>[EstimatorConsistency.p_catch_99_str]</code> of the time. Single-frame residual gating therefore admits a substantial fraction of corrupted measurements. Averaging independent innovations reduces sampling variation and can expose a persistent residual bias. For <code>[EstimatorConsistency.n_runs_str]</code> independent scalar innovations under the valid model, the mean NIS has the same <code>[EstimatorConsistency.nees_lo_str]</code> to <code>[EstimatorConsistency.nees_hi_str]</code> acceptance band derived for NEES. Independent repetitions of the worked misdated update average near <code>[EstimatorConsistency.nis_expected_str]</code>, far above it. A sequential filter can instead absorb a systematic dating error into its state, reducing later innovations; correlated innovations also invalidate this nominal band. A runtime sliding-window NIS supervisor therefore detects dating errors that persist in the innovations, but cannot replace capture-epoch validation. Once an estimator's covariance and innovations are calibrated on a single landmark, the next challenge is maintaining separate filters across multiple moving objects without confusing their identities.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h3\">Multi-object tracking and data association</h3>\n<p>Maintaining separate filters across multiple moving objects adds an immediate architectural constraint: a perception pipeline cannot withhold an obstacle detection until a multi-frame tracker confirms its persistent identity, or a worker stepping into the aisle remains invisible to collision avoidance during the confirmation window. A fresh occupied-region claim must therefore constrain vehicle motion immediately, before perception systems identify the specific obstacle class or confirm a track. Persistent identity serves a distinct behavioral objective: correlating detections across frames allows the motion planner to estimate target velocity vectors and anticipate dynamic crossing events, while immediate spatial occupancy protects reactive clearance.</p>\n<p>The software architecture therefore decouples immediate geometric spatial occupancy from the <strong>tracking-by-detection</strong> hypothesis. The tracking layer associates incoming detections, filters kinematic velocities, and tracks multi-dimensional uncertainty. Track retirement follows the track lifecycle of this section, and how state persists when measurements stop is the subject of @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion. The state equations that follow govern the kinematic information a confirmed track provides, rather than serving as a prerequisite for reactive collision avoidance.</p>\n<p>Each active track $\\TrackIndex$ maintains an independent state filter tracking a kinematic state vector $\\StateEstimate_t = [\\EstimatedPosition_t^\\top, \\EstimatedVelocity_t^\\top]^\\top$ with error covariance $\\EstimationErrorCovariance_t \\in \\mathbb{R}^{6\\times6}$. A discrete constant-velocity transition matrix $\\StateTransitionMatrix$ and process noise covariance $\\ProcessNoiseCovariance$ advance this state between sensory frames through the prediction step of @eq-percep-kf-predict, which carries the mean forward and grows the covariance by the process noise. When an incoming perception frame yields $\\DetectionCount$ candidate detections $\\{\\MeasurementVector_{t, j}\\}<em>{j=1}^{\\DetectionCount}$ (the frame index $\\TrackerFrameIndex$ plays the role of the step index $\\EstimatorStepIndex$ in @eq-percep-kf-predict), the tracker must resolve <strong>data association</strong>, mapping candidate detections to established state tracks. To prevent spurious sensor noise or distant clutter from corrupting legitimate tracks, the tracker evaluates the squared Mahalanobis distance $\\MahalanobisDistance^2$ between detection $\\MeasurementVector</em>{t, j}$ and the projected measurement $\\MeasurementMatrix\\TrackPredictedStateEstimate$: $$\\MahalanobisDistance^2 = (\\MeasurementVector_{t, j} - \\MeasurementMatrix\\TrackPredictedStateEstimate)^\\top \\InnovationCovariance_{ij}^{-1} (\\MeasurementVector_{t, j} - \\MeasurementMatrix\\TrackPredictedStateEstimate)$$ {#eq-percep-mahalanobis-gate} where $\\InnovationCovariance_{ij} = \\MeasurementMatrix\\TrackPredictedErrorCovariance\\MeasurementMatrix^\\top + \\DetectionMeasurementNoiseCovariance$ defines the innovation covariance from @eq-percep-kf-update and $\\DetectionMeasurementNoiseCovariance$ specifies the measurement noise covariance; this validation metric is identical to the NIS metric defined in @sec-memory-estimator-consistency. An ellipsoidal <strong>validation gate</strong> evaluates @eq-percep-mahalanobis-gate, rejecting any pairing where $\\MahalanobisDistance^2 &gt; \\ValidationGateThreshold$. The gating threshold $\\SquaredGaussianSumQuantile_3^2(1 - \\HypothesisTestSignificanceLevel)$ possesses three degrees of freedom corresponding to three-dimensional Cartesian position measurements, retaining its theoretical significance only while residual Gaussian assumptions hold. For all candidate pairings satisfying the gate, the tracker builds an assignment cost matrix with entries $\\AssociationCostEntry_{ij} = \\MahalanobisDistance^2$ and computes the global minimum-cost one-to-one match. It uses either the Hungarian algorithm [@kuhn1955hungarian], as the Simple Online and Realtime Tracking (SORT) baseline does [@bewley2016simple], or the Jonker-Volgenant shortest-augmenting-path solver [@jonker1987shortest]. The match is optimal only for the costs it was given; it does not guarantee that each pairing is physically correct.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Data Association in Clutter</strong>: Radar tracking met this problem first. @barshalom1975tracking showed that a filter which commits to the single nearest return inside its gate can follow clutter and lose the target, and they weighted every gated return by its probability of having come from the target instead. A tracker that commits to one assignment per frame, as this one does, reveals a wrong pairing only through inconsistent innovations, so a confirmed track's covariance must still pass the tests of @sec-memory-estimator-consistency.</p>\n<p>The tracking lifecycle organizes target hypotheses through three discrete states:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Tentative state</strong>: Unassociated detections instantiate tentative candidate tracks. Because sensor artifacts (specular reflections, dust particles, optical lens flare) generate transient false positives, candidate identities remain tentative until satisfying an $\\ConfirmationHitCount$-of-$\\ConfirmationWindowCount$ frame confirmation policy (associated in at least $\\ConfirmationHitCount$ of the last $\\ConfirmationWindowCount$ frames). Raw geometric occupancy claims remain immediately accessible to vehicle collision-avoidance buffers throughout this confirmation window. If a downstream behavioral planner requires confirmed object identity or calibrated velocity, its latency allocation must account for this multi-frame confirmation interval rather than relying solely on the single-frame pipeline latency described in @sec-perception-latency-waterfall.</li>\n<li><strong>Confirmed state</strong>: Upon passing confirmation criteria, the track registers as confirmed and publishes its complete kinematic state $(\\text{ID}_i, \\EstimatedPosition_i, \\EstimatedVelocity_i, \\EstimationErrorCovariance_i)$ to planning and safety subsystems. Each newly associated measurement refines the track via @eq-percep-kf-update, correcting the mean vector and contracting the error covariance matrix. Because this update achieves minimum variance only under linear-Gaussian assumptions, tracked covariances must continuously satisfy the consistency criteria established in @sec-memory-estimator-consistency.</li>\n<li><strong>Coasting state (occlusion)</strong>: When an obstacle moves behind storage shelving or suffers transient occlusion by another vehicle, object detection fails. Rather than immediately dropping the entity, the tracker transitions the hypothesis into an unobserved coasting state. During coasting, state propagation proceeds strictly through kinematic motion model $\\StateTransitionMatrix$ in @eq-percep-kf-predict, while process noise $\\ProcessNoiseCovariance$ progressively inflates state covariance in the absence of measurement constraints. Downstream path planners must compensate for this increasing uncertainty by applying progressively widening clearance margins. If no matching observation associates within timeout window $\\TrackLossTimeout$, the active track terminates. Terminating a track reflects the loss of persistent identity rather than the clearance of physical space, so the spatial map must retain an appropriate unknown-space constraint (@sec-memory-scene-attention-buffers). The rate at which coasting uncertainty degrades spatial traversability is the belief expiry problem formulated in @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>To support real-time planning under motion uncertainty, the tracker continuously serializes an auditable <strong>dynamic track manifest</strong> $[(\\text{ID}_k, \\EstimatedPosition_k, \\EstimatedVelocity_k, \\EstimationErrorCovariance_k, \\TrackLastUpdateTime, \\text{state})]$. The intent layer consumes these velocity vectors and covariance ellipses to bound the region each object could occupy over the planning horizon, while real-time safety enforcement systems compute certified stopping clearances against moving dynamic obstacles. Even when a detection associates cleanly with the right track, however, vision inference delay means that camera detections routinely arrive after newer LiDAR sweeps and IMU samples have already advanced the filter.</p>\n<p>↪ The track manifest's velocities and covariances are developed as forward occupancy sets in @sec-intent-dynamic-forecasting.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h3\">Applying a delayed observation</h3>\n<p>Fusing such a delayed visual detection directly into the estimator's current state pulls the position estimate backward toward where the obstacle stood at exposure time and corrupts the velocity vector. The physical capture epoch preserved by the observation contract enables the estimator to integrate these delayed measurements at their historical capture instant instead, provided the system maintains a calibrated state history and dynamic motion model. Rolling the filter back to that capture instant applies the update of @eq-percep-kf-update to the historical state estimate $\\StateEstimate$, error covariance $\\EstimationErrorCovariance$, observation model $\\MeasurementMatrix$, and Kalman gain $\\KalmanGain_{k}$.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ OUT-OF-SEQUENCE RING BUFFER SIZE ON THE RUNNING MACHINE\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-perception-discrete-state-estimation (ring-buffer sizing).\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Size the rewind buffer for the navigation camera&#x27;s observation\n# │          age at the IMU&#x27;s sample rate.\n# │ Show:    IMU sample period; buffer horizon equal to the 51.6 ms\n# │          observation age; slot count.\n# │ How:     N = ceil(T_buffer / dt_IMU); T_buffer = AISLE.perception_age\n# │          (the age at dispatch of @sec-perception-latency-waterfall).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport math\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_int, fmt_latency, fmt_frequency, check\n\nclass OosmBuffer:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Slots a rewind buffer needs to cover the navigation camera&#x27;s observation age.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    f_imu = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator.imu.sample_rate  # category: datasheet (base IMU sample rate)\n    t_buffer = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle.perception_age  # category: derived (observation age at dispatch, @sec-perception-latency-waterfall)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    dt_imu = Scenarios.WarehouseObservationReplayBuffer.result.sample_period\n    n_buffer = Scenarios.WarehouseObservationReplayBuffer.result.slots\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(n_buffer * dt_imu &gt;= t_buffer, &quot;The buffer must reach back at least one observation age.&quot;)\n    check(10 &lt; n_buffer &lt; 1000, f&quot;Slot count should be tens to hundreds: {n_buffer}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    f_imu_str = fmt_frequency(f_imu, unit=kilohertz, precision=0)\n    dt_imu_str = fmt_latency(dt_imu, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_buffer_str = fmt_latency(t_buffer.to(millisecond), unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    n_buffer_str = fmt_int(n_buffer)\n]```\n\nA delayed visual observation must correct the state at its true historical capture epoch before the estimator can project the correction forward to current wall-clock time. Robust systems implement this mechanism using an **out-of-sequence ring buffer**, a circular array whose slot $\\EstimatorStepIndex$ holds the prediction $\\PredictedStateEstimate$ and covariance $\\PredictedErrorCovariance$ for IMU sample $\\EstimatorStepIndex$, the IMU input $\\MotionModelInput_k$ that drove it, and that sample&#x27;s capture time. The buffer needs $\\RingBufferSlotCount = \\lceil \\RingBufferHorizon / \\FiniteChange \\ImuSampleTime \\rceil$ slots, where $\\FiniteChange \\ImuSampleTime$ is the IMU sample period and the buffer horizon $\\RingBufferHorizon$ is the oldest observation age the estimator will still accept. On the running machine, the IMU samples at `[OosmBuffer.f_imu_str]` ($\\FiniteChange \\ImuSampleTime =$ `[OosmBuffer.dt_imu_str]`), so covering the navigation camera&#x27;s `[OosmBuffer.t_buffer_str]` observation age takes `[OosmBuffer.n_buffer_str]` slots. A consumer must either interpolate the buffered state to the observation&#x27;s actual capture time or charge the bounded nearest-sample timing discrepancy to the error model. In the rollback equations that follow, subscripts index IMU samples; $\\CaptureSampleIndex$ and $\\ReceiptSampleIndex$ name the samples whose capture times are nearest the observation&#x27;s capture epoch and its receipt. When an out-of-sequence observation $\\DelayedObservation$ arrives at receipt sample $\\ReceiptSampleIndex$, the estimator rolls back to sample $\\CaptureSampleIndex$, retrieves the historical prediction $\\CaptureSamplePredictedEstimate$, computes the corresponding innovation and Kalman gain, and applies the update of @eq-percep-kf-update at that historical instant (@eq-percep-oosm-correction):\n$$\\CaptureSampleUpdatedEstimate = \\CaptureSamplePredictedEstimate + \\CaptureSampleKalmanGain (\\DelayedObservation - \\CaptureSampleMeasurementMatrix \\CaptureSamplePredictedEstimate), \\quad \\CaptureSampleUpdatedCovariance = (\\IdentityMatrix - \\CaptureSampleKalmanGain\\CaptureSampleMeasurementMatrix) \\CaptureSamplePredictedCovariance$$ {#eq-percep-oosm-correction}\nFor the forward propagation pass that follows, consider first the baseline case with no intervening updates from other sensors; in general configurations, any intervening sensor updates within the interval must be sequentially re-applied in timestamp order. Because nonlinear motion models and their linearized transition matrices $\\StateTransitionMatrix_k$ depend on the state trajectory itself, the estimator cannot simply add the historical state shift to the current estimate, and instead executes a rapid replay sweep, re-integrating the buffered IMU inputs $\\MotionModelInput_k$ from sample $\\CaptureSampleIndex$ to sample $\\ReceiptSampleIndex$ through the continuous motion model $\\ContinuousMotionModel(\\EstimatorState, \\MotionModelInput)$, the state&#x27;s rate of change under input $\\MotionModelInput$, using the corrected state $\\CaptureSampleUpdatedEstimate$ as its initial condition. This forward integration synchronizes current state estimate $\\ReplayedStateEstimate$ with the newly arrived observation without introducing non-causal state discontinuities (@eq-percep-oosm-replay):\n$$\\ReplayedStateEstimate = \\CaptureSampleUpdatedEstimate + \\sum_{k = \\CaptureSampleIndex}^{\\ReceiptSampleIndex-1} \\ContinuousMotionModel(\\StateEstimate_k, \\MotionModelInput_k) \\FiniteChange \\ImuSampleTime$$ {#eq-percep-oosm-replay}\nwith covariance propagation across the replay interval governed by unrolling the single-step covariance recursion of @eq-percep-kf-predict through the discrete transition matrix product $\\IntervalTransitionMatrix(\\ReceiptSampleIndex, \\CaptureSampleIndex) = \\prod_{k = \\CaptureSampleIndex}^{\\ReceiptSampleIndex-1} \\StateTransitionMatrix_k$ to carry the newly contracted historical covariance $\\CaptureSampleUpdatedCovariance$ and each intermediate process-noise increment forward:\n$$\\ReplayedErrorCovariance = \\IntervalTransitionMatrix(\\ReceiptSampleIndex, \\CaptureSampleIndex) \\CaptureSampleUpdatedCovariance \\IntervalTransitionMatrix^\\top(\\ReceiptSampleIndex, \\CaptureSampleIndex) + \\sum_{k = \\CaptureSampleIndex}^{\\ReceiptSampleIndex-1} \\IntervalTransitionMatrix(\\ReceiptSampleIndex, \\EstimatorStepIndex+1) \\ProcessNoiseCovariance_k \\IntervalTransitionMatrix^\\top(\\ReceiptSampleIndex, \\EstimatorStepIndex+1)$$ {#eq-percep-oosm-cov-replay}\nThe applicability of this rewind-and-replay routine (@eq-percep-oosm-cov-replay) is strictly limited by ring buffer horizon $\\RingBufferHorizon$. If scheduling jitter or memory bus contention delays an observation such that $\\ObservationAge &gt; \\RingBufferHorizon$, the circular buffer has already overwritten the slot for sample $\\CaptureSampleIndex$. In this scenario, the estimator must reject the stale measurement, permit process noise covariance $\\ProcessNoiseCovariance$ to grow the predictive covariance $\\EstimationErrorCovariance$, and immediately notify downstream safety paths that external visual grounding has degraded.\n\nEnd-to-end vision-language-action (VLA) policies confront this identical out-of-sequence dilemma when ingesting asynchronous visual frames without an explicit state-space ring buffer. The observation tokenizer developed in @sec-perception-observation-contract pairs each sensory token with its elapsed time since capture, leaving formal validation of the estimate and action-permission gating to dedicated safety monitors.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ ESTIMATOR CHECKPOINT: GUARD ON THE GAIN ESTIMATE</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @chk-mem-estimator-tracking (the gain-estimate item).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Guard the verdict the checkpoint's estimate item expects without</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          printing the answer: with the stereo spread doubled, stereo still</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          dominates the fused estimate.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    nothing (guards only); the item quotes AisleKalman's strings.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndRangeFusion's comparison update:</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          g = P / (P + (2 sigma_stereo)^2) with P the LiDAR posterior.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim.fmt import check from mlsysim.core.units import dimensionless</p>\n<p>class EstimatorCheckpoint: \"\"\"Guards for the estimator checkpoint's estimate item (answers stay private).\"\"\"</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>factor = Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndRangeFusion.comparison_scale  # category: chosen (checkpoint given: stereo spread doubled)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>g = Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndRangeFusion.result.comparison_gain sigma = Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndRangeFusion.result.comparison_sigma</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(0.5 &lt; g &lt; AisleKalman.g_stereo, f\"Doubled stereo spread must lower the gain yet keep stereo dominant: {g}\") check(AisleKalman.sigma_fused &lt; sigma &lt; AisleKalman.sigma_lidar_post, f\"Fused spread must sit between the two cases: {sigma}\") ]```</p>\n<p>Every belief the rest of the chapter ages is the output of this estimator. These items test whether you can run it and whether you can tell when its reported uncertainty is honest.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Predict vs. update</strong>: In the worked scalar drift model, can you explain why a prediction step widens the reported variance, while an independent measurement update narrows it below both the prediction and the measurement?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Gain estimate</strong>: If the stereo range's standard deviation doubled from <code>[AisleKalman.sigma_stereo_str]</code>, with the LiDAR-updated <code>[AisleKalman.sigma_lidar_post_str]</code> prior unchanged, what would the stereo gain and the fused standard deviation be, and which sensor would then dominate the fused estimate?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Estimator consistency</strong>: Can you explain why a misdated update leaves the reported variance unchanged, when averaging independent innovations can expose a dating error that a per-update gate misses, and why this monitor cannot replace capture-epoch validation?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Occupancy vs. identity</strong>: Can you explain why a tentative track's occupied region must reach clearance consumers before confirmation, and what a decision that needs confirmed velocity must add to its detection delay?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Late measurements</strong>: Can you explain what the estimator must do with a measurement whose age exceeds its ring buffer's horizon, and why?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The state estimator outputs the core data record that persistent spatial memory maintains: a kinematic state vector, its verified covariance, the physical instant the state describes, and the capture epochs of the raw sensor observations that last constrained it. Forward prediction and rewind-replay passes maintain temporal currency across execution cycles, but only external sensor measurements inject genuine physical evidence, and neither mathematical projection advances the underlying evidence timestamps. Because downstream consumers must independently audit when a stored belief expires once an obstacle slips into occlusion, this state record must carry explicit temporal provenance alongside its numerical estimates. The handoff challenge is defining the exact contract a downstream consumer requires to evaluate stored spatial beliefs when direct line of sight is lost.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h2\">The Spatial Memory Handoff and State Invariants</h2>\n<p>Defining that consumer contract begins with what a raw state estimate omits, because a position vector arriving over an inter-process communication bus cannot identify the entity, physical quantity, or units it describes. The same sequence of floating-point values could denote a workpiece center in meters, a joint angle in radians, or a contact force in newtons. A consumer requires these distinctions before executing a control law; a confusion between meters and millimeters alone scales the interpreted displacement by a factor of one thousand. The belief record therefore explicitly packages entity identity, physical quantity, and measurement units at its foundation, rather than relying on conventions hidden in the producing model or transport wrapper.</p>\n<p>Specifying the quantity and units remains incomplete without binding the value to a concrete reference frame. Identical spatial coordinates describe entirely different physical realities depending on the origin and orientation of the coordinate system. A Cartesian position $[0.142, -0.051, 0.812]\\text{ m}$ read in the robot base frame names a point about 0.8 m above the chassis origin, in open air over the base. Read in the tool flange frame, the same three numbers name a point 0.812 m out along the flange's approach axis, wherever the arm happens to point; with the gripper aimed at the cage door, that point lies inside the door rather than above the chassis. Because kinematic links flex, joints move, and coordinate transforms update at varying rates across distributed nodes, a belief record must carry an explicit frame identifier. Attaching the frame allows the receiving node to query the spatial transformation tree and project the stored geometry into the local frame required by the actuator, preventing the controller from acting on geometric misalignments caused by moving kinematic chains.</p>\n<p>Geometry and identity describe physical state at a single moment, but spatial memory must also capture the temporal anchor of that state. The record carries the evidence epoch $\\EvidenceEpoch$ of the physical interaction or optical exposure that last constrained the estimate, the instant at which a sensor transduced the evidence. In distributed machine architectures, intermediate nodes often republish data, execute Kalman filter prediction steps, or run forward kinematic propagations to maintain high-rate control streams; none of these computational steps adds physical evidence. Republishing and prediction must preserve the evidence epoch; a measurement update may renew only the components it physically constrains. Consumers therefore measure evidence age against the physical world rather than the scheduling frequency of the local computer.</p>\n<p>At the boundary of the multi-sensor state estimator (@sec-perception-state-estimation), downstream consumers do not need to rerun the filter recursion or repeat the consistency tests of @sec-memory-estimator-consistency; they need only the resulting state, its justified error model, and the physical evidence that constrains each relevant component. Within that recursion, a prediction step advances the estimate epoch, the instant the propagated state describes (formalized in @sec-memory-state-epoch), while its underlying physical evidence continues aging. A fresh measurement, by contrast, renews only the state dimensions it directly constrains. The spatial memory handoff therefore obliges the producer to expose bounded growth through occlusion and withdraw claims whose operating assumptions no longer hold.</p>\n<p>Knowing the evidence epoch $\\EvidenceEpoch$ establishes elapsed time $\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant = \\TimeInstant - \\EvidenceEpoch$, but elapsed time alone cannot determine whether a belief is valid for a given action. Two estimates of identical age diverge at different rates depending on physical constraints. A warehouse rack end bolted to the floor maintains a stationary position; its remembered boundary can remain useful while the machine's localization bound and the fixed-rack premise hold. In contrast, an occluded person hidden behind that rack can move at walking speed and close the narrow gap beside it before the next camera frame arrives.</p>\n<p>The minimum record fields necessary for a consumer to evaluate a stored state can be derived directly from the requirement to bound current error. Let $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)$ represent the upper bound of the discrepancy between the stored value and true physical state after an elapsed time $\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant = \\TimeInstant - \\EvidenceEpoch$. If an estimator establishes a state at time $\\EvidenceEpoch$ with an initial uncertainty bound $\\InitialTrackingError$, and external disturbances or unmodeled velocities cause the true state to diverge at a maximum rate $\\ErrorGrowthRate$, the upper bound on error at current time $\\TimeInstant$ obeys the linear expansion $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant) = \\InitialTrackingError + \\ErrorGrowthRate\\,\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$. At the rack end, the stored spatial claim asserts that no person stands near the machine. Here $\\InitialTrackingError$ is the machine's localization error, $\\ErrorGrowthRate$ is the walking speed at which an occluded person can advance toward the vehicle, and the safety tolerance is the depth of observed free space beyond the machine's clearance envelope. Other disturbances grow error faster. A bounded unmodeled acceleration adds a quadratic term, and random acceleration noise, integrated twice, makes position variance grow with the cube of elapsed time, both derived in @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion. Omitting the initial bound or the dynamic growth terms leaves the consumer unable to compute $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)$, making an initial bound and an explicit expansion model structurally required fields of the record.</p>\n<p>Expressing uncertainty through physical bounds and expansion rates distinguishes a physical AI belief from a probabilistic confidence score. An object detection network might output a class confidence of $0.94$, but that softmax score is not calibrated evidence (@sec-brain-softmax-illusion), and a downstream position controller cannot convert a unitless probability into a safe actuator displacement margin (that is, how far the arm can physically move before risking a collision). A confidence score of $0.94$ provides no information about whether an obstacle boundary is uncertain by $2.0\\text{ mm}$ or by $200\\text{ mm}$, nor does it indicate how rapidly that boundary uncertainty grows when occlusion occurs. A physical memory record must express its initial bound $\\InitialTrackingError$ and expansion rate $\\ErrorGrowthRate$ in concrete physical dimensions such as meters, radians, or newtons per second. These rates are valid only within a declared validity region, such as a maximum assumed acceleration or a verified contact friction coefficient. If physical operating conditions leave that region, the expansion rate no longer holds, and the belief record must be treated as invalid.</p>\n<p>At the spring-latched cage door, the gripper finger must enter a narrow latch-release slot that the finger itself hides from the wrist camera during the final approach. Before relying on the remembered slot pose, the consumer compares the belief's current error with the slot clearance. It converts the evidence epoch into its own clock domain, includes the clock-conversion bound, and evaluates the declared growth law at the time the state will be used. This check is a continuing obligation during execution, not a one-time path-generation check. A new motion proposal whose evidence fails the check is refused without automatically revoking a still-valid active command. If the active motion itself depends on expiring evidence, the controller must begin a feasible fallback maneuver early enough that the mechanism halts within the available clearance. The machine must therefore remain inside its recoverable set, the states from which a qualified stop still fits within the remaining clearance, a boundary that @sec-enforcement-stopping-envelopes derives from the finished stopping budget. The belief's expiry is a limit on supported motion, not a promise that an instantaneous stop is physically possible at that deadline.</p>\n<p>The fields a consumer needs for this check travel together as the <strong>belief record</strong>, the seventh record in the record chain. At this stage, the record binds an identified physical quantity, with its unit, to a reference frame, an evidence epoch, an initial error bound, an uncertainty-growth law, and the validity region in which that law holds. The sections that follow add the fields each new problem demands (the estimate epoch and clock-conversion bound, a per-consumer query-work limit, the expiry deadline, provenance, and lifecycle status), and @sec-memory-spatial-state-schema assembles the complete record. A classification score, a map publication time, or a transform value alone cannot supply that contract, because none couples physical units and evidence age to an error-growth law. The record makes the claims checkable; qualification must establish that they hold. A consumer cannot wait for an upstream publisher to announce expiry. If that publisher's thread hangs or its message stream stops, the consumer must still age the evidence autonomously and enforce its own safety deadline.</p>\n<p>Evaluating the belief record depends on calculating the elapsed time $\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant = \\CurrentTime - \\EvidenceEpoch$ against physical reality. If the consumer cannot verify the exact physical instant when energy transduction occurred, the calculated error bound $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)$ becomes an unanchored fiction, mistaking software compute cycles for physical evidence. Protecting that anchor is the job of the next two fields, the estimate epoch and the clock-conversion bound.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h2\">State Epochs and Temporal Anchoring</h2>\n<p>A bounding box or 3D Gaussian pose emitted by a neural object detector describes where an entity was when photons struck the camera CMOS sensor, not where it is when the inference tensor arrives in memory. Every spatial claim refers to a specific physical instant, its <strong>epoch</strong>, and any state crossing a physical AI pipeline carries three distinct epochs before an actuator can fire. The <strong>acquisition epoch</strong> ($\\AcquisitionEpoch$) anchors the observation contract to physical ground truth: the exposure midpoint for a camera frame, or the precise instant a hardware register latches an optical encoder count. Every observation carries its own acquisition epoch (the capture epoch of @sec-perception-handoff). For each state component used by a consumer, the evidence epoch is the latest acquisition epoch of a measurement that constrained that component; for a belief built from one observation, the two coincide. A record combining differently constrained components must preserve those epochs separately, or use a conservative shared epoch and growth bound that supports every component the query needs. The rest of the chapter speaks of the evidence epoch whenever the belief, not a single observation, is meant. Downstream processing then introduces two subsequent epochs. The <strong>estimate epoch</strong> ($\\EstimateEpoch$) marks the physical instant that the estimated state describes. A kinematic predictor or state observer can advance this temporal coordinate beyond the last observation without receiving fresh measurements; the instant computation completes is a separate scheduling detail. The <strong>publication epoch</strong> ($\\PublicationEpoch$) marks the instant the serialized state record is committed to the transport bus or shared-memory ring buffer for dispatch to downstream consumers.</p>\n<p>Collapsing these three timestamps into a single receipt or transmission timestamp destroys the physical anchor. This is the publish-timestamp anti-pattern of @sec-body-measurement-freshness, in which a wrapper stamps its output with the system wall clock after exposure, transport, inference, and postprocessing have already elapsed. What memory adds is a second way to commit it. A wrapper that stamps a propagated estimate with its publication epoch, or a filter that renews $\\EvidenceEpoch$ when it advances $\\EstimateEpoch$, hides the evidence age just as surely as one that stamps a raw frame on arrival. Downstream receivers then ingest a message that appears freshly minted but wraps stale evidence. The age-as-displacement relation established in @sec-perception-age-budget governs this entire pipeline. While publication epochs help diagnose pipeline latency and transport jitter, only the acquisition epoch enables a motion controller to compensate for physical movement that occurred while data were in flight.</p>\n<p>↩ The hardware capture stamp this anti-pattern discards is established in @sec-perception-handoff.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ CLOCK-CONVERSION BOUND AS DISTANCE AT THE DRIVE LIMIT\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-memory-state-epoch (the d_sync clearance term).\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Give the synchronization term a size on the running machine.\n# │ Show:    0.2 ms bound at the 1.5 m/s drive limit is 0.3 mm.\n# │ How:     d_sync = v_rel x eps_sync, v_rel = the base drive limit; eps_sync\n# │          is the observation contract&#x27;s illustrative bound\n# │          (@sec-perception-observation-contract), matching the\n# │          LatchSlotBelief cell below.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, check\n\nclass SyncTravel:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Clearance term the clock-conversion bound adds at the drive limit.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    eps_sync = (\n        Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle.clock_conversion_bound\n    )  # category: illustrative (clock-conversion bound, @sec-perception-observation-contract)\n    v_rel = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator.base.max_velocity  # category: datasheet (base drive limit)\n    delta_loc = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle.delta_loc  # category: illustrative (localization bound)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    d_sync = Scenarios.WarehouseLidarClockConversion.result.bound_travel  # v_rel x eps_sync\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(d_sync.magnitude - 0.3) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Sync travel at the drive limit should be 0.3 mm: {d_sync}&quot;)\n    check(d_sync &lt; delta_loc / 100, &quot;The sync term must be a small fraction of the localization bound.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    eps_sync_str = fmt_latency(eps_sync, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    v_rel_str = fmt_velocity(v_rel, precision=1)\n    d_sync_str = fmt_length(d_sync, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    delta_loc_str = fmt_length(delta_loc.to(millimeter), unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n]```\n\nEven when a system faithfully records acquisition epochs, physical AI architectures rely on distributed processing nodes whose local oscillator clocks drift independently. Connecting to a shared bus does not synchronize these time bases; the deployed clock-conversion protocol must establish an explicit error bound under realistic network loads and fault conditions. That temporal uncertainty directly converts to spatial uncertainty scaled by the relative speed of the observed entity. The observation contract defined in @sec-perception-observation-contract carries this bound as its clock-conversion error, and any spatial belief derived from that observation preserves it. Every consumer adds the synchronization bound to the elapsed evidence age before judging freshness, incorporating the resulting positional uncertainty alongside sensor resolution limits and structural deflection. The permission path therefore treats this clock-synchronization uncertainty $\\SynchronizationErrorBound$ as an irreducible temporal margin, expanding the required clearance by $\\SynchronizationClearanceMargin = \\ObservedRelativeSpeed \\SynchronizationErrorBound$ before granting motor torque authorization. With the observation contract&#x27;s illustrative `[SyncTravel.eps_sync_str]` bound and the base at its `[SyncTravel.v_rel_str]` drive limit, that term is `[SyncTravel.d_sync_str]`, small beside the `[SyncTravel.delta_loc_str]` localization bound but never zero. The bound matters most where it is loosest, on a link whose synchronization has not been measured under load.\n\n: **Clock Synchronization Bound**: Controller Area Network with Flexible Data-Rate (CAN FD) bit-phase synchronization aligns bit sampling within one frame; it does not align the distributed clocks that date physical observations. Precision Time Protocol and EtherCAT Distributed Clocks provide synchronization mechanisms, but their names alone do not establish the deployed error bound, which only a measurement under the stated load and fault conditions supplies. \n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ ILLUSTRATIVE SAMPLING EPOCHS FOR FUSION</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-memory-state-epoch (three sensors latch at different epochs).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Show that fusing two observations requires projecting one across</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the gap between their acquisition epochs.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    tactile 12 ms, camera 18.5 ms, encoders 21 ms; camera-to-encoder</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          gap 2.5 ms.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Illustrative epochs in one cycle; the gap is |t3 - t2|.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_latency, check</p>\n<p>class MemoryEpochAlignment:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>EPOCHS = Scenarios.ArmSensorEpochGap.epochs  # category: illustrative (MLSysIM scenario) t1 = EPOCHS[\"tactile\"]  # category: illustrative (tactile array latches force) t2 = EPOCHS[\"camera\"]  # category: illustrative (camera exposure midpoint) t3 = EPOCHS[\"encoders\"]  # category: illustrative (encoders sample angles)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>gap = Scenarios.ArmSensorEpochGap.result.gap</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(t1 &lt; t2 &lt; t3, \"The illustrative epochs must be in sampling order.\") check(abs(gap.magnitude - 2.5) &lt; 1e-9, \"Camera-to-encoder gap is 2.5 ms.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>t1_str = fmt_latency(t1, unit=millisecond, precision=0) t2_str = fmt_latency(t2, unit=millisecond, precision=1) t3_str = fmt_latency(t3, unit=millisecond, precision=0) gap_str = fmt_latency(gap, unit=millisecond, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ EPOCH GAP AS TOOL TRAVEL\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-memory-state-epoch (the camera-to-encoder gap).\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Turn the illustrative 2.5 ms epoch gap into distance at the arm&#x27;s\n# │          Cartesian speed limit.\n# │ Show:    2.5 ms at 1.7 m/s is 4.25 mm.\n# │ How:     d = v_cart x gap; gap from MemoryEpochAlignment; v_cart from the\n# │          registry (Panda Cartesian velocity limit).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_length, fmt_velocity, check\n\nclass EpochGapTravel:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Tool travel across the camera-to-encoder epoch gap.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    v_cart = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator.arm.cartesian_velocity_limit  # category: datasheet (arm Cartesian speed limit)\n    gap = MemoryEpochAlignment.gap  # category: illustrative (camera-to-encoder gap)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    d_gap = Scenarios.ArmSensorEpochGap.result.travel  # v_cart x gap\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(d_gap.magnitude - 4.25) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Gap travel should be 4.25 mm: {d_gap}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_cart_str = fmt_velocity(v_cart, precision=1)\n    d_gap_str = fmt_length(d_gap, unit=millimeter, precision=2)\n]```\n\nPhysical systems routinely combine asynchronously acquired signals, even when hardware triggers synchronize subsets of sensors. In an illustrative asynchronous cycle, a tactile array might latch normal force readings at $\\TimeInstant_1 =$ `[MemoryEpochAlignment.t1_str]`, an optical tracking camera might have its exposure midpoint at $\\TimeInstant_2 =$ `[MemoryEpochAlignment.t2_str]`, and joint optical encoders might sample angular positions at $\\TimeInstant_3 =$ `[MemoryEpochAlignment.t3_str]`. These observations occur at different epochs and cannot be combined through direct tensor concatenation or naive algebraic operations. Multimodal foundation models and vision-language-action (VLA) backbones frequently pack visual patch tokens, proprioceptive vectors, and tactile embeddings into a single sequence under the implicit assumption that all tokens capture a simultaneous physical snapshot. However, nothing in that token serialization records that the camera frame came from $\\TimeInstant_2$ and the encoder reading from $\\TimeInstant_3$. As a result, representations inferred from unaligned inputs carry unquantified temporal mismatch errors. Fusing a camera observation at $\\TimeInstant_2$ with an encoder observation at $\\TimeInstant_3$ requires the consumer to project one state across the temporal gap $\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant = |\\TimeInstant_3 - \\TimeInstant_2| =$ `[MemoryEpochAlignment.gap_str]`. The projection uses explicit kinematic models and bounded velocities (for example, propagating the historical camera observation along the joint velocity vector to reconcile it with the instantaneous encoder reading). The gap is small in time but not in space; at the arm&#x27;s `[EpochGapTravel.v_cart_str]` Cartesian speed limit, the tool moves `[EpochGapTravel.d_gap_str]` in `[MemoryEpochAlignment.gap_str]`, a millimeter-scale error that a fine insertion cannot absorb. Without the true acquisition epoch for each individual signal, the alignment calculation cannot determine the sign or magnitude of $\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$, leaving uncorrected temporal offsets to compound geometric errors across every inference cycle. Placed on one shared timeline (@fig-09-epoch-alignment), the three epochs fall at distinct instants, and the camera evidence must be carried across the `[MemoryEpochAlignment.gap_str]` gap before it can be fused with the encoder state.\n\n::: {#fig-09-epoch-alignment fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Three evidence epochs in one cycle**: The tactile array, the camera&#x27;s exposure midpoint, and the joint encoders each date their evidence on one clock, at `[MemoryEpochAlignment.t1_str]`, `[MemoryEpochAlignment.t2_str]`, and `[MemoryEpochAlignment.t3_str]` in this illustrative cycle. A fused state dated to the encoder epoch must carry the camera&#x27;s evidence across the `[MemoryEpochAlignment.gap_str]` gap with a kinematic model and a bounded velocity; packing the three readings into one token sequence records none of these epochs.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Timing strip with three lanes labeled tactile, camera, and encoders over a time axis in milliseconds. Each lane has one short sample mark at its own time, with vertical event lines labeled tactile latch, camera exposure midpoint, and encoder sample. A bracket above the strip spans the interval between the camera and encoder marks and is labeled with the gap to project across.&quot;}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ THREE EVIDENCE EPOCHS ON ONE CLOCK (TIMING STRIP FIGURE)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @fig-09-epoch-alignment in @sec-memory-state-epoch</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    show that each sensor dates its evidence at its own epoch and that</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          fusion must project across the gap between them</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    the tactile, camera, and encoder epochs; the camera-to-encoder gap</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     viz.TimingStrip / plot_timing_strip; every time from the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          MemoryEpochAlignment cell; sample marks have a nominal drawing</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          width only</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import viz import matplotlib.pyplot as plt</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>MEA = MemoryEpochAlignment t1 = MEA.t1.to(millisecond).magnitude  # category: illustrative (from the cell) t2 = MEA.t2.to(millisecond).magnitude  # category: illustrative (from the cell) t3 = MEA.t3.to(millisecond).magnitude  # category: illustrative (from the cell) w = 0.25  # drawing width of a sample mark (ms); not a physical duration</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>strip = viz.TimingStrip( spans=[ viz.Span(\"\", t1 - w / 2, t1 + w / 2, \"measured\", 0, solid=True), viz.Span(\"\", t2 - w / 2, t2 + w / 2, \"measured\", 1, solid=True), viz.Span(\"\", t3 - w / 2, t3 + w / 2, \"measured\", 2, solid=True), ], lanes=[\"tactile\", \"camera\", \"encoders\"], events=[ viz.Event(t1, f\"tactile latch {MEA.t1_str}\", \"measured\", 1), viz.Event(t2, f\"exposure midpoint {MEA.t2_str}\", \"measured\", 0, ha=\"right\"), viz.Event(t3, f\"encoder sample {MEA.t3_str}\", \"measured\", 0, ha=\"left\"), ], brackets=[viz.Bracket(t2, t3, f\"project across {MEA.gap_str}\", \"neutral\", \"time\", 0)], xlim=(t1 - 3, t3 + 5), figsize=(6.2, 2.6), xlabel=\"Time in the cycle (ms)\", )</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs((t3 - t2) - MEA.gap.to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"The bracket must span the cell's camera-to-encoder gap.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. PLOT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>fig, ax, _ = viz.plot_timing_strip(strip) plt.show() ]```</p>\n<p>Equipped with these explicit epochs and the clock-synchronization bound carried in the record of @sec-memory-handoff, downstream consumers can evaluate the elapsed duration between the evidence epoch and the execution deadline. They can then compute the worst-case expansion of the spatial error bound, and verify whether the state remains within task tolerances. Omitting the acquisition epoch leaves the consumer blind to transport delays and scheduling jitter, degrading deterministic closed-loop control into open-loop guesswork. Because physical geometry continuously evolves as bodies move through space, spatial transforms between reference frames are subject to the same temporal decay, carrying their own distinct evidence epochs and bounded uncertainties.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h2\">Coordinate Transform Expiry and Localization Drift</h2>\n<p>A coordinate frame binds a spatial representation to a physical body, but on an active robot that frame is continuously moving. Embodied architectures relate sensors, chassis links, and end-effectors through a transform tree, in which every frame has exactly one parent. A static drawing of this graph, however, conceals how rapidly its edges go stale. In the arm chain of @fig-09-robot-transform-tree, each edge stores a rigid-body pose parameterized by joint angles [@lynch2017modern; @craig2005introduction], describing the machine only at the instant its physical geometry was transduced. When an onboard computer chains transforms sampled at different timestamps, whether through neural inference latency, bus transport delays, asynchronous sensor clocks, or dropped packets, the resulting mathematical product yields a hybrid pose that the physical machine never occupied.</p>\n<p>↪ Rigid-body transforms, their composition along a kinematic chain, and their rotation parameterizations are developed in @sec-appendix-ml-spatial.</p>\n<p>Transform state also spans multiple machine words: three floating-point numbers for translation and four for rotation as a unit quaternion. A high-rate control reader racing an asynchronous perception writer can therefore tear a read, observing an old rotation paired with a new translation. The lock-free sequence lock (seqlock) mailbox established in @sec-nervous-mailbox-exchanges resolves this concurrency race without blocking hard real-time control threads. Each reader makes a single bounded attempt per tick and never blocks. If the sequence count reveals a torn read, the thread discards the sample and lets the last validated transform age in place.</p>\n<p>On the arm itself (@fig-09-robot-transform-tree, panel a), an effective lever arm $\\LeverArmLength$ separates the rotating wrist (joint 7) from the tool center point (<code>tool0</code>), so an angular lag $\\FiniteChange\\JointAngle = \\JointAngularRate\\,\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$ sweeps the end-effector through an unmodeled arc $\\ToolPositionError \\approx \\LeverArmLength\\,\\FiniteChange\\JointAngle$. The links (<code>link1</code> through <code>link7</code>) are connected by revolute joints whose optical encoders sample joint angles $\\JointCoordinate_i(\\TimeInstant)$ continuously, so the arm's own edges can be updated frequently; the edge that ages is the wrist camera's estimate of the target.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ TRANSFORM-TREE EDGE RATES ON THE MOBILE MANIPULATOR\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: The transform-tree paragraph of @sec-memory-reference-frames.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Show that the tree&#x27;s edges arrive at different rates on the\n# │          running machine: arm joint state at the joint-control rate, the\n# │          vision edge at the wrist camera&#x27;s frame rate.\n# │ Show:    arm joint-control rate; wrist-camera frame rate; their ratio.\n# │ How:     Registry values; ratio = f_joint / f_camera.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import dimensionless, hertz, kilohertz\n\nclass TransformTreeRates:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────\n    f_joint = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator.arm.control_frequency  # category: datasheet (Panda joint-control rate)\n    f_camera = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator.wrist_camera.frame_rate  # category: datasheet (wrist camera frame rate)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    ratio = (f_joint / f_camera).to(dimensionless).magnitude\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(ratio &gt; 10, &quot;Joint edges must arrive an order of magnitude more often than vision edges.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────\n    f_joint_str = fmt_frequency(f_joint, unit=kilohertz, precision=0)\n    f_camera_str = fmt_frequency(f_camera, unit=hertz, precision=0)\n]```\n\nPanel (b) shows the corresponding coordinate transform tree, maintained in robot middleware such as the Robot Operating System (ROS) library `tf2` [@foote2013tf]. Each directed edge represents a $4 \\times 4$ homogeneous transformation matrix $\\HomogeneousTransform$ mapping points from child frame $\\Glyph{B}$ into parent frame $\\Glyph{A}$. Because the arm&#x27;s joint state arrives at its `[TransformTreeRates.f_joint_str]` joint-control rate over a deterministic fieldbus while the wrist camera publishes detected object poses (`target_object`) at only `[TransformTreeRates.f_camera_str]` over an asynchronous transport, the transform tree inevitably buffers edges with disparate timestamps. Properly designed transform libraries refuse to mix these epochs. A `tf2` lookup for a transform chain at query time $\\TimeInstant$ evaluates every edge at that exact timestamp $\\TimeInstant$, interpolating each edge between its buffered samples. If $\\TimeInstant$ lies beyond the newest sample of any edge, the lookup raises an extrapolation error. Similarly, requesting the &quot;latest&quot; transform returns the chain at the newest timestamp common across all constituent edges, preserving a single consistent epoch [@foote2013tf].\n\nUnphysical hybrid poses arise when application code bypasses these library guarantees. If a node caches an edge, pairs a target pose from an asynchronous detection message with a live lookup of the arm at the current instant, or republishes an old edge under a refreshed timestamp, it multiplies the current forward kinematics $\\BaseWristTransform(\\TimeInstant)$ by a stale vision edge $\\WristObjectTransform(\\TimeInstant - \\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)$. This composition represents the **chimeric kinematic product anti-pattern**: an unphysical geometric artifact that corresponds to no configuration the physical robot ever occupied.\n\nEnforcing a common timestamp leaves the application with an operational decision whenever a query timestamp lies ahead of the newest buffered sample. A lookup can fail immediately, or a noncritical consumer can block until new samples arrive subject to a timeout. In safety-critical execution paths, such as permission enforcement, lookups must remain strictly nonblocking and fail closed upon rejection. If an application instead attempts to predict a future transform, it must propagate a validated uncertainty bound; naive dead-reckoning extrapolation during an unannounced sensor stall rapidly accumulates lever-arm error $\\LeverArmSweepError \\approx \\LeverArmLength\\,\\JointAngularRate\\,\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$.\n\nTransform expiration becomes spatial displacement at the plant interface. At the cage door the vision edge is the wrist camera&#x27;s estimate of the latch-release slot (the `target_object` of @fig-09-robot-transform-tree), which the controller composes with the arm chain to place the finger relative to the slot. Composing that stale camera-to-slot edge with the current arm pose adds lateral drift $\\TranslationalDriftError = \\DriftSpeedBound\\,\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$, from motion of the base and door that no sensor reported during the interval, to the initial error $\\InitialTrackingError$. It also adds a wrist lever-arm sweep $\\LeverArmSweepError \\approx \\LeverArmLength\\,\\JointAngularRate\\,\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$. The sum can carry the tool error $\\ToolPositionError$ outside the slot tolerance $\\PositionalTaskTolerance$ (@fig-frame-staleness-error). Applying the age-as-displacement principle to manipulator kinematics shows that uncompensated latency translates directly into geometric positioning error through the lever arm of downstream links.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The lever-arm kinematics behind this error propagation, built from the vector cross product, are developed in @sec-appendix-control-spatial.\n:::\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ STALE SLOT-TO-FINGER TRANSFORM AT THE LATCH SLOT</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-memory-reference-frames (lever-arm staleness example); owns</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the latch-slot constants that @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (LatchSlotBelief) reads.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Show that one missed wrist-camera frame on the slot-to-finger</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          edge can spend more than the latch-release slot's tolerance, most</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          of it through the lever arm.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    the frame period, the lateral drift at the finger's drift bound,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the lever-arm arc, and their sum with E0 against the slot.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     e_tool ~ E0 + v_drift dt + l omega dt, dt one wrist-camera frame.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import dimensionless, meter, millimeter, radian, second</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class LatchTransformStaleness:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>STALE = Scenarios.LatchSlotStaleWristTransform  # category: illustrative (MLSysIM scenario) frame_rate = RM.wrist_camera.frame_rate  # category: datasheet (wrist camera) e0 = STALE.belief.initial_error  # category: illustrative (finger-to-slot error at the wrist capture) v_drift = STALE.belief.growth_rate  # category: illustrative (unobserved lateral velocity error bound) e_max = STALE.belief.tolerance  # category: illustrative (latch-release slot tolerance) lever = STALE.lever  # category: illustrative (wrist-to-fingertip lever arm) omega = STALE.angular_rate  # category: illustrative (wrist rate while aligning the finger)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>R = STALE.result dt = R.interval e_trans = R.drift_term e_rot = R.lever_term e_tool = R.total ratio = 1 + R.over_tolerance</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(dt.magnitude - 16.667) &lt; 1e-3, f\"Wrist-camera frame period should be 16.7 ms, got {dt}.\") check(abs(e_trans.magnitude - 0.2) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Lateral drift over one frame should be 0.20 mm, got {e_trans}.\") check(abs(e_rot.magnitude - 0.75) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Lever-arm arc should be 0.75 mm, got {e_rot}.\") check(e_rot &gt; e_trans, \"The lever arm must contribute more than the lateral drift.\") check(e_tool &gt; e_max, \"One missed frame must exceed the slot tolerance.\") check(abs(e_tool.magnitude - 1.05) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Total finger error should be 1.05 mm, got {e_tool}.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>dt_str = fmt_latency(dt, unit=millisecond, precision=1) e0_str = fmt_length(e0, unit=millimeter, precision=1) v_drift_str = fmt_velocity(v_drift, unit=millimeter / second, precision=0) lever_str = fmt_length(lever, unit=meter, precision=2) omega_str = fmt_angular_velocity(omega, precision=2) e_trans_str = fmt_length(e_trans, unit=millimeter, precision=2) e_rot_str = fmt_length(e_rot, unit=millimeter, precision=2) e_tool_str = fmt_length(e_tool, unit=millimeter, precision=2) e_max_str = fmt_length(e_max, unit=millimeter, precision=0) ratio_pct_str = fmt_percent(ratio - 1, precision=0, style=\"prose\") ]```</p>\n<p>The vulnerability of high-precision manipulation to transform staleness becomes clear when positioning an end-effector at a latch-release slot while the slot remains in direct view of the wrist camera. The wrist camera captures a new frame every <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.dt_str]</code>, renewing the observed slot pose and updating the camera-to-slot transform that guides fine alignment. To clear the opening, the fingertip must remain within a tight mechanical tolerance $\\PositionalTaskTolerance =$ <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.e_max_str]</code>. To isolate the geometric penalty of a single missed frame, assume the camera observation is available at its capture epoch with zero ingestion delay, after which the transform edge ages by exactly one frame interval $\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant =$ <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.dt_str]</code>. On a production machine, pipeline ingestion latency makes the edge older still; control loops must always charge the full capture-to-use latency, including neural inference, message serialization, and transport. Starting from an initial calibration error $\\InitialTrackingError =$ <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.e0_str]</code>, lateral motion drifts by at most $\\TranslationalDriftError = \\DriftSpeedBound\\,\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant =$ <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.e_trans_str]</code> under an unobserved lateral velocity bound of $\\DriftSpeedBound =$ <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.v_drift_str]</code>.</p>\n<p>The kinematic lever arm magnifies this displacement substantially. With the fingertip positioned at a distance $\\LeverArmLength =$ <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.lever_str]</code> beyond the wrist joint, an alignment rotation at angular rate $\\JointAngularRate =$ <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.omega_str]</code> sweeps the tip through an arc of $\\LeverArmSweepError \\approx \\LeverArmLength\\,\\JointAngularRate\\,\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant =$ <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.e_rot_str]</code>. The optical encoders register the wrist rotation correctly; the error comes from what the rotation is applied to. The cached camera-to-slot transform states where the slot sat relative to the camera at the previous frame, and the camera rides on the wrist. Application code that chains that cached transform with the current wrist pose therefore swings the computed slot along with the wrist, while the real slot, fixed in the door, stays where it was. The result is a hybrid pose that the arm never occupied. Correcting this error requires either querying the kinematic chain at the exact capture epoch of the camera frame or expiring the stale transform outright. Unchecked, the stale transform displaces the estimated finger position by $\\ToolPositionError \\approx \\InitialTrackingError + \\TranslationalDriftError + \\LeverArmSweepError =$ <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.e_tool_str]</code>, which exceeds the mechanical tolerance by <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.ratio_pct_str]</code>. The lever arm contributes over three times as much error as translational drift alone. Because the matrix multiplications complete without raising a runtime exception, the control loop blindly aligns the tool to an invalid target, driving the finger into the surrounding strike plate.</p>\n<p>The red error vector in @fig-frame-staleness-error pierces the tolerance boundary even though every underlying matrix multiplication remains numerically valid. Because the dominant error originates in the kinematic lever arm rather than translational drift, a faster or more precise clock does not help; the stale chain is wrong at any timestamp resolution. Only composing every edge at the same epoch, or expiring the stale edge, removes the error, so systems software must enforce one or the other.</p>\n<p><em>A transform's admissible age is set at the point it serves, not at the joint it measures. The tool point drifts at roughly $\\DriftSpeedBound + \\LeverArmLength\\JointAngularRate$, so the longer the lever arm, the sooner an edge expires, and an edge still fresh enough to locate the wrist can be too old to locate the tool center point.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h3\">Localization drift and map frames that jump</h3>\n<p>Unlike a manipulator arm bolted to a chassis with optical encoders on every joint, a wheeled mobile base has no mechanical anchor to the floor. Dead-reckoning odometry drifts past the stopping budget's localization bound within a single aisle, while map-based loop closures snap the pose back into alignment by jumping remembered obstacles across the map frame. Every map-anchored spatial belief in this chapter, and every allocation in the safety stopping budget, relies on that localization bound $\\LocalizationBound$, the guarantee that the robot estimates its position in the map within a bounded uncertainty radius. Maintaining that bound requires combining two distinct state estimators with complementary failure modes, and spatial memory must account for how each estimator alters stored observations.</p>\n<p>Odometry integrates high-rate incremental motion from wheel encoders, inertial measurement units (IMUs), or scan-to-scan lidar odometry. Odometric integration produces smooth, high-frequency pose updates with low latency, but because it measures only relative displacements, incremental integration errors accumulate without bound. Absolute position along the trajectory represents an unobservable subspace (@sec-memory-state-estimation-bounds). Conversely, <strong>map-based localization</strong> matches real-time range scans against an authoritative pre-built map, bounding absolute drift at the expense of discrete, lower-frequency corrections. When an operating environment lacks a prior map, <strong>simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM)</strong> estimates the robot trajectory while constructing the map concurrently. Modern SLAM architectures formulate this estimation over a pose graph, a factor graph whose vertices represent historic robot poses and whose edges encode odometric and scan-matching constraints [@thrun2005probabilistic; @cadena2016past; @dellaert2017factor; @hess2016real]. When the robot revisits a previously mapped area, a <strong>loop closure</strong> introduces a cross-trajectory factor. Re-optimizing the nonlinear graph distributes the accumulated drift across historic poses, retroactively adjusting the estimated trajectory. While the underlying fusion filters follow the Bayesian prediction-correction mechanics detailed in @sec-perception-state-estimation and @sec-perception-discrete-state-estimation, spatial memory systems must manage the distinct ways these estimators distort the age and coordinates of stored evidence.</p>\n<p>: <strong>SLAM</strong>: @smith1990estimating showed that when a moving robot observes landmarks relative to itself, every landmark estimate inherits the common error in the robot's pose, so map error and pose error are correlated and must be estimated together. The acronym came later, in a survey presented at the 1995 International Symposium on Robotics Research [@durrant2006simultaneous]. That correlation is why a loop closure moves remembered cells that no sensor has revisited.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ ODOMETRY DRIFT AGAINST THE LOCALIZATION BOUND\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-memory-localization-slam (where delta_loc comes from).\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Show that odometry alone cannot hold the budget&#x27;s localization\n# │          bound for long, so delta_loc is a premise about how often map\n# │          corrections arrive.\n# │ Show:    UMBmark return error per meter of path, before and after\n# │          calibration; distance and time at the aisle speed before that\n# │          rate spends delta_loc; error accumulated over one aisle.\n# │ How:     eps = E_max,syst / L_path (L_path = 4 legs x 4 m);\n# │          d = delta_loc / eps; t = d / v_aisle; e_aisle = eps * L_aisle.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import dimensionless, meter, millimeter, second\n\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass OdometryDrift:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────\n    delta_loc = AISLE.delta_loc  # category: illustrative (budget localization bound)\n    v_aisle = AISLE.v_aisle  # category: chosen (aisle speed)\n    aisle_length = AISLE.aisle_length  # category: illustrative (aisle length)\n    # category: literature. Borenstein and Feng (1996), IEEE T-RA 12(6), Secs. 3.3 and 5,\n    # Table I, experiment 1 (Fig. 11): TRC LabMate differential-drive base, bidirectional\n    # 4 m x 4 m square path at 0.2 m/s with on-the-spot turns; E_max,syst before and after\n    # calibration of wheelbase and wheel-diameter ratio; run-to-run std. dev. about 25 mm.\n    # Values from cited literature via Scenarios.WarehouseUmbmarkOdometryDrift.\n    UMB = Scenarios.WarehouseUmbmarkOdometryDrift\n    square_side = UMB.square_side  # category: literature (UMBmark leg length)\n    n_legs = UMB.legs  # category: literature (legs per square run)\n    e_uncal = UMB.return_error_uncalibrated  # category: literature (E_max,syst, uncalibrated)\n    e_cal = UMB.return_error_calibrated  # category: literature (E_max,syst, calibrated)\n    sigma_run = UMB.run_sigma  # category: literature (return-error std. dev., &quot;about 25 mm&quot;)\n    v_test = UMB.test_speed  # category: literature (test speed on straight legs)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    R = UMB.result\n    path_length = R.path_length\n    eps_uncal = R.rate_uncalibrated\n    eps_cal = R.rate_calibrated\n    d_uncal = R.distance_uncalibrated\n    d_cal = R.distance_calibrated\n    t_uncal = R.time_uncalibrated\n    t_cal = R.time_calibrated\n    e_aisle_uncal = R.segment_error_uncalibrated\n    e_aisle_cal = R.segment_error_calibrated\n    aisle_ratio_uncal = R.segment_over_bound_uncalibrated\n    cal_gain = R.calibration_gain\n    speed_ratio = R.speed_ratio\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(path_length.magnitude - 16.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;UMBmark path should be 16 m, got {path_length}.&quot;)\n    check(d_uncal &lt; aisle_length, &quot;Uncalibrated odometry must spend delta_loc within one aisle.&quot;)\n    check(d_cal &gt; aisle_length, &quot;Calibrated lab odometry holds delta_loc over one aisle, barely.&quot;)\n    check(d_cal &lt; 2 * aisle_length, &quot;Even calibrated odometry must spend delta_loc within two aisles.&quot;)\n    check(sigma_run &gt; e_cal, &quot;Run-to-run spread must exceed the calibrated systematic error.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────\n    delta_loc_str = fmt_length(delta_loc, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    square_side_str = fmt_length(square_side, unit=meter, precision=0)\n    path_length_str = fmt_length(path_length, unit=meter, precision=0)\n    v_test_str = fmt_velocity(v_test, precision=1)\n    e_uncal_str = fmt_length(e_uncal, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    e_cal_str = fmt_length(e_cal, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    sigma_run_str = fmt_length(sigma_run, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    eps_uncal_pct_str = fmt_percent(eps_uncal, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    eps_cal_pct_str = fmt_percent(eps_cal, precision=2, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    cal_gain_mult_str = fmt_multiple(cal_gain, precision=1)\n    d_uncal_str = fmt_length(d_uncal, unit=meter, precision=1)\n    t_uncal_str = fmt_latency(t_uncal, unit=second, precision=1)\n    d_cal_str = fmt_length(d_cal, unit=meter, precision=1)\n    t_cal_str = fmt_latency(t_cal, unit=second, precision=1)\n    v_aisle_str = fmt_velocity(v_aisle, precision=1)\n    aisle_length_str = fmt_length(aisle_length, unit=meter, precision=0)\n    e_aisle_uncal_str = fmt_length(e_aisle_uncal, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    e_aisle_cal_str = fmt_length(e_aisle_cal, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    aisle_ratio_uncal_mult_str = fmt_multiple(aisle_ratio_uncal, precision=1)\n    speed_ratio_mult_str = fmt_multiple(speed_ratio, precision=1)\n]```\n\nHow fast odometry spends that bound can be measured. The standard protocol for quantifying systematic odometry drift is Borenstein and Feng&#x27;s University of Michigan Benchmark (UMBmark) [@borenstein1996measurement]. A differential-drive robot traverses a `[OdometryDrift.square_side_str]` square path covering `[OdometryDrift.path_length_str]` of total travel, executing five runs clockwise and five runs counterclockwise. By comparing the odometric return pose against ground-truth external measurements, the benchmark calculates the average return error across both directions. Traversal in opposite directions isolates the two primary sources of systematic error, unequal wheel diameters and wheelbase uncertainty, which would otherwise partially mask one another on unidirectional trajectories.\n\nOn their experimental base, uncalibrated runs produced an average systematic return error of `[OdometryDrift.e_uncal_str]`. Calibrating the wheelbase and wheel-diameter ratio reduced this error to `[OdometryDrift.e_cal_str]`, achieving a `[OdometryDrift.cal_gain_mult_str]` precision gain. Normalized by path length, the systematic drift rate drops from `[OdometryDrift.eps_uncal_pct_str]` to `[OdometryDrift.eps_cal_pct_str]` of distance traveled. At the uncalibrated rate, a mobile base exhausts an illustrative `[OdometryDrift.delta_loc_str]` localization bound after traversing only `[OdometryDrift.d_uncal_str]`, equivalent to `[OdometryDrift.t_uncal_str]` of operation at an aisle cruising speed of `[OdometryDrift.v_aisle_str]`. Traversing a single `[OdometryDrift.aisle_length_str]` aisle accumulates `[OdometryDrift.e_aisle_uncal_str]` of position drift, exceeding the bound by a factor of `[OdometryDrift.aisle_ratio_uncal_mult_str]`. Even with careful calibration, pure odometry exhausts the `[OdometryDrift.delta_loc_str]` bound after `[OdometryDrift.d_cal_str]` (`[OdometryDrift.t_cal_str]`), accumulating `[OdometryDrift.e_aisle_cal_str]` across one aisle. Plotted against distance driven (@fig-09-odometry-drift), even the calibrated line crosses the bound only just past one `[OdometryDrift.aisle_length_str]` aisle, which makes $\\LocalizationBound$ a constraint on how often external map corrections arrive rather than an intrinsic property of the mobile base.\n\nThree physical realities prevent this illustrative calibrated extrapolation from establishing an operational design margin. First, the laboratory benchmark operated at `[OdometryDrift.v_test_str]` along straight segments to minimize wheel slip, stopping to pivot at each corner; production warehouse bases travel `[OdometryDrift.speed_ratio_mult_str]` faster under varying operational payloads. Second, kinematic calibration eliminates only deterministic systematic errors. Non-systematic stochastic errors (floor irregularities and micro-slip) produced a run-to-run standard deviation of approximately `[OdometryDrift.sigma_run_str]`, eclipsing the residual calibrated systematic error of `[OdometryDrift.e_cal_str]`. Third, a constant per-meter error rate is merely a first-order linearization. An uncorrected heading error $\\SmallChange\\HeadingAngle$ produces lateral position displacement $\\SmallChange\\HeadingAngle\\,\\TraverseDistance$ over a straight traverse of distance $\\TraverseDistance$. Because wheel-diameter mismatch causes heading error to accumulate linearly with distance ($\\SmallChange\\HeadingAngle \\approx \\HeadingCurvature \\TraverseDistance$ for a constant heading curvature $\\HeadingCurvature$), the resulting lateral error grows quadratically, about $\\tfrac{1}{2}\\HeadingCurvature \\TraverseDistance^2$, quadrupling each time the straight run doubles. Under this illustrative drift model, reserving localization margin requires external corrections at intervals shorter than the calculated crossing distance; the deployed interval must come from the machine’s qualified drift envelope. In deployed systems, the site parameter $\\LocalizationBound$ must be established empirically, by driving the robot past surveyed fiducials at nominal velocity and maximum payload across actual facility flooring, reporting drift rates at a defined operating percentile.\n\n::: {#fig-09-odometry-drift fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Odometry drift against the localization bound**: The UMBmark return error, scaled per meter of path, grows linearly with distance driven. Uncalibrated, it spends the `[OdometryDrift.delta_loc_str]` localization bound after `[OdometryDrift.d_uncal_str]`; calibrated, after `[OdometryDrift.d_cal_str]`, only just beyond one `[OdometryDrift.aisle_length_str]` aisle. Both lines extrapolate return errors from a slow laboratory run; deployed correction spacing must be qualified against the machine’s own drift envelope before $\\\\LocalizationBound$ can be relied on.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Line plot of accumulated odometry error in millimeters against distance driven in meters, from 0 to 60 m. A steep line for uncalibrated odometry leaves the top of the plot within a few meters, and a shallow line for calibrated odometry rises slowly. A red dashed horizontal line marks the localization bound, and markers show where each line crosses it. A gray dashed vertical line marks the length of one aisle.&quot;}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ ODOMETRY DRIFT AGAINST THE LOCALIZATION BOUND (FIGURE)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @fig-09-odometry-drift in @sec-memory-localization-slam</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    show that delta_loc is a premise about how often corrections</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          arrive, not a property of the base</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    error vs distance for uncalibrated and calibrated odometry,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the delta_loc line, the crossings, one aisle length</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     e(d) = eps * d with eps from the OdometryDrift cell</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>import numpy as np from mlsysim import viz import matplotlib.pyplot as plt from mlsysim.core.units import meter, millimeter</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>OD = OdometryDrift DLOC = OD.delta_loc.to(millimeter).magnitude  # category: illustrative (from the cell) EU, EC = OD.eps_uncal, OD.eps_cal  # category: literature-derived (from the cell) DU = OD.d_uncal.to(meter).magnitude DC = OD.d_cal.to(meter).magnitude LA = OD.aisle_length.to(meter).magnitude  # category: illustrative (from the cell)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>d = np.linspace(0, 2 <em> LA, 400) check(abs(EU </em> DU <em> 1000 - DLOC) &lt; 1e-6 and abs(EC </em> DC * 1000 - DLOC) &lt; 1e-6, \"Crossings must spend delta_loc exactly.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. PLOT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>viz.set_book_style() C = viz.COLORS fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.2, 3.6)) ax.plot(d, EU <em> d </em> 1000, color=C[\"OrangeLine\"], lw=1.8, label=\"uncalibrated odometry\") ax.plot(d, EC <em> d </em> 1000, color=C[\"BlueLine\"], lw=1.8, label=\"calibrated odometry\") ax.axhline(DLOC, color=C[\"RedLine\"], lw=1.2, ls=(0, (5, 4))) ax.text(7, DLOC + 3, rf\"localization bound $\\delta_{{loc}}$ = {OD.delta_loc_str}\", ha=\"left\", va=\"bottom\", fontsize=9, color=C[\"RedLine\"]) ax.axvline(LA, color=\"#A0AEC0\", lw=0.9, ls=(0, (3, 3))) ax.text(LA + 0.6, 112, f\"one aisle, {OD.aisle_length_str}\", ha=\"left\", va=\"top\", fontsize=9, color=\"#4A5568\") for x, c in ((DU, C[\"OrangeLine\"]), (DC, C[\"BlueLine\"])): ax.plot([x], [DLOC], \"o\", ms=5, color=c, zorder=5) ax.annotate( OD.d_uncal_str, xy=(DU, DLOC), xytext=(DU + 3.5, DLOC + 30), fontsize=9, color=C[\"OrangeLine\"], arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-\", lw=0.7, color=C[\"OrangeLine\"]), ) ax.annotate( OD.d_cal_str, xy=(DC, DLOC), xytext=(DC + 3, DLOC - 25), fontsize=9, color=C[\"BlueLine\"], arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-\", lw=0.7, color=C[\"BlueLine\"]), ) ax.set_xlim(0, 2 * LA) ax.set_ylim(0, 120) ax.set_xlabel(\"Distance driven (m)\", fontsize=9) ax.set_ylabel(\"Odometry error (mm)\", fontsize=9) ax.tick_params(labelsize=9) ax.legend(loc=\"upper right\", bbox_to_anchor=(1.0, 0.86), fontsize=9, frameon=False) plt.tight_layout() plt.show() ]```</p>\n<p>Those laboratory calibration rates isolate the systematic part of wheel-odometry error on a slow square path. Camera and lidar odometry are measured differently, by total error at road speed over long runs. A public driving benchmark scores each submitted method by its translation error averaged over every subsequence of 100 to 800 m in held-out sequences, in percent of distance traveled [@geiger2012are]. @Fig-09-kitti-drift places every ranked method against the distance at which its drift rate would spend the machine's localization bound.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ VISUAL AND LIDAR ODOMETRY DRIFT AGAINST THE LOCALIZATION BOUND (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @fig-09-kitti-drift and the paragraph after it.\n# │ Goal: Show that even the best published odometry spends the bound\n# │       within one aisle.\n# │ Show: Best and median translation error for stereo and lidar methods;\n# │       distance to exhaust delta_loc at each; the aisle length.\n# │ How:  data/kitti_odometry_leaderboard.csv; delta_loc and aisle length from\n# │       Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle; d = delta_loc / eps.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport numpy as np\nfrom mlsysim import Embodied\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import meter\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt, fmt_int, fmt_percent\nfrom binder.tools.figures import style as book_style\n\nclass KittiDrift:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants) ──────────────────────────────────────────────\n    AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n    rows = book_style.load_series(&quot;vol4/09_memory/data/kitti_odometry_leaderboard.csv&quot;).entries\n    delta_loc_m = AISLE.delta_loc.to(meter).magnitude\n    aisle_m = AISLE.aisle_length.to(meter).magnitude\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (The Compute) ────────────────────────────────────────\n    _err = np.array([float(r[&quot;translation_err_pct&quot;]) for r in rows])\n    _stereo = np.array([float(r[&quot;translation_err_pct&quot;]) for r in rows if r[&quot;sensor_group&quot;] == &quot;stereo&quot;])\n    _laser = np.array([float(r[&quot;translation_err_pct&quot;]) for r in rows if r[&quot;sensor_group&quot;] == &quot;laser&quot;])\n    best = float(_err.min())\n    stereo_med, laser_med = float(np.median(_stereo)), float(np.median(_laser))\n    d_best = delta_loc_m / (best / 100.0)\n    d_stereo_med = delta_loc_m / (stereo_med / 100.0)\n    d_laser_med = delta_loc_m / (laser_med / 100.0)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(d_best &lt; aisle_m, &quot;Prose says even the best method spends the bound within one aisle&quot;)\n    check(d_stereo_med &lt; d_laser_med &lt; d_best, &quot;Prose orders the median stereo, median lidar, and best distances&quot;)\n    check(d_best &lt; aisle_m / 3, &quot;Prose says the best method spends the bound within a third of one aisle&quot;)\n    check(best / 100 &gt; OdometryDrift.eps_cal, &quot;Prose says the total-error rates sit above the calibrated UMBmark rate&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    n_ranked_str = fmt_int(len(rows))\n    best_pct_str = fmt(best, precision=2)\n    stereo_med_str = fmt(round(float(stereo_med), 2), precision=2)\n    laser_med_str = fmt(round(float(laser_med), 2), precision=2)\n    d_best_str = fmt_int(round(d_best))\n    d_stereo_med_str = fmt_int(round(d_stereo_med))\n    d_laser_med_str = fmt_int(round(d_laser_med))\n    aisle_str = fmt_int(round(aisle_m))\n    delta_loc_mm_str = fmt_int(round(delta_loc_m * 1000))\n]```\n\n:::: {#fig-09-kitti-drift fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Even the best odometry needs a correction within one aisle**: Each dot is one ranked method on a public odometry benchmark, placed by its translation error (percent of distance, averaged over 100 to 800 m subsequences) and grouped by sensor; the remaining group includes mixed stereo–lidar sensor sets and untagged methods, whose inputs include monocular, inertial, and event cameras. The top axis converts error into the distance at which a constant drift rate spends the running machine&#x27;s `[KittiDrift.delta_loc_mm_str]` mm localization bound, and the shaded region is shorter than one `[KittiDrift.aisle_str]` m aisle. The benchmark is road driving, so it bounds what these methods achieve rather than predicting warehouse drift. Data from the benchmark leaderboard [@geiger2012are].&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Strip plot with three rows labeled stereo camera, lidar, and mixed or untagged against translation error from 0.4 to about 20 percent on a logarithmic axis. Stereo and lidar rows cluster between 0.5 and 2 percent; the mixed or untagged row spreads more widely to the right. A secondary top axis converts error to meters of travel before a 50 millimeter bound is spent, decreasing to the right. A shaded band covers all distances shorter than one 30 meter aisle and contains every dot.&quot;}\n\n\n\n::::\n\nThe best of the `[KittiDrift.n_ranked_str]` ranked methods drifts `[KittiDrift.best_pct_str]` percent of distance, and the median stereo and lidar methods drift `[KittiDrift.stereo_med_str]` and `[KittiDrift.laser_med_str]` percent. At those rates the `[KittiDrift.delta_loc_mm_str]` mm bound lasts about `[KittiDrift.d_best_str]` m for the best method, `[KittiDrift.d_laser_med_str]` m for the median lidar method, and `[KittiDrift.d_stereo_med_str]` m for the median stereo method, all shorter than one `[KittiDrift.aisle_str]` m aisle. These total-error rates sit above the calibrated laboratory figure of `[OdometryDrift.eps_cal_pct_str]`, because they include the slip, vibration, and scene-dependent errors that UMBmark&#x27;s calibration deliberately excludes. Read together, the two benchmarks bracket the problem rather than resolve it: the optimistic systematic rate stretches the bound just past one aisle, and the measured total rates of the best published methods spend it within a third of one. Every odometry source on the base is a bridge between external corrections, and the bound remains a requirement on how often those corrections arrive.\n\nWhen those external corrections arrive as a SLAM loop closure, they introduce a consistency challenge that timestamp-based age tracking alone cannot resolve. An occupancy cell anchored in the global map frame retains its original capture epoch (the photons reflected from that obstacle are no newer), yet its spatial coordinates shift whenever the SLAM backend re-optimizes the underlying pose graph. Although the sensor evidence has not been refreshed, its spatial position changes discontinuously. Any local memory snapshot constructed prior to the loop closure now misplaces the obstacle by the magnitude of the graph correction, even while every timestamp within the snapshot passes validation. Robust spatial memory architectures must therefore tag spatial beliefs with the generation or revision identifier of the pose graph alongside individual cell epochs. When a loop closure shifts supporting trajectory poses, the system must either re-anchor dependent snapshots or invalidate them immediately.\n\nThe REP 105 frame convention met in @tbl-08-rep105-tree already separates the two estimators [@meeussen2010rep105]. Poses in `odom` are continuous but drift without bound. Poses in `map` stay globally bounded but jump whenever a scan-matching correction or loop closure resolves accumulated drift, and the localizer absorbs each correction by adjusting only the `map`-to-`odom` edge. What memory must decide is which frame its safety snapshot lives in. The permission controller builds its local collision-checking snapshots in the continuous `odom` frame, so a correction never moves an obstacle inside a snapshot that is in use. Over a snapshot&#x27;s short lifespan (typically hundreds of milliseconds to several seconds), the drift that calibrated odometry accumulates is small and is charged against $\\LocalizationBound$ in proportion to distance traversed; uncalibrated odometry, which spends the whole bound within `[OdometryDrift.d_uncal_str]`, cannot rely on this. Anchoring safety snapshots in the `map` frame instead would make obstacle bounding hulls teleport discontinuously upon every map correction. Global mission planners, long-term semantic maps, and snapshot construction routines operate in `map`. When a major map correction occurs, the host invalidates and rebuilds the local snapshot rather than forcing real-time controllers to absorb discontinuous jumps. The `map`-to-`odom` edge remains subject to the same temporal expiration rules as any kinematic transform.\n\nA stale transform displaces both the tool estimate and any observations mapped through it. The surrounding world ages too, but it has no joint encoders to report its motion. Memory must therefore record which surfaces and empty regions the sensors actually verified, as well as the frames in which those observations hold.\n\n## Certified Spatial Assertions in Scene Memory {#sec-memory-scene-attention-buffers}\n\nWhen a mobile manipulator sweeps its range sensors down an aisle, spatial memory must partition the physical volume by what the sensors established: space observed to be occupied, space observed to be empty, and unobserved space. Only observed empty space can support a physical clearance claim, and only while that evidence remains temporally valid; once it lapses, the space becomes a fourth category, expired, which @sec-memory-volumetric-metric-maps adds to the cell states. If a map representation merges unobserved space with free space, the permission path cannot detect occlusions, defeating evidence-freshness checks regardless of timestamp accuracy.\n\nDistinct downstream consumers place competing demands on this spatial memory architecture. A learned manipulation policy requires object identities and local geometry to plan collision-free grasps or navigation trajectories. In contrast, the hard-real-time permission path requires bounded geometric queries whose supporting evidence can be verified before an execution deadline expires. To satisfy these disparate consumers, spatial memory organizes into a functional hierarchy. Metric maps compute geometric clearances and volumetric occupancy. 3D scene graphs structure those measurements into topological places and semantically labeled objects. Episodic memory retains interaction histories needed to interpret operational context. While higher layers draw upon metric observations for geometric grounding, semantic labels and remembered events never possess the authority to certify physical clearance on their own.\n\n### Volumetric metric maps and distance fields {#sec-memory-volumetric-metric-maps}\n\nAt the metric layer, a clearance query along a warehouse aisle demands a single, safety-critical quantity: the minimum distance from the robot&#x27;s body hull (the simple solid that encloses the machine&#x27;s body for collision checking) to the nearest region that is occupied, unobserved, or backed by expired evidence. In contrast, a neural or graphics rendering query estimates what a camera would observe from a specific vantage point. If the safety microcontroller had to scan thousands of occupancy voxels or chase pointer-linked octree nodes to find the nearest obstacle for each of the robot&#x27;s body spheres, the clearance check would overrun its microsecond budget inside the permission tick. A spatial representation qualifies for the hard-real-time permission path only if it evaluates conservative physical clearance within the strict execution deadline of a permission tick, operating entirely within the memory footprint supported by the safety microcontroller. The design therefore allocates the query workload first, counts what each candidate representation must read from memory to answer it, and only then sizes the storage.\n\n#### The clearance query inside the permission tick {.unnumbered}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ CLEARANCE QUERY COST AND LOCAL SNAPSHOT STORAGE BY REPRESENTATION</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-memory-volumetric-metric-maps (query semantics, read budget,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          snapshot sizing), @tbl-09-representation-queries,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @fig-09-memory-3d-representation-pareto.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Replace stipulated latencies with counts a reader can recompute:</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          memory reads per sphere-hull clearance query, and the bytes a</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          local snapshot of the stopping corridor needs.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    the per-query read budget on the permission MCU; reads for a</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          dense occupancy scan, a worst-case octree scan, and an ESDF</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          lookup at 2, 5, and 10 cm; snapshot bytes against MCU SRAM.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.WarehousePermissionClearanceQuery (ClearanceQueryModel):</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          reads budget = t_query x SRAM bandwidth / cell bytes;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          occupancy scan = (4/3) pi ((r_hull + delta_margin) / r)^3 cells;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          octree = scan x ceil(log2(L_map / r)) dependent reads;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          ESDF = 8 geometric reads (plus fixed header checks); payload = V_slab / r^3 x</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          cell bytes; quantization = (sqrt 3 / 2) r.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>import math from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_qty, fmt_qty_int from mlsysim.core.units import byte, centimeter, dimensionless, kilobyte, kilohertz, megabyte, meter, millimeter</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class RepresentationQuery:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>QUERY = Scenarios.WarehousePermissionClearanceQuery  # category: illustrative (allocation, hulls, cell layouts; MLSysIM scenario) f_tick = RM.permission_rate  # category: chosen (permission-loop rate) t_reflex = QUERY.collision_allocation  # category: chosen (collision-check allocation within the permission tick) n_hulls = QUERY.hulls  # category: illustrative (sphere hulls covering the machine, worked case) n_hulls_min = QUERY.fewest_hulls  # category: illustrative (fewest evaluation spheres considered) r_hull = QUERY.hull_radius  # category: illustrative (sphere-hull radius) delta_margin = AISLE.delta_margin  # category: chosen (protective clearance) sram_bw = RM.permission_mcu.memory.sram_bandwidth  # category: datasheet (permission MCU SRAM, reference class) sram_cap = RM.permission_mcu.memory.sram_capacity  # category: datasheet (permission MCU SRAM, reference class) s_esdf = QUERY.esdf_cell_bytes  # category: illustrative (4 B distance, 2 B weight, 2 B evidence epoch) s_occ = QUERY.occupancy_cell_bytes  # category: illustrative (2 B log-odds, 2 B evidence epoch) l_map = QUERY.map_edge  # category: illustrative (host-map edge, the 10 m workcell below) aisle_width = AISLE.aisle_width  # category: illustrative (aisle width) d_clear = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative (clear distance ahead) h_slab = QUERY.slab_height  # category: chosen (snapshot height covering the arm's reach) n_trilinear = QUERY.trilinear_reads  # category: derived (corner cells of one trilinear interpolation)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>R = QUERY.result pitches_cm = tuple(R.rows)  # voxel pitches compared, cm t_tick = R.tick frac_spare = R.tick_spare_share  # tick left for other permission work t_query = R.query_time reads_budget = R.reads_budget reach = R.reach v_slab = R.slab_volume rows = { cm: dict( occ_reads=row.occupancy_reads, depth=row.octree_depth, oct_reads=row.octree_reads, esdf_reads=row.esdf_reads, esdf_bytes=row.esdf_bytes, occ_bytes=row.occupancy_bytes, quant=row.quantization, ) for cm, row in R.rows.items() } r2, r5, r10 = rows[2], rows[5], rows[10]</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(t_query.magnitude - 2.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Per-query allocation should be 2 us, got {t_query}.\") check(abs(frac_spare - 0.90) &lt; 1e-9, f\"The allocation should leave 90 percent of the tick, got {frac_spare}.\") check(n_hulls_min &lt; n_hulls, \"The hull range must be ordered.\") check(abs(reads_budget - 800) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Read budget should be 800 cells, got {reads_budget}.\") check(r5[\"occ_reads\"] &gt; reads_budget &gt; r10[\"occ_reads\"], \"An occupancy scan must fit only at the coarsest pitch.\") check(r10[\"oct_reads\"] &gt; reads_budget, \"A worst-case octree scan must miss the budget at every pitch.\") check(n_trilinear &lt; reads_budget, \"The geometric ESDF reads must stay below the bandwidth ceiling at every pitch.\") check(r5[\"esdf_bytes\"] &lt; sram_cap &lt; r2[\"esdf_bytes\"], \"The 5 cm voxel payload must lie below SRAM capacity and the 2 cm payload above it.\") check(r10[\"quant\"] &gt; delta_margin / 2, \"At 10 cm, quantization alone must exceed half the protective clearance.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>t_query_str = fmt_latency(t_query, unit=microsecond, precision=0) f_tick_str = fmt_frequency(f_tick, unit=kilohertz, precision=0) t_tick_str = fmt_latency(t_tick, unit=microsecond, precision=0, commas=True) t_reflex_str = fmt_latency(t_reflex, unit=microsecond, precision=0) n_hulls_str = fmt_int(n_hulls) n_hulls_min_str = fmt_int(n_hulls_min) frac_spare_pct_str = fmt_percent(frac_spare, precision=0, style=\"prose\") sram_bw_str = fmt_bandwidth(sram_bw, precision=1) sram_cap_str = fmt_memory(sram_cap, unit=megabyte, precision=2) reads_budget_str = fmt_int(reads_budget) r_hull_str = fmt_length(r_hull, unit=meter, precision=2) reach_str = fmt_length(reach, unit=meter, precision=2) v_slab_str = fmt_qty(v_slab, meter**3, precision=2) h_slab_str = fmt_length(h_slab, unit=meter, precision=0) occ2_str, occ5_str, occ10_str = (fmt_int(r[\"occ_reads\"]) for r in (r2, r5, r10)) oct2_str, oct5_str, oct10_str = (fmt_int(r[\"oct_reads\"]) for r in (r2, r5, r10)) depth5_str = fmt_int(r5[\"depth\"]) esdf_reads_str = fmt_int(n_trilinear) esdf2_bytes_str = fmt_memory(r2[\"esdf_bytes\"], unit=megabyte, precision=2) esdf5_bytes_str = fmt_qty_int(r5[\"esdf_bytes\"], kilobyte) esdf10_bytes_str = fmt_memory(r10[\"esdf_bytes\"], unit=kilobyte, precision=1) quant5_str = fmt_qty_int(r5[\"quant\"], millimeter) quant10_str = fmt_qty_int(r10[\"quant\"], millimeter) ]```</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ COLLISION ALLOCATION INSIDE THE PERMISSION TICK&#x27;S DECLARED WCET\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-memory-volumetric-metric-maps (reconciles the collision\n# │          allocation with the tick budget of @sec-nervous).\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Place the 100 us collision allocation inside the permission\n# │          job&#x27;s declared 250 us WCET and 400 us deadline.\n# │ Show:    share of the declared WCET the collision check takes (40 percent).\n# │ How:     share = t_reflex / C_i; WCET and deadline from the WarehouseAisle\n# │          scenario (the same values @fig-04-permission-tick plots).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_latency, fmt_percent, check\n\nclass CollisionWcetShare:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;The collision allocation as a share of the permission job&#x27;s declared WCET.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    t_wcet = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle.enforcer_wcet  # category: chosen (declared WCET of the permission job)\n    t_deadline = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle.enforcer_deadline  # category: chosen (relative deadline of the permission job)\n    t_reflex = RepresentationQuery.t_reflex  # category: chosen (collision-check allocation)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    share = Scenarios.WarehousePermissionClearanceQuery.result.wcet_share  # t_reflex / t_wcet\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(t_reflex &lt; t_wcet &lt; t_deadline, &quot;The collision allocation must sit inside the declared WCET, inside the deadline.&quot;)\n    check(abs(share - 0.40) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Collision share of the WCET should be 40 percent: {share}&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    t_wcet_str = fmt_latency(t_wcet.to(microsecond), unit=microsecond, precision=0)\n    t_deadline_str = fmt_latency(t_deadline.to(microsecond), unit=microsecond, precision=0)\n    share_pct_str = fmt_percent(share, precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n]```\n\nThe worked design allocates spatial collision checking within the machine&#x27;s `[RepresentationQuery.f_tick_str]` permission loop ($\\PermissionLoopPeriod =$ `[RepresentationQuery.t_tick_str]`). The required query returns conservative clearance at each body collision hull, where a hull is one of a set of spheres, the evaluation spheres, whose union covers the machine&#x27;s body. Where the selected check also needs a clearance gradient, that work belongs in the same measured query. For an illustrative `[RepresentationQuery.n_hulls_min_str]` to `[RepresentationQuery.n_hulls_str]` evaluation spheres, the total work is:\n$$\\TotalCollisionQueryTime = \\sum_{k=1}^{\\CollisionHullCount} \\HullQueryTime(\\CartesianPosition_k) \\le \\ReflexCollisionBudget$$ {#eq-mem-query-latency-budget}\nTo bound the total collision workload in @eq-mem-query-latency-budget, the worked $\\CollisionHullCount =$ `[RepresentationQuery.n_hulls_str]` case reserves $\\ReflexCollisionBudget =$ `[RepresentationQuery.t_reflex_str]` for local collision checking (the subscript names the reflexive, every-tick check, as opposed to planning). That allocation is part of the permission tick schedule of @sec-nervous (@fig-04-permission-tick), not an addition to it. The job declares a `[CollisionWcetShare.t_wcet_str]` WCET against a `[CollisionWcetShare.t_deadline_str]` deadline, and collision checking takes `[CollisionWcetShare.share_pct_str]` of that WCET, leaving the rest for the other admission checks. Measured against the whole period instead, collision checking leaves `[RepresentationQuery.frac_spare_pct_str]` of the tick for everything else. This is an allocation to be tested on the deployed controller; full-map updates and trajectory optimization run outside this tick. Dividing the allocation among the hulls gives\n$$\\HullQueryTime \\le \\frac{\\ReflexCollisionBudget}{\\CollisionHullCount},$$\nwhich for the worked case is `[RepresentationQuery.t_reflex_str]` over `[RepresentationQuery.n_hulls_str]` hulls, or `[RepresentationQuery.t_query_str]` per query.\n\nA latency allocation becomes a concrete architectural constraint only when translated into hardware resources: memory bus transactions and cache reads. The permission controller streams its local SRAM at `[RepresentationQuery.sram_bw_str]`, so the `[RepresentationQuery.t_query_str]` allocation covers at most `[RepresentationQuery.reads_budget_str]` reads of an 8-byte cell per hull query, before any arithmetic, cache effect, or contention; qualification on the deployed controller must still measure the real bound. Against that ceiling, the candidate representations differ less in execution speed than in the physical question a single read answers (@tbl-09-representation-queries). An **occupancy grid** stores, per cell, whether that cell is occupied [@elfes1989using; @moravec1985high]. Certifying that a sphere hull of radius `[RepresentationQuery.r_hull_str]` keeps its protective clearance means reading every cell within `[RepresentationQuery.reach_str]` of its center, $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi\\left((\\HullSphereRadius+\\ProtectiveClearanceMargin)/\\VoxelPitch\\right)^3$ cells at pitch $\\VoxelPitch$. A hierarchical **octree** such as OctoMap [@hornung2013octomap] answers the same point query after descending the tree, one dependent read per level, so near surfaces, where pruning cannot merge cells, the scan costs its cell count times the tree depth. A **truncated signed distance field (TSDF)** stores a projective distance to an observed surface along the sensor ray, clamped at a truncation distance of a few voxels [@curless1996volumetric]. This ray-based construction does not give the nearest-obstacle distance in all directions, even within the truncation band; hull clearance requires a separately validated occupancy or Euclidean-distance conversion. A **Euclidean signed distance field (ESDF)** stores the straight-line distance to the nearest obstacle [@oleynikova2017voxblox; @millane2024nvblox], so one interpolated geometric lookup, `[RepresentationQuery.esdf_reads_str]` corner reads together with the snapshot validity checks developed in the paragraphs that follow, supplies conservative clearance for an entire sphere. The hull passes when the distance at its center exceeds $\\HullSphereRadius + \\ProtectiveClearanceMargin$ plus the geometric error. Rendering representations answer the second question. A 3D Gaussian splatting (3DGS) scene fits anisotropic Gaussians to a photometric loss [@kerbl20233d], and a neural radiance field integrates learned density along camera rays. Neither stores a conservative distance, and collision checking on splats requires an added assumption that the fitted ellipsoids enclose the physical surface [@chen2025splatnav].\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The parameterization of a 3D Gaussian splatting scene is developed in @sec-appendix-ml-3dgs.\n:::\n\n: **Occupancy Grid**: Moravec and Elfes built the first grids at Carnegie Mellon to fuse returns from wide-beam sonar, whose single echo cannot say where across its beam the reflector lies [@moravec1985high]. Each cell therefore holds accumulated evidence rather than geometry, and a cell no return has reached stays at its prior. The representation was designed for sensors too coarse to vouch for what they did not touch, which is why a permission-grade grid must keep unknown distinct from free. \n\n| Representation         | Query one lookup answers                      |                  Reads per hull query at 5 cm | Can certify the hull&#x27;s clearance?                              |\n|:-----------------------|:----------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------:|:---------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Dense occupancy grid   | Is this cell occupied?                        |       `[RepresentationQuery.occ5_str]` | Yes, by scanning every cell within reach                       |\n| Octree occupancy       | Is this cell occupied? (after a tree descent) |       `[RepresentationQuery.oct5_str]` | Yes, by the same scan, one descent per cell                    |\n| TSDF                   | Is a surface within the truncation distance?  |                                             1 | No general Euclidean hull guarantee from projective TSDF alone |\n| ESDF                   | How far is the nearest obstacle?              | `[RepresentationQuery.esdf_reads_str]` | Yes, under a validated conservative snapshot certificate       |\n| 3DGS or radiance field | What would a camera at this pose see?         |                                             — | No; requires a separately qualified geometric conversion       |\n\n: **What one lookup answers, and what clearance costs**: Reads for a sphere hull of radius `[RepresentationQuery.r_hull_str]` plus the protective clearance, at a 5 cm pitch, against a budget of `[RepresentationQuery.reads_budget_str]` reads per query on the permission controller. ESDF counts are geometric corner reads; fixed header checks also consume the allocation. The octree count is the worst case near surfaces, a tree depth of `[RepresentationQuery.depth5_str]` levels per cell; pruning reduces it in open space. {#tbl-09-representation-queries tbl-colwidths=&quot;[20,32,16,32]&quot;}\n\n::: {#fig-09-memory-3d-representation-pareto fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Clearance query cost and snapshot storage by representation family**: (a) Memory reads to certify one sphere hull&#x27;s clearance, computed from voxel pitch, hull radius plus protective clearance, and tree depth, against the per-query read budget that the 2 µs allocation and the permission controller&#x27;s SRAM bandwidth allow. Occupancy scans grow with the cube of the inverse pitch and fit only at the coarsest pitch; an ESDF answers with one interpolated lookup at every pitch. (b) Voxel payload for a dense snapshot of the stopping corridor (aisle width by clear distance by a chosen 2 m height) against the controller&#x27;s SRAM. Fixed header checks also consume the query allocation, and metadata, active buffers, binary, and validation workspace add to resident storage. Counts, not measured latencies; rendering representations are absent because they do not answer a clearance query.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Two stacked log-scale panels against voxel pitch of 2, 5, and 10 cm. Top panel a, memory reads per sphere-hull clearance query: worst-case octree scan from about 200,000 to about 1,300 reads, dense occupancy scan from about 22,000 to 180 reads, and ESDF lookup flat at 8 reads, with a dashed red read budget at 800 per query, shaded above, that only the ESDF clears at every pitch and the occupancy scan clears only at 10 cm. Bottom panel b, voxel payload in kilobytes: ESDF at 8 bytes per cell falls from about 2,900 kB to 23 kB and occupancy at 4 bytes per cell from about 1,400 kB to 11 kB, with a dashed red line at the permission MCU SRAM of about 1 MB, shaded above, that both voxel payloads exceed at 2 cm and lie below at 5 and 10 cm.&quot;}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ CLEARANCE QUERY COST AND SNAPSHOT STORAGE (FIGURE)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @fig-09-memory-3d-representation-pareto</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    show which representation answers a sphere-hull clearance query</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          inside the per-query read budget, and which pitch fits the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          permission MCU's SRAM</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    reads per query vs pitch (occupancy scan, octree scan, ESDF);</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          snapshot storage vs pitch against SRAM capacity</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     all values from the RepresentationQuery cell</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import viz</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>RQ = RepresentationQuery PITCH = list(RQ.pitches_cm)  # cm, category: chosen (as in the cell) OCC = [RQ.rows[p][\"occ_reads\"] for p in PITCH] OCT = [RQ.rows[p][\"oct_reads\"] for p in PITCH] ESDF = [RQ.rows[p][\"esdf_reads\"] for p in PITCH] ESDF_KB = [RQ.rows[p][\"esdf_bytes\"].to(kilobyte).magnitude for p in PITCH] OCC_KB = [RQ.rows[p][\"occ_bytes\"].to(kilobyte).magnitude for p in PITCH] BUDGET = RQ.reads_budget SRAM_KB = RQ.sram_cap.to(kilobyte).magnitude</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. PLOT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>viz.set_book_style() C = viz.COLORS import matplotlib.pyplot as plt from mlsysim.core.units import kilobyte</p>\n<p>fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(6.2, 7.0)) FS = 9</p>\n<p>ax1.plot(PITCH, OCT, marker=\"s\", ms=4, lw=1.6, color=C[\"OrangeLine\"], label=\"octree occupancy scan (worst case)\") ax1.plot(PITCH, OCC, marker=\"o\", ms=4, lw=1.6, color=C[\"primary\"], label=\"dense occupancy scan\") ax1.plot(PITCH, ESDF, marker=\"^\", ms=4, lw=1.6, color=C[\"BlueLine\"], label=\"ESDF lookup (trilinear)\") ax1.axhline(BUDGET, color=C[\"RedLine\"], lw=1.2, ls=(0, (5, 4))) ax1.axhspan(BUDGET, 3e6, color=C[\"RedFill\"], alpha=0.35, lw=0, zorder=0) ax1.text(1.7, BUDGET * 0.6, f\"read budget: {BUDGET:.0f} per query\", ha=\"left\", va=\"top\", fontsize=FS, color=C[\"RedLine\"]) ax1.set_yscale(\"log\") ax1.set_xticks(PITCH) ax1.set_xlim(1.5, 10.5) ax1.set_ylim(3, 3e6) ax1.set_xlabel(\"Voxel pitch (cm)\", fontsize=9) ax1.set_ylabel(\"Memory reads per sphere-hull query\", fontsize=9) ax1.set_title(\"(a) Clearance query cost\", loc=\"left\", fontsize=9) ax1.legend(loc=\"upper right\", fontsize=9) ax1.tick_params(labelsize=FS)</p>\n<p>ax2.plot(PITCH, ESDF_KB, marker=\"^\", ms=4, lw=1.6, color=C[\"BlueLine\"], label=\"ESDF payload, 8 B per cell\") ax2.plot(PITCH, OCC_KB, marker=\"o\", ms=4, lw=1.6, color=C[\"primary\"], ls=(0, (6, 3)), label=\"occupancy payload, 4 B per cell\") ax2.axhline(SRAM_KB, color=C[\"RedLine\"], lw=1.2, ls=(0, (5, 4))) ax2.axhspan(SRAM_KB, 6e4, color=C[\"RedFill\"], alpha=0.35, lw=0, zorder=0) ax2.text(10.1, SRAM_KB * 1.25, \"permission MCU SRAM\", ha=\"right\", va=\"bottom\", fontsize=FS, color=C[\"RedLine\"]) ax2.set_yscale(\"log\") ax2.set_xticks(PITCH) ax2.set_xlim(1.5, 10.5) ax2.set_ylim(5, 6e4) ax2.set_xlabel(\"Voxel pitch (cm)\", fontsize=9) ax2.set_ylabel(\"Voxel payload (kB)\", fontsize=9) ax2.set_title(\"(b) Stopping-corridor snapshot\", loc=\"left\", fontsize=9) ax2.legend(loc=\"upper right\", fontsize=9) ax2.tick_params(labelsize=FS)</p>\n<p>plt.tight_layout() plt.show() ]```</p>\n<p>Counted against the <code>[RepresentationQuery.reads_budget_str]</code>-read budget (@fig-09-memory-3d-representation-pareto), the representation families separate cleanly. A dense occupancy scan exceeds the budget at 2 cm and 5 cm and fits only at 10 cm, where spatial quantization error alone, up to half the voxel diagonal or <code>[RepresentationQuery.quant10_str]</code>, consumes most of the protective clearance. A worst-case octree scan exceeds the budget at every pitch, because each cell lookup first descends a chain of dependent tree pointers. An ESDF needs exactly <code>[RepresentationQuery.esdf_reads_str]</code> geometric reads for one trilinear interpolation regardless of pitch, plus fixed snapshot-header validity checks. Both the metadata checks and the geometric lookup must fit inside the query allocation. The fixed geometric cost favors an ESDF for real-time safety snapshots, while memory capacity constrains the voxel pitch.</p>\n<p>A dense ESDF snapshot of the stopping corridor, spanning the aisle width, the clear distance, and an illustrative <code>[RepresentationQuery.h_slab_str]</code> manipulator reach height (<code>[RepresentationQuery.v_slab_str]</code> in all), requires a voxel payload of <code>[RepresentationQuery.esdf5_bytes_str]</code> at 5 cm resolution, below the microcontroller's <code>[RepresentationQuery.sram_cap_str]</code> SRAM capacity. The complete static allocation must also include the snapshot header and certificate, active buffers, controller binary, and validation workspace. At 2 cm pitch, the voxel payload alone swells to <code>[RepresentationQuery.esdf2_bytes_str]</code>, exhausting available SRAM. Adopting the 5 cm pitch introduces up to <code>[RepresentationQuery.quant5_str]</code> of spatial quantization error into each lookup, an uncertainty that the collision filter must explicitly subtract from the reported clearance. A projective TSDF also cannot substitute for a Euclidean distance field simply because its point lookup is computationally inexpensive; a projective distance along a sensor ray fails to bound the distance to off-axis obstacles.</p>\n<p>The host computer maintains the global reconstruction and extracts a bounded local snapshot enclosing the proposed robot trajectory and its emergency stopping corridor. The permission microcontroller admits a new snapshot only after validating its cryptographic provenance, spatial bounding box, per-voxel evidence timestamps, and geometric consistency against a qualified schema. Generating, transferring, and validating these snapshots introduces latency and bus traffic that must be scheduled and qualified independently from the inner-loop collision query. If the host pipeline stalls or drops frames, the active snapshot in microcontroller SRAM continues to age monotonically. The robot may sustain autonomous motion only as long as every cell the snapshot relies on remains within its invalidation horizon and the snapshot's geometric coverage encloses the vehicle's stopping envelope.</p>\n<p>Eight recent corner samples do not establish that the whole region behind a distance claim remains free. Although a trilinear lookup reads only eight local corner voxels around the hull center, the Euclidean distance value stored inside each corner vouches for the absence of obstacles across a surrounding sphere of voxels that the microcontroller never inspects at runtime, and if any distant voxel within that radius carries older evidence or borders an occlusion frontier from which an unseen hazard can advance, the local distance value becomes optimistic before the corner cell's own timestamp expires. The qualified construction path therefore selects an absolute support deadline for the snapshot, fixing its frame and pose-graph revision, spatial coverage, clock mapping, and physical growth and entry-speed assumptions. Before building the frozen ESDF, it treats unknown and occupied regions as obstacles and removes from the observed free set every region whose evidence cannot support that set through the selected deadline. This conservative erosion reserves the entire declared interval, including the assumed entry and disturbance envelope. Independent validation must establish that the resulting field lower-bounds clearance throughout that interval; neither a producer's declaration nor its digest establishes the claim.</p>\n<p>The local consumer checks the fixed header against its own upper-bounded clock, the declared frame revision, coverage, and validity conditions before reading the eight geometric corners. It refuses dependent proposals at the support deadline without a host callback or a map scan; a changed frame or physical assumption invalidates the certificate earlier. Header loads and checks belong inside the same per-query allocation, and motion admission must reserve a feasible response before expiry. The later per-hull erosion calculation can impose a shorter horizon. Because the frozen field already includes the declared interval's erosion, the consumer must not subtract that identical erosion a second time; hull motion and its stopping continuation still require complete swept coverage and qualified aggregate work.</p>\n<p>↪ How host memory traffic would stall permission-path reads on a shared die, and why the selected placement confines it to evidence age, is developed in @sec-placement-resource-contention.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h4\">Volumetric memory footprint scaling</h4>\n<p>The local snapshot decision leaves the host compute platform with capacity and update constraints. Keeping the chosen snapshot supplied requires sufficient memory capacity for the underlying map and sufficient memory bandwidth to integrate range observations before their physical evidence expires.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ ILLUSTRATIVE HOST MAP SIZING: DENSE GRID VERSUS HASHED NARROW BAND\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-memory-volumetric-metric-maps, host capacity calculation.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Separate a dense workcell grid from a hashed surface band, and\n# │          voxel payload from total allocated storage.\n# │ Show:    dense grid bytes; band volume; block count; hashed payload.\n# │ How:     M_dense = V / r^3 x S; V_band = 2 delta A; N_blocks = V_band /\n# │          (B r)^3; payload = N_blocks x B^3 x S.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_memory, fmt_qty, fmt_qty_int, fmt_sci, sci_latex, check\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import byte, centimeter, dimensionless, gigabyte, megabyte, meter\n\nclass HashedVoxelPayload:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────\n    HOST = Scenarios.WorkcellHostMapSizing  # category: illustrative (workcell, pitch, band; MLSysIM scenario)\n    l_x, l_y, l_z = HOST.extent  # category: illustrative (workcell length, width, height)\n    pitch = HOST.pitch  # category: chosen (manipulation resolution)\n    voxel_bytes = HOST.voxel_bytes  # category: illustrative (distance, weight, epoch payload)\n    a_surface = HOST.surface_area  # category: illustrative (obstacle surface area in the workcell)\n    delta_band = HOST.band_half_width  # category: chosen (truncation band half-width)\n    block_side = HOST.block_edge  # category: illustrative (voxels per block edge, the nvblox layout)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    R = HOST.result\n    v_ws = R.workspace_volume\n    n_dense = R.dense_voxels\n    m_dense = R.dense_bytes\n    v_band = R.band_volume\n    blocks = R.blocks\n    payload = R.hashed_bytes\n    block_bytes = R.block_bytes\n    band_fraction = R.band_share\n    ratio = R.ratio\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(m_dense.magnitude - 2.4) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Dense grid should be 2.4 GB, got {m_dense}.&quot;)\n    check(abs(v_band.magnitude - 9.6) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Band volume should be 9.6 m^3, got {v_band}.&quot;)\n    check(abs(blocks - 18_750) &lt; 1e-6, f&quot;Block count should be 18,750, got {blocks}.&quot;)\n    check(abs(payload.magnitude - 76.8) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Voxel payload is 76.8 MB before overhead.&quot;)\n    check(abs(block_bytes.magnitude - 4096) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;One 8^3 block of 8 B voxels is 4,096 B.&quot;)\n    check(30 &lt; ratio &lt; 32, f&quot;Dense-to-hashed payload ratio should be about 31x, got {ratio}.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_ws_str = fmt_qty_int(v_ws, meter**3)\n    pitch_str = fmt_length(pitch, unit=centimeter, precision=0)\n    voxel_bytes_str = fmt_int(voxel_bytes.magnitude)\n    n_dense_str = fmt_sci(n_dense, precision=0)\n    m_dense_str = fmt_memory(m_dense, unit=gigabyte, precision=1)\n    a_surface_str = fmt_qty_int(a_surface, meter**2)\n    delta_band_str = fmt_length(delta_band, unit=centimeter, precision=0)\n    v_band_str = fmt_qty(v_band, meter**3, precision=1)\n    blocks_str = fmt_int(blocks)\n    payload_str = fmt_memory(payload, unit=megabyte, precision=1)\n    block_bytes_str = fmt_int(block_bytes.magnitude)\n    band_pct_str = fmt_percent(band_fraction, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n\n    # visible arithmetic for notebook nbk-memory-empty-air-tax\n    ratio_str = fmt_int(ratio)\n    empty_pct_str = fmt_percent(1 - band_fraction, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    l_x_str = fmt_qty_int(l_x, meter)\n    l_y_str = fmt_qty_int(l_y, meter)\n    l_z_str = fmt_qty_int(l_z, meter)\n    _m = lambda q, p=2: fmt_magnitude(q, meter, precision=p)\n    dense_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;M_{{\\text{{dense}}}} = \\frac{{{fmt_magnitude(v_ws, meter**3, precision=0)}\\ \\text{{m}}^3}}{{({_m(pitch)}\\ \\text{{m}})^3}} \\times {fmt_int(voxel_bytes.magnitude)}\\ \\text{{B}} = {sci_latex(n_dense, precision=0)} \\times {fmt_int(voxel_bytes.magnitude)}\\ \\text{{B}} = {fmt_magnitude(m_dense, gigabyte, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{GB}}&quot;\n    )\n    band_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;V_{{\\text{{band}}}} = 2 \\times {_m(delta_band)}\\ \\text{{m}} \\times {fmt_magnitude(a_surface, meter**2, precision=0)}\\ \\text{{m}}^2 = {fmt_magnitude(v_band, meter**3, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{m}}^3&quot;\n    )\n    hashed_math = fmt_display_math(\n        rf&quot;N_{{\\text{{blocks}}}} = \\frac{{{fmt_magnitude(v_band, meter**3, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{m}}^3}}{{({block_side} \\times {_m(pitch)}\\ \\text{{m}})^3}} = \\text{{{fmt_int(blocks)}}}, \\qquad \\text{{{fmt_int(blocks)}}} \\times {block_side}^3 \\times {fmt_int(voxel_bytes.magnitude)}\\ \\text{{B}} = {fmt_magnitude(payload, megabyte, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{MB}}&quot;\n    )\n]```\n\nFor the illustrative host-map sizing calculation, consider an operational workspace of physical volume $\\WorkspaceVolume = \\WorkspaceExtentX \\WorkspaceExtentY \\WorkspaceExtentZ$. Discretizing this domain into cubic voxels of uniform pitch $\\VoxelPitch$ requires memory proportional to workspace volume and to the inverse cube of voxel width:\n$$\\DenseVoxelGridMemory = \\left(\\frac{\\WorkspaceVolume}{\\VoxelPitch^3}\\right) \\VoxelStorageSize$$ {#eq-mem-footprint-dense}\nwhere $\\VoxelStorageSize$ represents the storage footprint per voxel, here `[HashedVoxelPayload.voxel_bytes_str]` bytes: 4 for a 32-bit distance, 2 for the observation weight, and 2 for the evidence epoch.\n\nObstacles occupy physical 2D surfaces embedded in 3D space rather than solid volumes, and a TSDF requires stored values only near those surfaces. Truncating the distance field to a band $[-\\TsdfTruncationBand, +\\TsdfTruncationBand]$ around observed surfaces restricts storage to a shell $\\TruncationBandVolume \\approx 2\\TsdfTruncationBand \\cdot \\ObstacleSurfaceArea$, where $\\ObstacleSurfaceArea$ is the total obstacle surface area. Partitioning space into blocks of $\\BlockEdgeVoxelCount^3$ voxels with block pitch $\\VoxelBlockEdgeLength = \\BlockEdgeVoxelCount \\cdot \\VoxelPitch$, and allocating a block only when it holds data, the sparse hashed footprint obeys:\n$$\\HashedGridMemory = \\AllocatedBlockCount \\left( \\BlockEdgeVoxelCount^3 \\VoxelStorageSize + \\BlockMetadataSize \\right) + \\HashBucketCount \\HashEntrySize$$ {#eq-mem-footprint-hashed}\nwhere $\\AllocatedBlockCount \\approx \\lceil 2\\TsdfTruncationBand \\ObstacleSurfaceArea / (\\BlockEdgeVoxelCount \\VoxelPitch)^3 \\rceil$, $\\BlockMetadataSize$ is the block metadata (coordinates, allocation flags), and $\\HashEntrySize$ is the hash-table entry size.\n\nSparse volumetric storage requires an indexing mechanism that resolves 3D coordinates without the sequential pointer traversals of an octree. **Voxel block hashing** discretizes continuous world coordinates $\\CartesianPosition = [\\CartesianXCoordinate, \\CartesianYCoordinate, \\CartesianZCoordinate]^\\top \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ into an integer block coordinate vector $\\VoxelBlockCoordinate = [\\BlockCoordinateX, \\BlockCoordinateY, \\BlockCoordinateZ]^\\top \\in \\mathbb{Z}^3$:\n$$\\VoxelBlockCoordinate(\\CartesianPosition) = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{\\CartesianPosition}{\\BlockEdgeVoxelCount \\cdot \\VoxelPitch} \\right\\rfloor$$ {#eq-mem-block-index}\nA spatial hash function then maps the block index $\\VoxelBlockCoordinate$ from @eq-mem-block-index to a specific hash table bucket index $\\SpatialHashFunction(\\VoxelBlockCoordinate) \\in \\{0, \\dots, \\HashBucketCount-1\\}$. The scheme comes from real-time surface reconstruction [@niessner2013real], built on the spatial hashing of @teschner2003optimized, and underlies the Voxblox and nvblox mapping frameworks [@oleynikova2017voxblox; @millane2024nvblox]. In an architecture packing $8^3 = 512$ voxels per block with an 8-byte payload per voxel, each allocated block forms a contiguous `[HashedVoxelPayload.block_bytes_str]` byte memory buffer. A single hash resolution loads an entire local 3D neighborhood into the processor cache. While average-case access time is $\\mathcal{O}(1)$, worst-case retrieval latency depends on hash bucket collisions, probe sequence lengths, cache misses, and lock contention during concurrent sensor updates. Because hard-real-time safety guarantees require deterministic execution bounds rather than average-case performance, the local permission snapshot avoids pointer chasing and hashing overhead altogether by organizing its bounded stopping corridor as a dense, pre-allocated array in microcontroller SRAM. Notebook \\ref{nbk-memory-empty-air-tax} applies both footprint equations to one warehouse workcell.\n\n::: {#nbk-memory-empty-air-tax .callout-notebook title=&quot;The empty-air tax&quot;}\n\n**Problem**: A host map must cover a warehouse workcell of `[HashedVoxelPayload.l_x_str]` by `[HashedVoxelPayload.l_y_str]` by `[HashedVoxelPayload.l_z_str]` at the resolution manipulation needs, with an evidence epoch in every stored voxel so that each region&#x27;s claim can expire. How much memory does a dense grid take, how much does a hashed band around the obstacle surfaces take, and what fraction of the dense grid describes empty air?\n\n**Variables**:\n\n* Workcell volume $\\WorkspaceVolume =$ `[HashedVoxelPayload.v_ws_str]` (illustrative) at pitch $\\VoxelPitch =$ `[HashedVoxelPayload.pitch_str]` (chosen), with $\\VoxelStorageSize =$ `[HashedVoxelPayload.voxel_bytes_str]` B per voxel (illustrative).\n* Obstacle surface $\\ObstacleSurfaceArea =$ `[HashedVoxelPayload.a_surface_str]` (illustrative) and band half-width $\\TsdfTruncationBand =$ `[HashedVoxelPayload.delta_band_str]` (chosen).\n* Block edge $\\BlockEdgeVoxelCount = 8$ voxels, the nvblox layout (datasheet).\n\n**Math**: The dense grid, by @eq-mem-footprint-dense:\n`[HashedVoxelPayload.dense_math]`\nThe band and its voxel payload, by @eq-mem-footprint-hashed without metadata or hash entries:\n`[HashedVoxelPayload.band_math]`\n`[HashedVoxelPayload.hashed_math]`\n\n**Result**: The dense grid needs `[HashedVoxelPayload.m_dense_str]` for `[HashedVoxelPayload.n_dense_str]` voxels. The band is `[HashedVoxelPayload.band_pct_str]` of the workcell, so `[HashedVoxelPayload.empty_pct_str]` of the dense grid describes space with no surface in it, and the hashed payload of `[HashedVoxelPayload.payload_str]` in `[HashedVoxelPayload.blocks_str]` blocks is about `[HashedVoxelPayload.ratio_str]` times smaller.\n\n**Systems insight**: Obstacles are surfaces, not volumes, so a dense grid allocates the vast majority of its capacity to empty air. In an embedded robot computer, that multi-gigabyte memory footprint directly contends with memory pools required by vision foundation backbones and real-time motion controllers. The hashed calculation represents a theoretical lower bound: block metadata headers, hash table bucket arrays, and boundary fragmentation in partially occupied blocks increase this overhead in practice. This memory reduction applies strictly to the truncated surface representation. It also alters temporal bookkeeping: because the sparse map allocates memory only near detected surfaces, unallocated open space carries no explicit evidence timestamp. Unallocated space cannot vouch for its own clearance until the system explicitly logs a free-space ray-clearing observation.\n\n:::\n\n: **Truncated Signed Distance Field**: @curless1996volumetric averaged many range scans into a signed distance stored only near the surface to build detailed models of real objects, and KinectFusion ran the same fusion in real time on a GPU from a handheld depth camera [@newcombe2011kinectfusion]. Truncation bought both systems their memory savings. It makes the TSDF a representation for reconstructing surfaces, not for certifying the empty space between them. \n\nThis narrow-band scaling applies strictly to a TSDF, not to the full Euclidean distance field required by the permission snapshot. An ESDF stores metric clearance throughout the entire navigable volume where queries might occur. Its memory footprint therefore scales with the observed volume $\\ObservedVolume/\\VoxelPitch^3$ rather than surface area $\\ObstacleSurfaceArea$. For a mobile warehouse manipulator, this observed volume corresponds to the spatial corridor reachable by the chassis and arm during stopping maneuvers, which dictates the snapshot dimensions evaluated for the local distance grid. Memory allocation semantics also dictate safety interpretation. In systems that perform volumetric free-space carving along sensor rays, unallocated blocks reliably represent unobserved, unknown space. In systems that allocate only narrow surface bands, however, an unallocated block could represent either confirmed open space or unobserved space hidden behind an occlusion. A safety-critical spatial memory system cannot conflate allocation status with occupancy state. Alongside observed occupied space, it must explicitly represent the three states that allocation cannot tell apart: observed free space backed by a valid evidence timestamp, unobserved space, and expired space where past observations have exceeded their maximum validity horizon. Every cell therefore carries one of four states, occupied, free, unknown, or expired.\n\n#### Ray-marching update traffic and negative evidence {.unnumbered}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ NAIVE RAY-MARCHING UPDATE TRAFFIC</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-memory-volumetric-metric-maps (@eq-mem-dram-bandwidth).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Price one depth stream's naive map-update traffic and compare it</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          with the host SoC's registered peak DRAM bandwidth.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    rays per frame, steps per ray, traffic, share of peak.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     BW = f_cam x N_rays x (d_ray / r) x S_rw.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_qty_int from mlsysim.core.units import byte, centimeter, dimensionless, gigabyte, hertz, meter, second</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator</p>\n<p>class RaymarchBandwidth:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>RAYS = Scenarios.WarehouseDepthRaymarchTraffic  # category: illustrative (depth stream and map; MLSysIM scenario) width_px = RAYS.width_px  # category: illustrative (VGA depth stream) height_px = RAYS.height_px  # category: illustrative (VGA depth stream) f_cam = RAYS.frame_rate  # category: illustrative (depth frame rate) d_ray = RAYS.ray_length  # category: illustrative (mean marched distance) pitch = RAYS.pitch  # category: chosen (voxel pitch) s_rw = RAYS.bytes_per_step  # category: illustrative (read-modify-write traffic per voxel, low end) soc_bw = RM.brain_soc.memory.bandwidth  # category: datasheet (host SoC peak DRAM bandwidth)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>R = RAYS.result n_rays = R.rays n_steps = R.steps bw = R.traffic share = R.share</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(bw.magnitude - 7.3728) &lt; 1e-6, f\"Ray-march traffic should be 7.37 GB/s, got {bw}.\") check(share &lt; 0.05, \"One naive depth stream should take under 5 percent of peak.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">margin_volumetric_raycasting_bandwidth_ladder.svg draws 1 depth sample, 100 voxel steps, x 307,200 rays x 30 Hz x 8 B, and 7.37 GB/s</h1>\n<p>check(abs(n_steps - 100) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Margin figure step label (100 voxel steps) is stale, got {n_steps}.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>n_rays_str = fmt_int(n_rays) f_cam_str = fmt_qty_int(f_cam, hertz) d_ray_str = fmt_length(d_ray, unit=meter, precision=0) pitch_str = fmt_length(pitch, unit=centimeter, precision=0) n_steps_str = fmt_int(n_steps) bw_str = fmt_bandwidth(bw, unit=gigabyte / second, precision=2) soc_bw_str = fmt_bandwidth(soc_bw, unit=gigabyte / second, precision=0) share_pct_str = fmt_percent(share, precision=1, style=\"prose\") ]```</p>\n<p>Alongside storage capacity, updating those cell states as depth cameras stream range frames at framerate $\\DepthFrameRate$ with $\\DepthRayCount = \\ImageWidth \\times \\ImageHeight$ pixels per frame requires ray marching from the sensor origin to each observed surface point [@curless1996volumetric]. Each ray traverses a line-of-sight distance $\\RayTraversalLength$ at voxel stepping pitch $\\VoxelPitch$, requiring $\\RayStepCount = \\RayTraversalLength / \\VoxelPitch$ discrete cell evaluations. The raw DRAM update bandwidth consumed by naive ray marching is: $$\\text{BW}_{\\text{DRAM}} = \\DepthFrameRate \\cdot \\DepthRayCount \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\RayTraversalLength}{\\VoxelPitch}\\right) \\cdot \\VoxelUpdateTraffic$$ {#eq-mem-dram-bandwidth} where $\\VoxelUpdateTraffic$ is the read-modify-write memory traffic per traversed voxel (8 to 16 bytes for reading the current occupancy or distance, updating running weights, and writing back).</p>\n<p>For an illustrative VGA depth stream of <code>[RaymarchBandwidth.n_rays_str]</code> rays at <code>[RaymarchBandwidth.f_cam_str]</code>, marched over <code>[RaymarchBandwidth.d_ray_str]</code> at a <code>[RaymarchBandwidth.pitch_str]</code> pitch (<code>[RaymarchBandwidth.n_steps_str]</code> steps per ray) with 8 bytes per step, @eq-mem-dram-bandwidth gives <code>[RaymarchBandwidth.bw_str]</code>, <code>[RaymarchBandwidth.share_pct_str]</code> of the peak <code>[RaymarchBandwidth.soc_bw_str]</code> of the host system-on-chip (SoC). This share of peak understates the actual performance impact, because these volumetric map updates share the memory interconnect with vision foundation models and motion controllers, and their throughput penalty stems from non-sequential access locality and memory bus contention rather than aggregate bandwidth alone. This update bandwidth represents a substantial baseline load that system architects must incorporate into hardware contention profiling, as memory bus saturation and cache thrashing degrade perception pipelines long before theoretical DRAM bandwidth is exhausted. Block hashing reduces this traffic by keeping active blocks in on-chip memory, and projective TSDF updates project voxels inside the viewing frustum onto the depth image instead of marching every ray. These methods reduce repeated memory traffic, but the reduction depends on scene coverage and update strategy. Sparse allocation still grows as the machine explores new regions; a bounded host store needs an explicit retention policy.</p>\n<p>What a map can certify comes from how a range return updates it. Traversing the ray from the sensor to the return [@amanatides1987fast], the map records <strong>negative evidence</strong> along the traversed ray and positive evidence at its return. Treating an entire crossed voxel as free requires qualified beam coverage, resolution, and geometric error allowances. The ray alone does not observe every point in that voxel. A valid empty ray can retire a phantom obstacle, but it says nothing about the volume behind a return, the hatched cells in @fig-09-ray-update-epochs that keep their earlier epoch. A new scan down the aisle therefore cannot renew cells hidden behind the rack. Sparse allocation saves capacity without eliminating ray traversal, and any resulting processing delay adds to evidence age.</p>\n<p>↪ The log-odds occupancy update and signed-distance fusion are developed in @sec-appendix-ml-voxels.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Voxel Traversal</strong>: @amanatides1987fast step a ray from one voxel boundary to the next by comparing the distances to the next crossing on each axis and adding one fixed increment, so each voxel visited costs a few comparisons and one addition. The work per ray is therefore proportional to the number of voxels it crosses, range divided by pitch, which is the $\\RayTraversalLength/\\VoxelPitch$ factor of @eq-mem-dram-bandwidth.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Log-Odds</strong>: Storing $\\ell = \\ln\\big(\\CellOccupancyProbability/(1-\\CellOccupancyProbability)\\big)$ turns each Bayesian occupancy update into an addition, with an uninformed prior at $\\ell = 0$. A cell no ray has touched and a cell that received as many hits as misses both read $\\ell = 0$, so the value alone cannot separate never observed from observed and ambiguous. The manifest of @tbl-09-schema-volumetric-belief therefore carries an explicit <code>cell_state</code>.</p>\n<p>Because a return renews only the cells it touched, a map's global publication timestamp cannot certify the freshness of individual voxels. The manifest in @tbl-09-schema-volumetric-belief therefore carries cell evidence epochs alongside occupancy. For the worked local snapshot, construction and independent validation establish support for every admitted distance claim through the fixed support deadline. The local hull query checks that certificate's header and validity conditions within its allocated time, rather than scanning the supporting cells each tick. A freshly copied distance value without valid support cannot establish clearance. The manifest travels with the bounded snapshot as well as the host map; the isolated controller does not traverse the full host store.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Map field</th><th>Unit or type</th><th>Meaning and consumer check</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>voxel_grid_origin</code>, <code>voxel_resolution_m</code></td><td>m; m</td><td>Spatial anchor and pitch; match the active frame and calibration</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>pose_graph_revision</code></td><td>integer</td><td>Revision of the pose graph that placed the cells; a loop closure that changes it re-anchors or expires the snapshot</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>map_update_epoch</code></td><td>capture-clock ns</td><td>Last map publication/update; <strong>not</strong> freshness of every cell</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>cell_evidence_epoch</code></td><td>capture-clock ns per cell</td><td>Last physical observation for that cell; never refreshed by prediction or unrelated map updates</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>free_space_sweep_epoch</code></td><td>capture-clock ns per occupancy cell</td><td>Last valid observed free ray through that cell</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>cell_state</code></td><td>occupied, free, unknown, or expired per cell</td><td>Set explicitly, never inferred from allocation; unknown and expired read as obstacles</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>clock_conversion</code>, <code>sync_error_ns</code></td><td>mapping; ns</td><td>Convert capture epochs to the consumer's monotonic clock with bounded error</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>support_deadline</code>, <code>validation_scope</code></td><td>consumer-clock deadline; fixed certificate</td><td>Validated conservative interval, coverage, frame revision, and growth assumptions; refuse on expiry or scope violation</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>query_latency_budget_us</code></td><td>$\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$ (at most <code>[RepresentationQuery.t_query_str]</code>)</td><td>Bound collision and distance gradient query time to preserve the <code>[RepresentationQuery.f_tick_str]</code> permission loop</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Volumetric belief manifest and cell evidence</strong>: The map header describes layout, pose-graph revision, query latency budget, and update time, while cell-local acquisition epochs and states support construction of a conservative local snapshot. Its fixed header carries the independently validated support deadline and scope checked by the local consumer. The consumer treats unobserved or expired cells as unknown unless a separately validated policy permits traversal. {#tbl-09-schema-volumetric-belief tbl-colwidths=\"[26,22,52]\"}</p>\n<p>Synthesizing these elements yields the primary metric the reactive permission path requires, the <strong>free-space depth</strong> $\\FreeSpaceDepth$. To construct this bound, the qualified snapshot path generates and independently validates an ESDF wherein occupied, unobserved, expired, and insufficiently supported regions are obstacles throughout the declared support interval. Evaluating this field at the center of a spherical body hull and subtracting the hull radius yields $\\FreeSpaceDepth$: the Euclidean distance from the hull surface to the nearest physical coordinate that lacks certified, fresh evidence of being empty. Under the validated coverage and entry-envelope assumptions, any dynamic hazard (a pedestrian stepping around a corner, a falling carton) remains within the regions treated as unobserved or uncertified, so $\\FreeSpaceDepth$ conservatively bounds the spatial margin available for vehicle motion within that interval. When evaluating a candidate trajectory segment down an aisle, the collision filter subtracts the composite geometric error (encompassing sensor calibration noise, localization covariance, and spatial voxel quantization) as well as the protective safety clearance $\\ProtectiveClearanceMargin$ from $\\FreeSpaceDepth$. The test is applied not at the hull's present pose alone but along every pose the admitted motion and its stopping trajectory will sweep, so the stopping distance of the stopping budget enters through the length of that swept path. The motion is permitted if and only if the remaining margin is strictly positive at every pose along it. If the robot approaches an occluded cross aisle, $\\FreeSpaceDepth$ contracts rapidly regardless of how quickly the microcontroller evaluates the memory lookup; computational throughput cannot compensate for physical occlusion. How rapidly this usable clearance margin decays under persistent occlusion is analyzed in @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h4\">Forgetting, decay, and change</h4>\n<p>On the host map that feeds those local snapshots, a dynamic physical environment requires spatial memory to evict stale data systematically. In a logistics facility, pallets move, access doors swing open, and workers cross aisles that range finders observed to be vacant moments earlier. An embodied system that preserves observations indefinitely accumulates both phantom obstacles (retaining records of objects that have moved away) and phantom free space (assuming unobserved regions remain empty after hazards enter). Active negative evidence clears phantom obstacles whenever fresh sensor rays traverse previously occupied cells. However, detecting hazards that enter unobserved voids presents a different challenge, because no sensor returns exist to contradict the stale assumption of clearance.</p>\n<p>Practical mapping systems handle unobserved space through varying assumptions (@tbl-09-map-maintenance). OctoMap clamps cell log-odds between fixed saturation limits, ensuring that a voxel reinforced by hundreds of historical returns can still be cleared rapidly by a small batch of contradicting observations [@hornung2013octomap]. Clamping accelerates adaptation upon re-observation, but it cannot alter belief in unvisited cells. Independent Markov chain occupancy grids assign each cell an explicit two-state transition model with online-learned transition rates, allowing an unobserved voxel's occupancy belief to relax gradually toward its historical steady-state prior [@saarinen2012independent]. Modern GPU-accelerated mapping pipelines adapt these concepts to real-time volumetric streams. For example, the nvblox library periodically decays the occupancy probability of out-of-view voxels toward 0.5 (the uninformative state), discarding the assumption that the unobserved world is strictly static [@millane2024nvblox; @nvidia2026nvbloxdocs]. Dynablox identifies transient dynamic objects by maintaining an incremental model of high-confidence free space and flagging unexpected point returns that penetrate that volume [@schmid2023dynablox]. Similarly, Khronos separates spatial dynamics across temporal scales, employing a fast tracking loop for short-term movements inside an active window alongside an asynchronous factor-graph optimizer for persistent architectural shifts [@schmid2024khronos].</p>\n<p>These approaches rely on statistical rate models that parameterize the expected rate of environmental change from past traffic patterns or heuristic tuning. While average-case models are well suited for high-level motion planning and route optimization, the permission path requires hard worst-case bounds. The deterministic expiration mechanism formulated in this chapter establishes that worst-case counterpart. An observed free cell reverts to the unknown state no later than the minimum time required for an unobserved object, moving at the facility's maximum physical velocity bound, to traverse the distance from the nearest occlusion boundary into that cell. A heuristic decay model satisfies safety requirements only if its decay schedule transitions cells to unknown at or before this physical traversal deadline. Otherwise, statistical decay remains an optimization heuristic for path planners rather than a certifiable safety assertion for motion gating.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What happens to a cell nobody observes</th><th>Representative system (as of 2026)</th><th>What it cannot certify</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Clamped log-odds</td><td>Nothing; clamping only speeds change when the cell is seen again</td><td>OctoMap</td><td>Any change in cells no ray reaches</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Per-cell transition model</td><td>Occupancy relaxes toward a learned long-run value</td><td>Independent Markov chain grids</td><td>A worst case; learned rates describe past traffic</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Decay toward unknown</td><td>Occupancy relaxes toward 0.5 at a fixed rate once out of view</td><td>nvblox</td><td>Safety, unless the rate is set from a speed bound</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dynamic-object detection</td><td>Unchanged; returns inside confident free space are flagged as moving</td><td>Dynablox</td><td>Objects the sensors do not currently see</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spatio-temporal SLAM</td><td>Unchanged until revisited; change is detected on reobservation</td><td>Khronos</td><td>The interval between visits</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deterministic expiry (this chapter)</td><td>Free becomes unknown at the erosion deadline set by the speed bound</td><td>Worked design</td><td>Nothing past the deadline, by construction</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Map maintenance mechanisms and what they certify, as of 2026</strong>: Rate models describe expected change and serve planning; only an expiry derived from a speed bound certifies that an unobserved cell is still free. The erosion deadline in the last row is derived in @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion. The named systems are point-in-time implementations of each mechanism. {#tbl-09-map-maintenance tbl-colwidths=\"[20,32,18,30]\"}</p>\n<p>Together, these volumetric grid and distance-field rules bound where the machine may move, but they stop at geometry. While a volumetric distance field certifies where free space ends, it treats every occupied cell as an anonymous obstacle and cannot tell the task planner which surface is the tote handle or the cage door.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h3\">3D dynamic scene graphs</h3>\n<p>Supplying that identity is the job of semantic scene memory, which sits above the raw metric layer and inherits its physical constraints. Learned neural models infer what an object is, whereas the underlying metric mapping pipeline anchors where that object sits in physical space. Open-vocabulary scene memory bridges these domains using <strong>3D vision foundation models</strong>. A self-supervised visual backbone such as DINOv2 [@oquab2023dinov2] extracts dense patch feature representations. A promptable segmentation model such as the Segment Anything Model (SAM) [@kirillov2023segment] generates class-agnostic instance masks. Systems such as ConceptGraphs [@kuwajerwala2024conceptgraphs; @jatavallabhula2023conceptfusion] and Hydra [@hughes2022hydra] lift those 2D masks and semantic features through calibrated depth, extrinsics, and visual odometry into an <strong>object-centric 3D scene graph</strong>. In this structured representation, each node represents an entity parameterized by a 3D centroid, a spatial covariance matrix, and a text-aligned multimodal embedding vector.</p>\n<p>Drawn for the warehouse (@fig-09-dynamic-scene-graph), such a graph stacks five layers: a metric mesh, the objects and agents segmented from it (mugs, totes, the cage door, a walking person), topological places linked through free space, rooms such as aisles and dock bays, and the building itself. Edges between layers run both ways, compressing metric surfaces into symbols on the way up and grounding task tokens in metric coordinates on the way down.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ FOUNDATION-MODEL ENCODER AT CAMERA RATE\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: The construction-cost paragraph of\n# │          @sec-memory-hierarchical-scene-graphs.\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Show that a heavy encoder at the navigation camera&#x27;s frame rate\n# │          exceeds the application processor&#x27;s peak dense FP16 rate.\n# │ Show:    3 TFLOP per frame at 60 Hz = 180 TFLOP/s against 43 TFLOP/s.\n# │ How:     Assumed encoder cost; RM.nav_camera.frame_rate; the Jetson AGX\n# │          Orin dense FP16 peak from the registry (RM.brain_soc); illustrative\n# │          semantic keyframe rates for the multi-rate alternative.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_flops, fmt_frequency, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\n\nclass EncoderAtCameraRate:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Sustained throughput a per-frame foundation encoder would need.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    ENCODER = Scenarios.WarehouseFoundationEncoderAtCameraRate  # category: illustrative (MLSysIM scenario)\n    encoder_tflop = ENCODER.flop_per_frame.to(TFLOP).magnitude  # category: illustrative (assumed encoder cost per frame, TFLOP)\n    f_frame = RM.nav_camera.frame_rate  # category: datasheet\n    orin_fp16_dense = RM.brain_soc.compute.precision_flops[&quot;fp16&quot;]  # category: datasheet (NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin technical brief, dense FP16 peak)\n    f_semantic_lo = ENCODER.keyframe_rate_low  # category: illustrative (semantic keyframe rate, low end)\n    f_semantic_hi = ENCODER.keyframe_rate_high  # category: illustrative (semantic keyframe rate, high end)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    orin_fp16_dense_tflops = orin_fp16_dense.to(TFLOPs / second).magnitude\n    required_tflops = ENCODER.result.required.to(TFLOPs / second).magnitude\n    shortfall = ENCODER.result.shortfall\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(required_tflops - 180.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Unexpected required throughput: {required_tflops}&quot;)\n    check(shortfall &gt; 1.0, &quot;Prose says the encoder cannot run at camera rate even at peak&quot;)\n    check(abs(orin_fp16_dense_tflops - 43.0) &lt; 1e-9, f&quot;Orin dense FP16 peak drifted: {orin_fp16_dense_tflops}&quot;)\n    check(f_semantic_hi &lt; f_frame, &quot;Semantic keyframes must update slower than the camera.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    encoder_tflop_str = fmt_flops(encoder_tflop * TFLOP, unit=TFLOP, precision=0)\n    f_frame_str = fmt_frequency(f_frame, unit=hertz, precision=0)\n    required_tflops_str = fmt(required_tflops, precision=0)\n    orin_fp16_dense_tflops_str = fmt(orin_fp16_dense_tflops, precision=0)\n    f_semantic_lo_num_str = fmt(f_semantic_lo.to(hertz).magnitude, precision=0)\n    f_semantic_hi_str = fmt_frequency(f_semantic_hi, unit=hertz, precision=0)\n]```\n\nConstructing and maintaining this semantic hierarchy consumes substantial compute and latency budgets. An illustrative vision foundation encoder requiring `[EncoderAtCameraRate.encoder_tflop_str]` per frame at the navigation camera&#x27;s full `[EncoderAtCameraRate.f_frame_str]` capture rate demands `[EncoderAtCameraRate.required_tflops_str]` TFLOP/s of sustained tensor throughput, even before accounting for image preprocessing, memory bandwidth, or post-processing clustering. By comparison, an edge application processor such as the NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin achieves a manufacturer-specified peak of `[EncoderAtCameraRate.orin_fp16_dense_tflops_str]` dense FP16 TFLOP/s (as documented in its [technical brief](https://www.nvidia.com/content/dam/en-zz/Solutions/gtcf21/jetson-orin/nvidia-jetson-agx-orin-technical-brief.pdf)). This is a theoretical arithmetic ceiling that sustained neural workloads rarely approach, because memory bandwidth caps any kernel whose arithmetic per byte falls short of the processor&#x27;s ratio of peak compute to peak bandwidth [@williams2009roofline].\n\nBecause the illustrative foundation backbone exceeds this processor’s peak throughput at full camera rate, the worked architecture implements a **multi-rate hierarchy**. In this decoupled design, lightweight metric processing pipelines running on the application processor consume raw frames delivered via direct memory access (DMA) to extract geometric obstacle points, update occupancy voxels, and track camera poses at the camera&#x27;s full `[EncoderAtCameraRate.f_frame_str]` rate. Meanwhile, compute-intensive vision foundation backbones run asynchronously on selected keyframes at a reduced rate of `[EncoderAtCameraRate.f_semantic_lo_num_str]` to `[EncoderAtCameraRate.f_semantic_hi_str]`, associating semantic embeddings and updating topological nodes. This multi-rate separation remains safe only because the high-frequency metric loop and the independent permission filter maintain certified physical stopping envelopes during the intervals between semantic updates.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Each rate tier meets its own deadline across a nonblocking boundary, the decoupling established in @sec-nervous-cadences.\n:::\n\nThe vertical edges spanning the hierarchy in @fig-09-dynamic-scene-graph enable dual-path reasoning. An agentic planner can query spatial relationships among objects and topological rooms without traversing millions of raw mesh vertices, while downstream manipulation requests (such as grasping a bin handle) resolve downward into precise Cartesian frames and localized geometry. From a systems memory perspective, each node encapsulates state elements that age at radically different physical rates:\n\n1. *Geometric pose*: The spatial coordinates of an entity age according to the physical velocity bound of its category. A permanently anchored storage rack does not drift, whereas an unobserved cart or tote may displace at human walking speeds.\n2. *Existence confidence*: The probability that an object remains present decays independently of its pose estimate. An unobserved tote may be removed entirely by a human worker; a narrow spatial covariance on an unobserved object provides false confidence if the entity itself is no longer present (@sec-memory-fallacies-pitfalls).\n3. *Semantic classification*: Node category labels originate from learned visual embeddings. While semantic labels do not suffer physical drift over time, their validity is bounded by model calibration and out-of-distribution uncertainty, remaining uncorrected until the object is re-observed.\n\nContemporary frameworks update graph structures primarily upon re-observation: Hydra optimizes scene graph node poses when pose-graph loop closures update the underlying trajectory [@hughes2022hydra], and Khronos registers object additions or removals when the robot revisits previously mapped areas [@schmid2024khronos]. Neither system explicitly tracks the temporal decay of unobserved entities using physical kinematic bounds. Tracking and enforcing these physical validity limits remains the responsibility of the belief record, and high-level symbolic abstractions can never refresh the evidence timestamps of the underlying metric cells. Beyond knowing where objects sit in the scene graph, a manipulation policy also needs to remember which physical actions it has already completed upon them.\n\n### Episodic memory and interaction history {#sec-memory-episodic}\n\nWhen a transformer policy&#x27;s sliding context window evicts older tokens as new camera frames stream in, the model can forget that it already unlatched the cage door or experienced a torque stall a hundred cycles ago. Metric representations and 3D scene graphs capture *where* surfaces, free space, and objects exist in the physical environment; **episodic memory** records *what happened*: the sequence of discrete contact transitions, grasping events, torque stalls, and human interventions that define a task execution history. By indexing physical events by their monotonic acquisition timestamps, episodic memory enables task planners to verify preconditions, for example confirming whether a mechanical latch was successfully unlatched or a transport tote was deposited into a fixture.\n\nLearned policies frequently use attention mechanisms over past interaction tokens to infer task progress. However, maintaining unconstrained sequence histories is computationally intractable. A **streaming window** bounds transformer attention buffers by evicting historical tokens based on recency or attention sink criteria [@xiao2024streamingllm]. Such eviction policies operate independently of physical causality; an observation confirming that a container latch was secured a hundred navigation cycles ago is precisely the type of token dropped by a sliding context window. The durable metric record and structured episodic state anchors must therefore persist outside the transformer&#x27;s transient attention buffer whenever their physical consequences continue to constrain manipulation. Retrieval augmentation supplies the policy with task context and relevant geometry, while the underlying belief record carries the evidence epochs and error bounds that the permission path independently checks before admitting execution.\n\nFor reactive motion permission, any spatial assertion lacking an explicit evidence timestamp is treated as unverified, and any state estimate whose spatial uncertainty bound exceeds certified safety tolerances must be rejected before trajectory commands reach the hardware controller. Nevertheless, an embodied system cannot trigger emergency braking on every dropped sensor packet. Sustaining safe, fluid motion across transient sensor dropouts requires state estimators that propagate spatial belief forward in time under a declared error-growth law, with a deadline that governs execution until fresh observations either corroborate or correct the state.\n\n## Forward State Propagation and Occlusion Decay {#sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion}\n\nWhen a sensor loses sight of a moving body, bringing the machine to a safe stop takes time and demands a usable state estimate throughout the deceleration interval. Forward propagation bridges this blind duration by projecting the most recent observation forward in time, whether the machine continues an authorized task or executes a protective fallback. A learned generative model can predict future trajectories autoregressively, and ensemble dispersion or Monte Carlo rollouts can quantify prediction variance. However, statistical spread alone does not constitute a certified upper bound on physical tracking error. In physical AI systems, forward state propagation must be anchored to explicit error-growth dynamics, whether implemented via the open-loop Kalman prediction step of @eq-percep-kf-predict, learned stochastic state observers, or expanding covariance ellipsoids.\n\nForward propagation updates the state estimate and expands its uncertainty envelope while preserving the original hardware capture timestamp. Across the unobserved interval, open-loop state estimation remains valid only so long as the growing error envelope remains within the task&#x27;s geometric clearance. The rack end in the warehouse aisle shows where that boundary falls.\n\n### Bounded error growth and free-space erosion {#sec-memory-free-space-erosion}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ RACK-END FREE SPACE THROUGH OCCLUSION (EROSION LAW)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion (free-space erosion, the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          walking-person term, worked roots, the expiry table, the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          skewed-record pitfall), @sec-memory-summary.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Show that memory certifies the aisle only to a depth set by the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          distance from the occlusion boundary: nothing beside the rack</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          end, a useful interval in the open aisle ahead.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    the erosion deadline abreast of the rack end (side clearance) and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          for the aisle ahead (clear distance); what the frame's age leaves</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          of the second; the running total memory leaves unchanged; the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          walking-person term at the drive limit against the clear distance.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndFreeSpace (calc_free_space_expiry:</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          max(0, (d_free - dloc - dmargin) / v_h)); the running total from</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          Scenarios.WarehouseBaseStopFullDelay at the drive limit; the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          walking term v_h (tau_delay + v / a_brake) for a constant-</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          deceleration stop at the drive limit (the C2 suffix is Ch11's, and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the speed ceiling this term implies is computed in Ch12).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import dimensionless, meter, millimeter, second</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class RackEndBelief:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>delta_loc = AISLE.delta_loc  # category: illustrative (localization bound) delta_margin = AISLE.delta_margin  # category: chosen (protective clearance) v_h = AISLE.v_human_approach  # category: datasheet (ISO 13855 walking speed) d_clear = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative (rack-end clear distance; free depth ahead) side_clearance = AISLE.side_clearance  # category: illustrative (hull-to-rack gap; free depth abreast of the rack end) frame_rate = RM.nav_camera.frame_rate  # category: datasheet (navigation camera) t_age = AISLE.perception_age  # category: derived (@sec-perception-latency-waterfall) v_drive = RM.base.max_velocity  # category: illustrative (drive limit) a_brake = AISLE.a_brake  # category: illustrative (credible deceleration) tau_delay = AISLE.tau_delay  # category: derived (pre-brake delay, excl. dispatch-to-admission)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>R = Scenarios.WarehouseRackEndFreeSpace.result t_exp_abreast = R.horizon_abreast t_exp_ahead = R.horizon_ahead t_left_at_dispatch = R.time_left_at_dispatch frame_period = (1 / frame_rate).to(millisecond) frames_bridged = R.frames_bridged d_age_eroded = R.depth_eroded_by_age slack_abreast = R.certified_depth_abreast</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">rows 1-5 of the stopping budget at the drive limit (Ch2, Ch4, Ch8 terms)</h1>\n<p>running_total = Scenarios.WarehouseBaseStopFullDelay.result.required_distance.to(millimeter)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">walking person: a person approaching at v_h for the whole stop adds</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">v_h (tau_delay + T_stop); at the drive limit with a constant-deceleration</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">stop, T_stop = v / a_brake.</h1>\n<p>d_walk_term = R.walker_intrusion ms_per_mm = R.time_per_depth</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(slack_abreast.magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Abreast of the rack end the free depth should be exactly spent, got {slack_abreast}.\") check(t_exp_abreast.magnitude == 0.0, f\"Memory must certify nothing abreast of the rack end, got {t_exp_abreast}.\") check(abs(t_exp_ahead.magnitude - 593.75) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Aisle-ahead expiry should be 593.75 ms, got {t_exp_ahead}.\") check(t_left_at_dispatch &gt; 10 * frame_period, \"The aisle-ahead claim must outlast many camera frames after dispatch.\") check(abs(t_age.to(millisecond).magnitude - 51.6) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Observation age should be 51.6 ms, got {t_age}.\") check(abs(running_total.to(millimeter).magnitude - 912.9) &lt; 5e-3, f\"Running total after Ch8 should be 912.9 mm, got {running_total}.\") check(abs(d_walk_term.to(meter).magnitude - 1.41) &lt; 5e-3, f\"Walking term at the drive limit should be 1.41 m, got {d_walk_term}.\") check(d_walk_term &gt; d_clear, \"The walking term alone must exceed the rack-end clear distance.\") check(abs(ms_per_mm.magnitude - 0.625) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Each millimeter of free depth should buy 0.625 ms, got {ms_per_mm}.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>delta_loc_str = fmt_length(delta_loc, unit=millimeter, precision=0) delta_margin_str = fmt_length(delta_margin, unit=millimeter, precision=0) v_h_str = fmt_velocity(v_h, precision=1) d_clear_str = fmt_length(d_clear, unit=meter, precision=2) side_clearance_str = fmt_length(side_clearance, unit=millimeter, precision=0) t_exp_abreast_str = fmt_latency(t_exp_abreast, unit=millisecond, precision=0) t_exp_ahead_str = fmt_latency(t_exp_ahead, unit=millisecond, precision=2) t_left_str = fmt_latency(t_left_at_dispatch, unit=millisecond, precision=2) frames_bridged_str = fmt_int(frames_bridged) d_age_eroded_str = fmt_length(d_age_eroded, unit=millimeter, precision=1) frame_period_str = fmt_latency(frame_period, unit=millisecond, precision=1) t_age_str = fmt_latency(t_age, unit=millisecond, precision=1) running_total_str = fmt_length(running_total, unit=millimeter, precision=1) v_drive_str = fmt_velocity(v_drive, precision=1) d_walk_term_str = fmt_length(d_walk_term, unit=meter, precision=2)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">visible arithmetic for notebook nbk-memory-erosion-deadline</h1>\n<p>_mm = lambda q: fmt_magnitude(q, meter, precision=2) _vh = fmt_magnitude(v_h, meter / second, precision=1) ms_per_mm_str = fmt(ms_per_mm.magnitude, precision=3) erosion_abreast_math = fmt_display_math( rf\"T_{{\\text{{inv}}}} = \\frac{{({_mm(side_clearance)} - {_mm(delta_loc)} - {_mm(delta_margin)})\\ \\text{{m}}}}{{{_vh}\\ \\text{{m/s}}}} = {fmt_magnitude(t_exp_abreast, millisecond, precision=0)}\\ \\text{{ms}}\" ) erosion_ahead_math = fmt_display_math( rf\"T_{{\\text{{inv}}}} = \\frac{{({_mm(d_clear)} - {_mm(delta_loc)} - {_mm(delta_margin)})\\ \\text{{m}}}}{{{_vh}\\ \\text{{m/s}}}} = {fmt_magnitude(t_exp_ahead, millisecond, precision=2)}\\ \\text{{ms}}\" ) erosion_left_math = fmt_display_math( rf\"{fmt_magnitude(t_exp_ahead, millisecond, precision=2)} - {fmt_magnitude(t_age, millisecond, precision=1)} = {fmt_magnitude(t_left_at_dispatch, millisecond, precision=2)}\\ \\text{{ms}} \\approx {frames_bridged_str} \\times {fmt_magnitude(frame_period, millisecond, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{ms}}\" ) ]```</p>\n<p>At an aisle intersection, the gap between propagated prediction and live observation decides what the robot may safely execute. Where a storage rack ends, a worker may step abruptly into the clear distance $\\ClearDistance =$ <code>[RackEndBelief.d_clear_str]</code> defended by the machine's stopping budget. Once the rack structure occludes the cross aisle, the central question becomes how long remembered free space can certify that the vehicle's path remains clear.</p>\n<p>Every mathematical dynamics model simplifies physical reality, causing the discrepancy between the projected state and the physical world to compound continuously during sensor dropouts. This uncorrected drift is the open-loop prediction step of @eq-percep-kf-predict running without measurement updates, the same widening seen over one sample step in @fig-08-kalman-predict-update, now compounded across the entire occlusion. In state estimation, this dispersion is called <strong>occlusion covariance expansion</strong>, the continuous widening of uncertainty while the sensors have no line of sight. Formalized by continuous Lyapunov or Riccati covariance propagation equations (@sec-appendix-control-covariance), this propagation describes modeled dispersion before sensors regain visibility; hard divergence bounds require separately qualified disturbance limits.</p>\n<p>In a deterministic bounded-disturbance framework, empirical sensor characterization and mechanical disturbance limits establish an initial position error bound $\\InitialTrackingError$ at the instant of occlusion ($\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant = 0$). Two bounded physical terms subsequently degrade the state estimate. First, unmodeled kinematics introduce a drift velocity bounded by $\\DriftVelocityBound$. Second, unmodeled forces and structural dynamics introduce a disturbance acceleration bounded by $\\DisturbanceAccelerationBound$. Over an occlusion interval $\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$, the worst-case position error bound $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)$ expands quadratically:</p>\n<p>$$\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant) = \\InitialTrackingError + \\DriftVelocityBound\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant + \\frac{1}{2}\\DisturbanceAccelerationBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)^2$$ {#eq-mem-occlusion-expansion}</p>\n<p>At the rack end, the stored belief is not the tracked position of an external object, but rather a spatial occupancy assertion: no person stands within the protective clearance margin $\\ProtectiveClearanceMargin$ (<code>[RackEndBelief.delta_margin_str]</code>) of the robot. The spatial map vouches for this claim out to the free-space depth $\\FreeSpaceDepth$ (@sec-memory-volumetric-metric-maps), defined as the distance from the vehicle hull to the nearest cell lacking active sensory evidence. Any obstacle hidden from the sensors lies across that occlusion boundary.</p>\n<p>Two physical uncertainties consume this free-space buffer. First, onboard localization uncertainty introduces a bounded error $\\LocalizationBound$ (<code>[RackEndBelief.delta_loc_str]</code>), meaning the true physical clearance may already be smaller than the nominal map indicates. Second, an unobserved person can advance outward from behind the rack corner at an approach velocity bounded by $\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound =$ <code>[RackEndBelief.v_h_str]</code>, the standardized human walking speed mandated in safety standards [@iso13855]. Matching these physical parameters to the quadratic expansion in @eq-mem-occlusion-expansion yields an error growth $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant) = \\LocalizationBound + \\HumanWalkingSpeedBound\\,\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$ with zero acceleration disturbance ($\\DisturbanceAccelerationBound = 0$). The allowable task tolerance is the uncommitted clearance $\\FreeSpaceDepth - \\ProtectiveClearanceMargin$. As a result, the certified free region erodes inward from the unobserved frontier at walking speed, causing the spatial claim to lapse at:</p>\n<p>$$\\BeliefInvalidationHorizon = \\frac{\\FreeSpaceDepth - \\LocalizationBound - \\ProtectiveClearanceMargin}{\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound}$$ {#eq-mem-free-space-expiry}</p>\n<p>: <strong>Walking Approach Speed</strong>: ISO 13855 positions a safeguard at $\\SafeguardSeparationDistance = \\HumanWalkingSpeedBound\\,\\SafeguardStoppingTime + \\IntrusionDistanceAllowance$ from a hazard, where $\\SafeguardStoppingTime$ is the overall stopping time, $\\IntrusionDistanceAllowance$ is an intrusion allowance, and $\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound$ is the approach speed (<code>[RackEndBelief.v_h_str]</code> for a walking body) [@iso13855]. $\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound$ is a design convention the standard adopts, not a measured human maximum, and a person who runs exceeds it. The rack-end deadline therefore inherits a site premise, that nobody in the aisle moves faster, which the site must enforce or qualify.</p>\n<p>This derivation starts from the observed free-space depth at capture. A frozen snapshot constructed with erosion already reserved through its support deadline must not apply that same erosion again; its fixed header still limits use to the validated interval. The safety pipeline evaluates this <strong>free-space erosion</strong> law across every swept volume that the admitted motion and its stopping trajectory will occupy. Any nonpositive result ($\\BeliefInvalidationHorizon \\le 0$) indicates that memory certifies no safe clearance along that path. This relationship formalizes the worst-case boundary of occlusion-aware reachable sets used in automated vehicle navigation, where the reachable volume of an occluded traffic participant expands outward from the visibility horizon [@orzechowski2018tackling].</p>\n<p>Two positions of the hull bracket the problem. Abreast of the rack end, the unobserved cross aisle begins immediately at the upright beam, so $\\FreeSpaceDepth$ equals the lateral clearance between the hull and the rack. Ahead of the vehicle along its current heading, the nearest unobserved boundary lies at the rack corner, $\\ClearDistance$ away. In the top view of @fig-09-rack-end-erosion, a person hidden in the cross aisle can be anywhere inside a disc that grows at $\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound$ from the rack end. In its time plot, the depth certified ahead of the hull falls to zero at <code>[RackEndBelief.t_exp_ahead_str]</code>, while the depth abreast sits at zero from the instant of capture. Notebook \\ref{nbk-memory-erosion-deadline} evaluates both cases numerically.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: The navigation camera has just seen the aisle empty, and the rack uprights hide the cross aisle. For how long does that frame certify the space abreast of the rack end, and the space ahead of the hull, and how much of the second deadline is left when memory receives the frame?</p>\n<p><strong>Variables</strong>:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Localization bound $\\LocalizationBound =$ <code>[RackEndBelief.delta_loc_str]</code> (illustrative) and protective clearance $\\ProtectiveClearanceMargin =$ <code>[RackEndBelief.delta_margin_str]</code> (chosen).</li>\n<li>Approach speed $\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound =$ <code>[RackEndBelief.v_h_str]</code> (datasheet: the ISO 13855 walking speed).</li>\n<li>Free-space depth abreast, the side clearance, $\\FreeSpaceDepth =$ <code>[RackEndBelief.side_clearance_str]</code>; ahead, $\\FreeSpaceDepth = \\ClearDistance =$ <code>[RackEndBelief.d_clear_str]</code> (both illustrative).</li>\n<li>Frame age at dispatch $\\ObservationAge =$ <code>[RackEndBelief.t_age_str]</code> (derived, @sec-perception-latency-waterfall); navigation-camera frame period <code>[RackEndBelief.frame_period_str]</code> (datasheet).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Abreast of the rack end, @eq-mem-free-space-expiry gives <code>[RackEndBelief.erosion_abreast_math]</code> Ahead of the hull it gives <code>[RackEndBelief.erosion_ahead_math]</code> and the frame's age at dispatch leaves <code>[RackEndBelief.erosion_left_math]</code></p>\n<p><strong>Result</strong>: Abreast of the rack end the frame certifies nothing, even at the instant of capture. Ahead of the hull it certifies <code>[RackEndBelief.t_exp_ahead_str]</code>, of which the frame's age spends <code>[RackEndBelief.d_age_eroded_str]</code> of depth before memory receives it, leaving about <code>[RackEndBelief.frames_bridged_str]</code> frame periods.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: The deadline is set by the distance from the unobserved boundary, not by the frame rate or the speed of the lookup. Each millimeter of free depth beyond $\\LocalizationBound + \\ProtectiveClearanceMargin$ buys <code>[RackEndBelief.ms_per_mm_str]</code> ms, so no camera, however fast, gives the position beside the rack end any memory at all; only a sensor that sees the cross aisle can.</p>\n<p>In the open aisle, spatial memory reliably bridges temporary camera frame drops. However, the notebook's deadline is computed for a hull standing at its current pose. A hull that drives toward the rack end at speed $\\SpeedAtDetection$ closes on the occlusion boundary while the hidden person closes on it from the other side. The depth certified ahead therefore shrinks at $\\SpeedAtDetection + \\HumanWalkingSpeedBound$ rather than at $\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound$. Each successive swept volume along the planned path starts closer to the boundary, until the horizon reaches zero abreast of the rack uprights. The stopping budget makes the same point in distance. At the <code>[RackEndBelief.v_drive_str]</code> drive limit, the stopping-budget terms charged before memory enters already claim <code>[RackEndBelief.running_total_str]</code> of the <code>[RackEndBelief.d_clear_str]</code> clear distance before anyone moves in the cross aisle. The safety enforcement path must therefore evaluate @eq-mem-free-space-expiry along the entire planned trajectory. The robot must bring itself to a controlled stop, or reacquire line-of-sight visibility into the cross aisle, before any point on the swept path breaches its invalidation deadline. The clear distance the sensors can still see caps the speed at which the hull may approach an occluded rack end, a ceiling that @sec-enforcement-phase-portraits plots against clearance. Memory certifies clearance only to a depth governed by the physical distance to the occlusion boundary. At the rack corner, that geometric depth vanishes completely.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ RACK-END FREE-SPACE EROSION (FIGURE)\n# │ Context: @fig-09-rack-end-erosion\n# │ Goal:    show where memory&#x27;s claim of empty space comes from and how fast\n# │          it lapses: the unobserved cross aisle erodes the free region at\n# │          walking speed, so the claim is long ahead and nil abreast\n# │ Show:    (a) top view: sightline, occluded cross aisle, hull, D_clear, side\n# │          clearance, and a hidden person&#x27;s reach at the frame&#x27;s dispatch\n# │          and at T_inv; (b) certified depth vs elapsed time, ahead and\n# │          abreast, with the dispatch age marked\n# │ How:     all values from the RackEndBelief cell; reach radius = v_h t;\n# │          certified depth = d_free - delta_loc - delta_margin - v_h t.\n# │          Hull width = aisle width - 2 x side clearance (drawing only);\n# │          rack depth, hull length, and cross-aisle extent are schematic.\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport numpy as np\nfrom matplotlib.patches import Rectangle, Polygon, Circle\nfrom mlsysim import viz\n\n# ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────────\nRB = RackEndBelief\nD_CLEAR = RB.d_clear.to(meter).magnitude  # category: illustrative (from the cell)\nSIDE = RB.side_clearance.to(meter).magnitude  # category: illustrative (from the cell)\nD_LOC = RB.delta_loc.to(meter).magnitude  # category: illustrative (from the cell)\nD_MARGIN = RB.delta_margin.to(meter).magnitude  # category: chosen (from the cell)\nV_H = RB.v_h.to(meter / second).magnitude  # category: datasheet (from the cell)\nT_AGE = RB.t_age.to(second).magnitude  # category: derived (from the cell)\nT_INV = RB.t_exp_ahead.to(second).magnitude  # category: derived (from the cell)\nW_AISLE = AISLE.aisle_width.to(meter).magnitude  # category: illustrative (registry)\n\n# ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nhalf = W_AISLE / 2  # rack faces at +/- half\nhull_half = half - SIDE  # hull side sits one side clearance from each rack face\ncorner = (D_CLEAR, half)  # rack end: nearest unobserved boundary, D_CLEAR ahead\nr_age = V_H * T_AGE\nr_inv = V_H * T_INV  # equals D_CLEAR - D_LOC - D_MARGIN\ncheck(abs(r_inv - (D_CLEAR - D_LOC - D_MARGIN)) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Reach at T_inv must spend the free depth exactly.&quot;)\nX0, X1, YT = -1.3, 2.5, 1.9  # schematic extents (m)\nt = np.linspace(0, 0.7, 400)\nahead = np.maximum(0.0, D_CLEAR - D_LOC - D_MARGIN - V_H * t)\nabreast = np.maximum(0.0, SIDE - D_LOC - D_MARGIN - V_H * t)\n\n# ┌── 3. PLOT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nviz.set_book_style()\nC = viz.COLORS\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import meter, second\n\nfig, (ax, bx) = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(6.2, 7.6), gridspec_kw=dict(height_ratios=[1.45, 1.0], hspace=0.18))\nFS = 9\n\n# (a) top view\nax.set_aspect(&quot;equal&quot;)\nax.set_xlim(X0, X1)\nax.set_ylim(-1.2, YT)\nax.axis(&quot;off&quot;)\nax.set_title(&quot;(a) Top view at the rack end&quot;, loc=&quot;left&quot;, fontsize=9)\nrack_kw = dict(facecolor=&quot;#E2E8F0&quot;, edgecolor=&quot;#A0AEC0&quot;, lw=0.8)\nax.add_patch(Rectangle((X0, half), D_CLEAR - X0, 0.4, **rack_kw))  # upper rack ends at the cross aisle\nax.add_patch(Rectangle((X0, -half - 0.4), X1 - X0, 0.4, **rack_kw))  # lower rack continues\nax.text(0.55, half + 0.2, &quot;rack&quot;, ha=&quot;center&quot;, va=&quot;center&quot;, fontsize=FS, color=&quot;#4A5568&quot;)\n# occluded part of the cross aisle: beyond the rack end and above the sightline\nslope = half / D_CLEAR\noccl = Polygon([corner, (X1, slope * X1), (X1, YT), (D_CLEAR, YT)], closed=True, facecolor=&quot;white&quot;, edgecolor=&quot;#A0AEC0&quot;, hatch=&quot;////&quot;, lw=0)\nax.add_patch(occl)\nax.text(\n    1.35,\n    1.62,\n    &quot;unknown:\\nhidden cross aisle&quot;,\n    ha=&quot;left&quot;,\n    va=&quot;center&quot;,\n    fontsize=FS,\n    color=&quot;#4A5568&quot;,\n    bbox=dict(boxstyle=&quot;round,pad=0.2&quot;, fc=&quot;white&quot;, ec=&quot;none&quot;),\n)\n# reach disc of a hidden person, clipped to walkable floor\nfloor = Polygon([(X0, -half), (X1, -half), (X1, YT), (D_CLEAR, YT), corner, (X0, half)], closed=True, facecolor=&quot;none&quot;, edgecolor=&quot;none&quot;)\nax.add_patch(floor)\ndisc = Circle(corner, r_inv, facecolor=C[&quot;RedFill&quot;], edgecolor=C[&quot;RedLine&quot;], lw=1.1, ls=(0, (4, 2)), alpha=0.9)\nax.add_patch(disc)\ndisc.set_clip_path(floor)\nring = Circle(corner, r_age, facecolor=&quot;none&quot;, edgecolor=C[&quot;RedLine&quot;], lw=0.8)\nax.add_patch(ring)\nring.set_clip_path(floor)\n# sightline from the navigation camera past the rack end\nax.plot([0, X1], [0, slope * X1], color=&quot;#007A87&quot;, lw=1.0, ls=(0, (5, 2)))\nax.text(\n    2.2,\n    1.08,\n    &quot;sightline&quot;,\n    rotation=np.degrees(np.arctan(slope)),\n    ha=&quot;center&quot;,\n    va=&quot;bottom&quot;,\n    fontsize=FS,\n    color=&quot;#007A87&quot;,\n    rotation_mode=&quot;anchor&quot;,\n    bbox=dict(boxstyle=&quot;square,pad=0.1&quot;, fc=&quot;white&quot;, ec=&quot;none&quot;),\n)\n# hull and camera\nhull_len = 0.9  # schematic\nax.add_patch(Rectangle((-hull_len, -hull_half), hull_len, 2 * hull_half, facecolor=&quot;#F8FAFC&quot;, edgecolor=&quot;#1A4D3E&quot;, lw=1.2))\nax.text(-hull_len / 2, -0.12, &quot;hull&quot;, ha=&quot;center&quot;, va=&quot;center&quot;, fontsize=FS, color=&quot;#1A202C&quot;)\nax.plot([0], [0], marker=&quot;o&quot;, ms=4, color=&quot;#007A87&quot;)\n# D_clear dimension\nax.annotate(&quot;&quot;, xy=(D_CLEAR, -0.42), xytext=(0, -0.42), arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;&lt;-&gt;&quot;, lw=0.8, color=&quot;#1A202C&quot;))\nax.plot([D_CLEAR, D_CLEAR], [-0.5, half], color=&quot;#A0AEC0&quot;, lw=0.6, ls=(0, (2, 2)))\nax.text(D_CLEAR / 2, -0.46, f&quot;$D_{{clear}}$ = {D_CLEAR:.2f} m&quot;, ha=&quot;center&quot;, va=&quot;top&quot;, fontsize=FS, color=&quot;#1A202C&quot;)\n# side clearance\nax.plot([-0.25, -0.25], [hull_half, half], color=&quot;#1A202C&quot;, lw=0.8)\nax.plot([-0.25, -0.25], [half, half + 0.12], color=&quot;#1A202C&quot;, lw=0.6)\nax.text(-0.3, half + 0.2, f&quot;side {SIDE*1000:.0f} mm&quot;, ha=&quot;right&quot;, va=&quot;center&quot;, fontsize=FS, color=&quot;#1A202C&quot;)\n# reach radius and the leftover localization + margin band\nax.annotate(\n    &quot;&quot;, xy=(D_CLEAR - r_inv, 0.58), xytext=(D_CLEAR, 0.58), arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;-&gt;&quot;, lw=0.8, color=C[&quot;RedLine&quot;], shrinkA=0, shrinkB=0)\n)\nax.text(2.1, -0.41, f&quot;reach at $T_{{inv}}$:\\n$v_h T_{{inv}}$ = {r_inv:.2f} m&quot;, ha=&quot;center&quot;, va=&quot;center&quot;, fontsize=FS, color=C[&quot;RedLine&quot;])\nax.text(D_CLEAR + 0.1, half - 0.08, f&quot;at dispatch\\n{r_age*1000:.0f} mm&quot;, ha=&quot;left&quot;, va=&quot;top&quot;, fontsize=FS, color=C[&quot;RedLine&quot;])\n\n# (b) certified depth vs elapsed time\nbx.plot(t * 1000, ahead * 1000, color=C[&quot;BlueLine&quot;], lw=1.8, label=f&quot;hull now: $d_{{free}}$ = {D_CLEAR:.2f} m&quot;)\nbx.plot(t * 1000, abreast * 1000, color=&quot;#4A5568&quot;, lw=1.6, ls=(0, (5, 2)), label=f&quot;hull abreast: $d_{{free}}$ = {SIDE*1000:.0f} mm&quot;)\nbx.axvspan(0, T_AGE * 1000, color=&quot;#E2E8F0&quot;, lw=0)\nbx.text(T_AGE * 1000 + 8, 110, f&quot;frame age at\\ndispatch {T_AGE*1000:.1f} ms&quot;, fontsize=FS, color=&quot;#4A5568&quot;, va=&quot;bottom&quot;)\nbx.plot([T_INV * 1000], [0], &quot;o&quot;, ms=5, color=C[&quot;RedLine&quot;], zorder=5)\nbx.annotate(\n    f&quot;$T_{{inv}}$ = {T_INV*1000:.2f} ms&quot;,\n    xy=(T_INV * 1000, 0),\n    xytext=(690, 260),\n    fontsize=FS,\n    color=C[&quot;RedLine&quot;],\n    ha=&quot;right&quot;,\n    arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;-&gt;&quot;, lw=0.8, color=C[&quot;RedLine&quot;]),\n)\nbx.plot([0], [0], &quot;o&quot;, ms=5, color=C[&quot;RedLine&quot;], zorder=5, clip_on=False)\nbx.text(14, 22, &quot;abreast: 0 ms&quot;, fontsize=FS, color=C[&quot;RedLine&quot;], va=&quot;bottom&quot;)\nbx.set_xlim(0, 700)\nbx.set_ylim(-40, 1050)\nbx.set_xlabel(&quot;Time since the frame (ms)&quot;, fontsize=9)\nbx.set_ylabel(&quot;Certified free depth (mm)&quot;, fontsize=9)\nbx.tick_params(labelsize=9)\nbx.set_title(&quot;(b) Erosion at walking speed&quot;, loc=&quot;left&quot;, fontsize=9)\nbx.legend(loc=&quot;upper right&quot;, fontsize=9, frameon=False)\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n]```\n\n:::\n\nNothing in the software data structures explicitly marks the moment a spatial record expires. The occupancy map may continue to report an empty cross aisle, and the state estimator may continue to publish syntactically valid ROS messages, yet the prediction has ceased to function as physical evidence. Cells within the erosion front revert to unobserved space, and the region beside the rack end becomes uncertified the instant the uprights obscure line of sight. The robot&#x27;s stopping budget defends against a static obstacle at $\\ClearDistance$, such as a stationary worker or a dropped tote. Spatial memory adds a temporal deadline to that stopping budget without granting any additional physical braking clearance; the `[RackEndBelief.running_total_str]` partial stopping distance established in @sec-perception-latency-waterfall is the same with memory as without it.\n\nA pedestrian actively walking toward the robot during a stopping maneuver alters the safety budget completely. In standardized safety calculations, an approaching human introduces an intrusion distance $\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound(\\PrebrakeDelay + \\StoppingDuration)$, where walking speed acts across both the system reaction latency $\\PrebrakeDelay$ and the mechanical braking time $\\StoppingDuration$ [@iso13855]. At the `[RackEndBelief.v_drive_str]` drive limit, where a constant-deceleration stop requires $\\StoppingDuration = \\SpeedAtDetection/\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$, this pedestrian displacement term alone consumes `[RackEndBelief.d_walk_term_str]`. This distance already includes the declared reaction latency and exceeds the entire `[RackEndBelief.d_clear_str]` clearance before adding the separately charged controller dispatch interval or the vehicle’s own stopping travel. The walking-person speed ceiling this term imposes on a jerk-limited stop is derived in @sec-enforcement-composite-budget.\n\nStatic clearance models therefore cannot justify an unrestricted shared pedestrian crossing. Mitigating this risk requires engineered site controls, such as designated pedestrian crosswalks coupled with fixed infrastructure presence sensing at rack corners. Until detection coverage, sensor latency, and traffic protocols are qualified, site operations must retain this premise as an active operational constraint. Under no circumstances can stale spatial memory substitute for missing sensory evidence. Regardless of which traffic rules govern the facility, the robot&#x27;s permission pipeline must reject any trajectory whose clearance margin relies on unobserved space past the belief&#x27;s invalidation deadline.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ Unqualified environmental premises are enforced as operational restrictions in @sec-frontier-residual-claims-register.\n:::\n\n### The invalidation horizon {#sec-memory-invalidation-horizon}\n\nThe two rack-end deadlines, for the aisle ahead and for the space abreast of the rack end, are instances of one physical quantity: the elapsed time at which a belief&#x27;s growing error bound reaches the tolerance of the task it supports. Beyond that point, an extrapolated belief can no longer prevent a collision or a mechanical jam.\n\n::: {#dfn-memory-belief-invalidation-horizon .callout-definition title=&quot;Belief state invalidation horizon&quot;}\n\n***Belief state invalidation horizon*** ($\\BeliefInvalidationHorizon$) is the elapsed time since the last physical observation at which the belief&#x27;s validated error bound $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)$ first reaches the tolerance $\\PositionalTaskTolerance$:\n$$\\BeliefInvalidationHorizon = \\inf \\{ \\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant \\ge 0 : \\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant) \\ge \\PositionalTaskTolerance \\}$$ {#eq-mem-invalidation-horizon}\nwhere $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)$ is a bounded-disturbance growth law such as @eq-mem-occlusion-expansion or a calibrated error quantile, and a belief whose evidence epoch is $\\EvidenceEpoch$ stops supporting motion at $\\EvidenceEpoch + \\BeliefInvalidationHorizon$.\n\n1.  **Significance**: Marks the instant at which forward-propagated state estimation ceases to function as valid physical evidence, requiring the permission path to refuse dependent motion proposals before geometric clearance is breached. The same walking-speed bound gives the free space ahead of the hull `[RackEndBelief.t_exp_ahead_str]` and the space abreast of the rack end `[RackEndBelief.t_exp_abreast_str]`, so the horizon belongs to a belief and its tolerance, never to the machine as a whole.\n2.  **Distinction**: Unlike open-loop timeout watchdogs that monitor software process heartbeat intervals, the invalidation horizon is derived from continuous physical kinematics, bounded disturbance dynamics, and mechanical clearance constraints.\n3.  **Common pitfall**: Allowing a neural planner or Kalman filter to continuously extrapolate state estimates during extended sensor dropouts without bounding error expansion, mistaking smooth mathematical extrapolation for physical ground truth.\n\n:::\n\nWhen the error growth law is linear, $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant) = \\InitialTrackingError + \\ErrorGrowthRate\\,\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$, the invalidation horizon simplifies to a direct closed form:\n$$\\BeliefInvalidationHorizon = \\frac{\\PositionalTaskTolerance - \\InitialTrackingError}{\\ErrorGrowthRate}$$ {#eq-mem-linear-horizon}\nwhere a nonpositive result ($\\BeliefInvalidationHorizon \\le 0$) indicates that the belief cannot support the task even at the instant of capture. The free-space erosion law of @eq-mem-free-space-expiry instantiates this linear form with initial localization uncertainty $\\InitialTrackingError = \\LocalizationBound$, approach speed $\\ErrorGrowthRate = \\HumanWalkingSpeedBound$, and task clearance $\\PositionalTaskTolerance = \\FreeSpaceDepth - \\ProtectiveClearanceMargin$. The moving mug-handle belief in @sec-memory-spatial-state-schema applies this exact formulation using conveyor belt velocity. Computed for the evidence behind one action chunk, the root of @eq-mem-invalidation-horizon is the evidence horizon of @sec-brain-two-proposers.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The intent lease uses the invalidation horizon as the evidence horizon in its expiry; for an untracked conveyor target that horizon takes this linear form, as developed in @sec-intent-goals-expire.\n:::\n\n### Accelerating error at the latch slot {#sec-memory-latch-slot-growth}\n\nIn the rack-end scenario, a linear velocity bound set the erosion rate; at the cage door, the narrow opening of the latch-release slot sets the task tolerance $\\PositionalTaskTolerance$ while unmodeled accelerations compound quadratically over time. When the gripper finger enters the slot, the end-effector blocks the wrist camera&#x27;s view, forcing the state estimator to integrate commanded joint torques through a nominal plant model without visual feedback. Because unmodeled forces and cable drag accelerate position error far faster than constant-velocity drift, an open-loop bridge that held across a dropped frame in the aisle can jam the finger against the slot wall in tens of milliseconds.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ LATCH-SLOT FINGER BELIEF</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion (quadratic root, tightened slot,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          constant-velocity overrun), @fig-uncertainty-growth (plot inputs),</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @sec-memory-spatial-state-schema (the filled belief record).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Show how initial error and acceleration constrain the horizon</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          as slot tolerance tightens, and fill the chapter's belief record</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          on the machine's own arm.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    the invalidation horizon of the finger in the latch-release slot;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the horizon for a tighter slot; what a constant-velocity estimator</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          would admit; the clock-conversion bound the record carries.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     E(t) = E0 + v_drift t + a_dist t^2 / 2; each horizon is the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          positive root of E(t) = E_max.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_math, fmt_magnitude, fmt_qty_int from mlsysim.core.units import dimensionless, millimeter, second</p>\n<p>class LatchSlotBelief:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>e0 = LatchTransformStaleness.e0  # category: illustrative (initial error at the wrist capture, calc-memory-latch-transform-staleness) v_drift = LatchTransformStaleness.v_drift  # category: illustrative (unobserved velocity error bound, calc-memory-latch-transform-staleness) a_dist = Scenarios.LatchSlotFingerBelief.growth_acceleration  # category: illustrative (disturbance acceleration bound) e_max = LatchTransformStaleness.e_max  # category: illustrative (latch-release slot tolerance, calc-memory-latch-transform-staleness) e_max_tight = Scenarios.LatchSlotFingerBeliefTightSlot.tolerance  # category: illustrative (tighter machined slot) eps_sync = ( Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle.clock_conversion_bound )  # category: illustrative (clock-conversion bound, @sec-perception-observation-contract)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>R = Scenarios.LatchSlotFingerBelief.result t_exp = R.horizon t_exp_tight = Scenarios.LatchSlotFingerBeliefTightSlot.result.horizon tol_ratio = (e_max / e_max_tight).to(dimensionless).magnitude horizon_ratio = (t_exp / t_exp_tight).to(dimensionless).magnitude t_linear = R.constant_velocity_horizon e_linear = R.error_at_constant_velocity_horizon overshoot = R.overshoot t_extra = R.overrun dt_frame = LatchTransformStaleness.dt e_one_frame = Scenarios.LatchSlotStaleWristTransform.result.same_epoch_error</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(t_exp.magnitude - 65.9) &lt; 0.05, f\"Latch-slot expiry should be 65.9 ms, got {t_exp}.\") check(abs(t_exp_tight.magnitude - 16.1) &lt; 0.05, f\"Tight-slot expiry should be 16.1 ms, got {t_exp_tight}.\") check(horizon_ratio &gt; tol_ratio, \"In this scenario the horizon ratio exceeds the total-tolerance ratio.\") check(abs(t_linear.magnitude - 75.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Constant-velocity horizon should be 75 ms, got {t_linear}.\") check(abs(e_linear.magnitude - 1.1406) &lt; 1e-3, f\"Bound at the constant-velocity horizon should be 1.141 mm, got {e_linear}.\") check(t_extra.magnitude &gt; 0, \"The constant-velocity rule must admit the belief past its bounded deadline.\") check(e_one_frame &lt; e_max &lt; LatchTransformStaleness.e_tool, \"Consistent-epoch composition must fit the slot where the chimeric chain did not.\") check( abs(eps_sync.to(millisecond).magnitude - 0.2) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Clock-conversion bound should match the perception contract's 0.2 ms, got {eps_sync}.\" )</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>e0_str = fmt_length(e0, unit=millimeter, precision=1) v_drift_str = fmt_velocity(v_drift, unit=millimeter / second, precision=0) a_dist_str = fmt_acceleration(a_dist, unit=millimeter / second**2, precision=0) e_max_str = fmt_length(e_max, unit=millimeter, precision=0) e_max_tight_str = fmt_length(e_max_tight, unit=millimeter, precision=2) slack_str = fmt_length(e_max - e0, unit=millimeter, precision=1) slack_tight_str = fmt_length(e_max_tight - e0, unit=millimeter, precision=1) t_exp_str = fmt_qty_int(t_exp, millisecond) t_exp_tight_str = fmt_latency(t_exp_tight, unit=millisecond, precision=1) tol_ratio_mult_str = fmt_multiple(tol_ratio, precision=1) horizon_ratio_mult_str = fmt_multiple(horizon_ratio, precision=1) t_linear_str = fmt_latency(t_linear, unit=millisecond, precision=0) e_linear_str = fmt_length(e_linear, unit=millimeter, precision=3) overshoot_pct_str = fmt_percent(overshoot, precision=1, style=\"prose\") t_extra_str = fmt_qty_int(t_extra, millisecond) eps_sync_str = fmt_latency(eps_sync, unit=millisecond, precision=1) e_one_frame_str = fmt_length(e_one_frame, unit=millimeter, precision=2)</p>\n<p>_e0 = fmt_magnitude(e0, millimeter, precision=1) _v = fmt_magnitude(v_drift, millimeter / second, precision=0) _half_a = fmt_magnitude(a_dist / 2, millimeter / second<strong>2, precision=0) _a = fmt_magnitude(a_dist, millimeter / second</strong>2, precision=0) growth_math = fmt_math( rf\"\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant) = {_e0} + {_v}\\,\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant + {_half_a}\\,(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)^2\" ) quad_math = fmt_math( rf\"{_half_a}\\,(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)^2 + {_v}\\,\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant - {fmt_magnitude(e_max - e0, millimeter, precision=1)} = 0\" ) quad_tight_math = fmt_math( rf\"{_half_a}\\,(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)^2 + {_v}\\,\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant - {fmt_magnitude(e_max_tight - e0, millimeter, precision=1)} = 0\" ) growth_law_math = fmt_math( rf\"\\BeliefErrorBound(\\TimeInstant) = {_e0}\\text{{ mm}} + ({_v}\\text{{ mm/s}})\\,\\TimeInstant + \\tfrac12({_a}\\text{{ mm/s}}^2)\\,\\TimeInstant^2\" ) ]```</p>\n<p>Throughout that blind insertion, the finger must remain aligned within <code>[LatchSlotBelief.e_max_str]</code> of the slot centerline, defining the mechanical task clearance $\\PositionalTaskTolerance$. The other bounds carry over from the missed-frame scenario in @sec-memory-reference-frames: an initial position error $\\InitialTrackingError \\le$ <code>[LatchSlotBelief.e0_str]</code> and an unobserved velocity drift $\\DriftVelocityBound \\le$ <code>[LatchSlotBelief.v_drift_str]</code>. During occlusion, dynamic disturbances add an unmodeled acceleration bounded by $\\DisturbanceAccelerationBound \\le$ <code>[LatchSlotBelief.a_dist_str]</code>. Under these bounds the error grows quadratically from $\\InitialTrackingError$ and meets $\\PositionalTaskTolerance$ at the invalidation horizon $\\BeliefInvalidationHorizon =$ <code>[LatchSlotBelief.t_exp_str]</code> (@fig-uncertainty-growth). A naive constant-velocity estimate rises more slowly and keeps admitting the belief out to <code>[LatchSlotBelief.t_linear_str]</code>, after the bounded error has already breached the clearance.</p>\n<p>Because the belief representation transforms the slot relative to the wrist pose at its exact capture epoch, which resolves the coordinate staleness analyzed in @sec-memory-reference-frames, the growth law in @eq-mem-occlusion-expansion is freed from lever-arm rotational compounding. Position error expands solely through drift velocity and disturbance acceleration. Over a single <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.dt_str]</code> frame interval, this formulation limits position error to <code>[LatchSlotBelief.e_one_frame_str]</code>, compared to <code>[LatchTransformStaleness.e_tool_str]</code> under an uncoordinated coordinate transform chain. Eliminating lever-arm extrapolation error allows the manipulation system to bridge brief sensor dropouts reliably where an unaligned pipeline would fail immediately.</p>\n<p>Evaluating this bounded growth model with the scenario parameters yields <code>[LatchSlotBelief.growth_math]</code>, where elapsed time $\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$ is in seconds and error $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)$ is in millimeters. Setting $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant) = \\PositionalTaskTolerance$ produces the quadratic characteristic equation <code>[LatchSlotBelief.quad_math]</code>. The positive real root establishes an invalidation horizon of $\\BeliefInvalidationHorizon =$ <code>[LatchSlotBelief.t_exp_str]</code>. Motion plans relying on this visual estimate must complete or transition to a qualified fallback before this deadline, with deceleration commands dispatched in advance.</p>\n<p>If the mechanical slot is machined to a tighter clearance of <code>[LatchSlotBelief.e_max_tight_str]</code>, the invalidation horizon contracts sharply to <code>[LatchSlotBelief.t_exp_tight_str]</code> (<code>[LatchSlotBelief.quad_tight_math]</code>). Tightening the mechanical clearance by a factor of <code>[LatchSlotBelief.tol_ratio_mult_str]</code> reduces the available prediction window by a factor of <code>[LatchSlotBelief.horizon_ratio_mult_str]</code>, because the initial error consumes a larger share of the tighter tolerance. Subtracting the unchanged initial error leaves <code>[LatchSlotBelief.slack_str]</code> in the original slot and <code>[LatchSlotBelief.slack_tight_str]</code> in the tighter one, so the usable-slack ratio exceeds the total-tolerance ratio. Acceleration separately shortens both horizons. In contrast, an estimator that naively assumes constant velocity ($\\DisturbanceAccelerationBound = 0$) predicts an overly optimistic horizon of $(\\PositionalTaskTolerance - \\InitialTrackingError)/\\DriftVelocityBound =$ <code>[LatchSlotBelief.t_linear_str]</code>. By that time, the bounded acceleration model permits error up to <code>[LatchSlotBelief.e_linear_str]</code>, exceeding the allowable clearance by <code>[LatchSlotBelief.overshoot_pct_str]</code>. A controller operating on constant-velocity extrapolation would continue unguided insertion for <code>[LatchSlotBelief.t_extra_str]</code> beyond safe physical bounds, risking mechanical jam or tool damage.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ BELIEF UNCERTAINTY AND INVALIDATION HORIZON (FIGURE)\n# │ Context: @fig-uncertainty-growth\n# │ Goal:    show that a belief has a shelf life set by disturbance dynamics\n# │ Show:    the bounded error growth against task clearance, and what a\n# │          constant-velocity estimator would have admitted instead\n# │ How:     E(dt) = e0 + v*dt + a*dt^2; the horizon is its positive root\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport numpy as np\nfrom mlsysim import viz\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import millimeter, second\n\n# ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────────\nE0 = LatchSlotBelief.e0.to(\n    millimeter\n).magnitude  # mm, residual error at the last observation  # category: illustrative (@sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion cell)\nV_DIST = LatchSlotBelief.v_drift.to(millimeter / second).magnitude  # mm/s, bounded disturbance velocity  # category: illustrative (as above)\nA_DIST = (\n    (LatchSlotBelief.a_dist / 2).to(millimeter / second**2).magnitude\n)  # mm/s^2, half the bounded disturbance acceleration (a_dist / 2)  # category: illustrative (as above)\nE_MAX = LatchSlotBelief.e_max.to(millimeter).magnitude  # mm, task clearance  # category: illustrative (as above)\n\n# ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nbounded = lambda dt: E0 + V_DIST * dt + A_DIST * dt**2\nlinear = lambda dt: E0 + V_DIST * dt\n\n# positive root of A dt^2 + V dt + (E0 - E_MAX) = 0\nt_exp = (-V_DIST + np.sqrt(V_DIST**2 - 4 * A_DIST * (E0 - E_MAX))) / (2 * A_DIST)\nt_linear = (E_MAX - E0) / V_DIST  # what constant velocity predicts\novershoot = bounded(t_linear) / E_MAX - 1.0  # by how much that overruns\n\ndt = np.linspace(0, 0.120, 400)\n\n# ┌── 3. PLOT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\ncheck(abs(t_exp - LatchSlotBelief.t_exp.to(second).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;The plotted horizon must match the LatchSlotBelief root.&quot;)\nfig, ax, COLORS, plt = viz.setup_plot(figsize=(6.2, 3.8))\nax.axvspan(t_exp * 1000, 120, color=COLORS[&quot;RedFill&quot;], alpha=0.35, lw=0, zorder=0)\nax.text(118, 0.08, &quot;belief expired&quot;, ha=&quot;right&quot;, va=&quot;bottom&quot;, fontsize=9, color=COLORS[&quot;RedLine&quot;])\nax.plot(dt * 1000, bounded(dt), color=COLORS[&quot;BlueLine&quot;], lw=2.4, label=&quot;bounded growth  $E(\\\\Delta t)$&quot;, zorder=4)\nax.plot(dt * 1000, linear(dt), color=COLORS[&quot;primary&quot;], lw=1.6, ls=(0, (6, 3)), label=&quot;constant-velocity estimate&quot;, zorder=3)\nax.axhline(E_MAX, color=COLORS[&quot;RedLine&quot;], lw=1.4, ls=(0, (5, 4)), zorder=3)\nax.text(2, E_MAX * 1.03, f&quot;task clearance $r_{{\\\\mathrm{{tol}}}}$ = {E_MAX:.0f} mm&quot;, color=COLORS[&quot;RedLine&quot;], fontsize=9, va=&quot;bottom&quot;)\n\nax.plot([t_exp * 1000], [E_MAX], &quot;o&quot;, ms=7, color=COLORS[&quot;BlueLine&quot;], zorder=6)\nax.vlines(t_exp * 1000, 0, E_MAX, color=COLORS[&quot;BlueLine&quot;], lw=1.0, ls=(0, (1, 2)), zorder=3)\nax.annotate(\n    f&quot;invalidation horizon\\n$T_{{inv}}$ = {t_exp*1000:.0f} ms&quot;,\n    xy=(t_exp * 1000, E_MAX),\n    xytext=(t_exp * 1000 - 8, E_MAX * 1.55),\n    fontsize=9,\n    color=COLORS[&quot;BlueLine&quot;],\n    ha=&quot;right&quot;,\n    zorder=6,\n    arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;-&gt;&quot;, color=COLORS[&quot;BlueLine&quot;], lw=0.9),\n)\n\nax.plot([t_linear * 1000], [bounded(t_linear)], &quot;o&quot;, ms=7, color=COLORS[&quot;RedLine&quot;], zorder=6)\nax.annotate(\n    f&quot;still admitted at {t_linear*1000:.0f} ms:\\n&quot; f&quot;{bounded(t_linear):.3f} mm, {overshoot*100:.1f}% over clearance&quot;,\n    xy=(t_linear * 1000, bounded(t_linear)),\n    xytext=(118, 0.42),\n    fontsize=9,\n    color=COLORS[&quot;RedLine&quot;],\n    ha=&quot;right&quot;,\n    zorder=6,\n    arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;-&gt;&quot;, color=COLORS[&quot;RedLine&quot;], lw=0.9),\n)\n\nax.set_xlim(0, 120)\nax.set_ylim(0, 2.1)\nax.set_xlabel(&quot;Elapsed time since last observation, $\\\\Delta t$ (ms)&quot;, fontsize=9)\nax.set_ylabel(&quot;Spatial error bound, $E(\\\\Delta t)$ (mm)&quot;, fontsize=9)\nax.tick_params(labelsize=9)\nax.legend(loc=&quot;upper left&quot;, fontsize=9)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n]```\n\n:::\n\n### Covariance growth and probabilistic bounds {#sec-memory-covariance-growth}\n\nWhereas the rack-end and latch-slot horizons bounded worst-case drift deterministically, stochastic filters track sensor dropouts through variance, where double-integrating unmodeled acceleration noise causes position variance to grow with the cube of elapsed time and feeding a raw variance directly into a geometric clearance check confuses statistical spread with a physical boundary. Inside a stochastic state estimator, variance propagation separates three distinct physical mechanisms that drive error growth during an occlusion. An unmeasured velocity error at the onset of occlusion causes position variance to expand quadratically as $\\InitialVelocityStandardDeviation^2(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)^2$. Unmodeled stochastic forces such as torque ripple or structural vibration, integrated twice across the dynamics, expand position variance cubically as $\\frac{1}{3}\\AccelerationNoiseSpectralDensity(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)^3$; these variance terms define the spread that calibrated confidence quantiles must absorb. In contrast, a persistent unmodeled force bounded by $|\\DisturbanceAccelerationBound|$ (cable drag or sliding friction, for instance) deflects the body deterministically by at most $\\frac{1}{2}\\DisturbanceAccelerationBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)^2$, corresponding to the quadratic term in @eq-mem-occlusion-expansion.\n\n: **Non-Smooth Contact Dynamics**: Coulomb friction is set-valued at zero sliding speed, and a switch between stick and slip changes the contact dynamics. An impact can also change velocity abruptly. A smooth drift model qualified for one contact regime does not automatically bound these transitions. The belief record therefore treats a change of contact mode as leaving its validity region (@sec-memory-spatial-state-schema), not as one more bounded disturbance.\n\nThese stochastic variance terms populate the estimator&#x27;s internal **covariance matrix**, which quantifies the dispersion of a probabilistic error model rather than a hard physical clearance bound. Position variances carry squared length units ($\\text{m}^2$), so their growth cannot be substituted directly into geometric clearance checks. To examine how stochastic uncertainty expands during complete sensor dropouts, consider a kinematic body whose continuous translational acceleration is driven by unmodeled disturbance or actuator jitter modeled as zero-mean continuous Gaussian white noise. The acceleration obeys $\\CartesianAcceleration(\\TimeInstant) = \\ProcessNoiseVector(\\TimeInstant)$, with $\\ProcessNoiseVector(\\TimeInstant) \\sim \\WhiteNoiseProcess(0, \\AccelerationNoiseDensityMatrix)$, where $\\AccelerationNoiseDensityMatrix = \\AccelerationNoiseSpectralDensity \\IdentityMatrix$ represents the continuous power spectral density of acceleration noise with physical units $\\text{m}^2/\\text{s}^3$.\n\nIntegrating acceleration once yields velocity $\\Velocity(\\TimeInstant) = \\Velocity(0) + \\int_0^\\TimeInstant \\ProcessNoiseVector(\\IntegrationDummyTime)\\,d\\IntegrationDummyTime$. Integrating a second time over elapsed duration $\\TimeInstant$ gives the physical position:\n$$\\CartesianPosition(\\TimeInstant) = \\CartesianPosition(0) + \\Velocity(0)\\TimeInstant + \\int_0^\\TimeInstant (\\TimeInstant-\\IntegrationDummyTime)\\ProcessNoiseVector(\\IntegrationDummyTime)\\,d\\IntegrationDummyTime$$\n\nTo isolate the contribution of process noise, assume initial position and velocity are known precisely. During an occlusion interval where external measurement updates cease, the estimator projects state forward using its nominal transition model $\\EstimatedPosition(\\TimeInstant) = \\EstimatedPosition(0) + \\EstimatedVelocity(0)\\TimeInstant$. The unobserved position error covariance $\\PositionErrorCovariance(\\TimeInstant) = \\mathbb{E}[(\\CartesianPosition(\\TimeInstant) - \\EstimatedPosition(\\TimeInstant))(\\CartesianPosition(\\TimeInstant) - \\EstimatedPosition(\\TimeInstant))^\\top]$ accumulates continuous noise variance through double integration:\n$$\\PositionErrorCovariance(\\TimeInstant) = \\int_0^\\TimeInstant (\\TimeInstant-\\IntegrationDummyTime)^2 \\AccelerationNoiseSpectralDensity \\IdentityMatrix\\,d\\IntegrationDummyTime = \\AccelerationNoiseSpectralDensity \\IdentityMatrix \\left[ -\\frac{(\\TimeInstant-\\IntegrationDummyTime)^3}{3} \\right]_0^\\TimeInstant = \\frac{1}{3} \\AccelerationNoiseSpectralDensity \\TimeInstant^3 \\IdentityMatrix$$\n\nDouble integration causes process-noise covariance to expand cubically with elapsed time ($\\propto \\TimeInstant^3$), the same $\\sqrt{\\TimeInstant^3/3}$ growth that set the BMI088 accelerometer&#x27;s noise contribution in @sec-perception-state-estimation. If initial state uncertainty is nonzero, the complete error covariance additionally incorporates propagated initial position variance, initial velocity variance, and their cross-covariance terms. Raw covariance represents stochastic dispersion rather than a guaranteed physical clearance boundary. To use this uncertainty in safety enforcement, the system must project covariance into a spatial safety margin through a calibrated coverage factor $\\CoverageFactor$ (for instance, corresponding to a Chebyshev or Gaussian confidence bound), augmented by bounded systemic biases omitted by the noise model. For spherical or polyhedral bounding volumes, the nominal geometric hull can be inflated dynamically:\n$$\\InflatedHullRadius(\\TimeInstant) = \\HullSphereRadius + \\CoverageFactor \\sqrt{\\LargestEigenvalue(\\PositionErrorCovariance(\\TimeInstant))}$$\nwhere $\\LargestEigenvalue(\\PositionErrorCovariance(\\TimeInstant))$ denotes the principal eigenvalue of the position covariance matrix. The belief record must explicitly furnish these growth bounds and underlying assumptions before the permission path can rely on them to keep execution collision free.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The barrier-function permission test that consumes such clearance margins is developed in @sec-enforcement-safe-sets-conditional-permission.\n:::\n\nA safety argument can rest on one of two distinct paradigms. In a **bounded-disturbance** framework, rigorous physical qualification establishes explicit limits on initial state errors, maximum drift velocities, disturbance accelerations, clock synchronization offsets, and contact mode transitions. Under these conditions, the `[RackEndBelief.t_exp_ahead_str]` aisle-ahead horizon and the `[LatchSlotBelief.t_exp_str]` latch-slot horizon represent deterministic, conditional worst-case deadlines within the qualified operating domain. Alternatively, in an empirical or data-driven regime, controlled sensor dropout trials benchmarked against high-precision ground truth can calibrate residual error quantiles (such as p99 or p99.9) across a representative operational distribution. This yields a probabilistic admission bound with a 1 percent or 0.1 percent tail risk, prior to unmodeled structural shifts. In both architectures, the spatial memory record and the consuming controller must explicitly declare the active validation regime, track operating domain invariants, and trigger a verified safe fallback if observations exceed the validity boundary.\n\nBoth frameworks converge on one engineering invariant. The invalidation horizon (definition \\ref{dfn-memory-belief-invalidation-horizon}) marks the precise instant at which the propagated error bound intersects the allowable task tolerance, regardless of whether that bound stems from worst-case physics or calibrated statistical quantiles. Belief expiration is physical rather than computational. It is neither a generic network socket timeout nor an arbitrary 100 ms polling heuristic selected for scheduling convenience. Rather, $\\BeliefInvalidationHorizon$ is a deadline dictated by spatial clearance, machine kinematics, and disturbance dynamics. Beyond $\\EvidenceEpoch + \\BeliefInvalidationHorizon$, downstream controllers cannot assume the estimated pose satisfies the task&#x27;s safety envelope.\n\n### Expiry across the machine&#x27;s beliefs {#sec-memory-expiry-across-beliefs}\n\nA single global staleness timeout across the robot either admits a blind latch insertion long after the finger has drifted past the slot wall, or halts the mobile base in an open aisle while half a second of certified clearance remains. Because operating speed, carried payload, and contact state reshape disturbance bounds dynamically, the invalidation horizon in @eq-mem-invalidation-horizon cannot be reduced to a single global constant for the entire robot. Instead, the perception and memory interface must compute distinct expiry horizons for each operational regime. Transitions between regimes can alter validity instantaneously; for example, shifting from free flight to mechanical contact introduces impulsive impact dynamics within milliseconds, violating the continuity assumptions of smooth drift models.\n\nMultiple spatial beliefs coexisting on the same physical platform therefore expire along completely different timelines (@tbl-09-belief-decay-horizons). A manipulator finger aligning with a latch slot is constrained by tight millimeter tolerances and compounding acceleration disturbances. Simultaneously, free-space claims around the mobile base and tracking estimates of a coworker near the arm share the same standardized human walking speed bound $\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound$. Even these human-centric beliefs, however, lapse at markedly different intervals, because each originates from a different initial localization uncertainty and must satisfy a distinct geometric tolerance. For volumetric free space, that tolerance is determined by the distance to the nearest occlusion frontier.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ BELIEF-EXPIRY ROOTS ON THE MACHINE</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @tbl-09-belief-decay-horizons (@sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Set the machine's beliefs side by side: free space abreast of the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          rack end and in the aisle ahead, a coworker beside the arm, and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the latch-slot finger.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    each row's growth law and its root against the task limit.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     E(t) = E0 + v t + a t^2 / 2; the root is the positive solution of</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          E(t) = E_max. The coworker and free-space rows are bounded by</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the ISO walking speed, so a = 0 and E(t) is linear; free-space</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          limits are d_free - delta_margin. The rack-end and latch-slot rows</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          reuse the chapter's RackEndBelief and LatchSlotBelief cells.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_math, fmt_magnitude, fmt_qty_int from mlsysim.core.units import millimeter, second</p>\n<p>class MachineExpiryRoots:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>COWORKER = Scenarios.CoworkerBesideArmBelief  # category: illustrative (MLSysIM scenario) cw_e0 = COWORKER.initial_error  # category: illustrative (coworker position error at last observation) cw_v = COWORKER.growth_rate.to(millimeter / second)  # category: datasheet (ISO 13855 walking speed, calc-memory-rack-end) cw_e_max = COWORKER.tolerance  # category: chosen (belief tolerance to the arm's swept volume; not the planner's obstacle clearance)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>cw_t_exp = COWORKER.result.horizon  # v_h bounds speed directly, so a_dist = 0</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(cw_t_exp.magnitude - 84.375) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Coworker expiry should be 84.375 ms, got {cw_t_exp}.\") check( RackEndBelief.t_exp_abreast &lt; LatchSlotBelief.t_exp &lt; cw_t_exp &lt; RackEndBelief.t_exp_ahead, \"Rows must order rack end, latch-slot finger, coworker, aisle ahead.\", )</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>_mm, _mms = millimeter, millimeter / second rack_growth_math = fmt_math( rf\"{fmt_magnitude(RackEndBelief.delta_loc, _mm, precision=0)}+{fmt_magnitude(RackEndBelief.v_h, _mms, precision=0)}\\TimeInstant\" ) rack_limit_str = fmt_magnitude(RackEndBelief.side_clearance - RackEndBelief.delta_margin, _mm, precision=0) rack_root_str = RackEndBelief.t_exp_abreast_str ahead_limit_str = fmt_magnitude(RackEndBelief.d_clear - RackEndBelief.delta_margin, _mm, precision=0) ahead_root_str = RackEndBelief.t_exp_ahead_str cw_growth_math = fmt_math(rf\"{fmt_magnitude(cw_e0, _mm, precision=0)}+{fmt_magnitude(cw_v, _mms, precision=0)}\\TimeInstant\") cw_limit_str = fmt_magnitude(cw_e_max, _mm, precision=0) cw_root_str = fmt_qty_int(cw_t_exp, millisecond) latch_growth_math = fmt_math(rf\"{LatchSlotBelief._e0}+{LatchSlotBelief._v}\\TimeInstant+{LatchSlotBelief._half_a}\\TimeInstant^2\") latch_limit_str = fmt_magnitude(LatchSlotBelief.e_max, _mm, precision=0) latch_root_str = LatchSlotBelief.t_exp_str ]```</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Belief</th><th>Error bound $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\TimeInstant)$ (mm; $\\TimeInstant$ in s)</th><th>Task limit (mm)</th><th>Root</th><th>Response at expiry</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Free space abreast of the rack end</td><td><code>[MachineExpiryRoots.rack_growth_math]</code></td><td><code>[MachineExpiryRoots.rack_limit_str]</code></td><td><code>[MachineExpiryRoots.rack_root_str]</code></td><td>Treat the cross aisle as unknown; refuse dependent advance</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Free space to the rack end ahead</td><td><code>[MachineExpiryRoots.rack_growth_math]</code></td><td><code>[MachineExpiryRoots.ahead_limit_str]</code></td><td><code>[MachineExpiryRoots.ahead_root_str]</code></td><td>Refuse motion that relies on the claim; renew from the next frame</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Latch-slot finger</td><td><code>[MachineExpiryRoots.latch_growth_math]</code></td><td><code>[MachineExpiryRoots.latch_limit_str]</code></td><td><code>[MachineExpiryRoots.latch_root_str]</code></td><td>Refuse the insertion; hold compliantly or reacquire the slot</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Coworker beside the arm</td><td><code>[MachineExpiryRoots.cw_growth_math]</code></td><td><code>[MachineExpiryRoots.cw_limit_str]</code></td><td><code>[MachineExpiryRoots.cw_root_str]</code></td><td>Revoke dependent arm motion; stop only if braking clearance remains</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Belief-expiry roots on the machine</strong>: Each root solves $\\BeliefErrorBound(\\TimeInstant)=\\PositionalTaskTolerance$ for that belief's error model and task limit. The two free-space rows are @eq-mem-free-space-expiry, with limits set by the depth to the nearest unobserved boundary less the protective clearance. The coworker's initial error is illustrative and its tolerance chosen, while its speed is the same walking-speed bound, so all three rows set by a person's motion grow linearly. The last column names the required disposition by that root; any physical response must begin early enough to remain feasible. The mug-handle belief, whose growth is set by the conveyor rather than a person, is worked in @sec-memory-spatial-state-schema. {#tbl-09-belief-decay-horizons tbl-colwidths=\"[20,28,14,12,26]\"}</p>\n<p>When an expiry deadline is reached, the motion permission system must refuse dependent actuation proposals until fresh sensory observations restore valid evidence. Even upon reacquisition, sensory updates renew state authority only under strict validation checks. Upon receiving new sensor data, the estimator computes the measurement innovation (@sec-perception-state-estimation), the discrepancy between incoming physical measurements and predicted sensor observations. Incorporating this innovation requires verifying data association, sensor calibration, and physical feasibility constraints. A large innovation residual typically signals an unmodeled disturbance, an outlier measurement, or a tracking failure, which the estimation filter must isolate before granting motion permissions. Conversely, a state update must never trigger discontinuous step commands to low-level motor controllers; trajectory generation must preserve acceleration continuity. As the force-and-bias case of @sec-memory-state-estimation-bounds shows, a deceptively small innovation residual in observable dimensions also provides no guarantee of accuracy along unobserved state dimensions.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ EXPIRY CHECKPOINT: GIVEN AND GUARD FOR THE COWORKER ESTIMATE\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @chk-mem-expiry-representation (the expiry-estimate item).\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Print the changed tolerance and guard the verdict the item\n# │          expects without printing the answer: at 100 mm the coworker row\n# │          expires before the latch-slot finger, reversing the table order.\n# │ Show:    the 100 mm tolerance (a checkpoint given).\n# │ How:     T = (E_max - E0) / v_h with MachineExpiryRoots&#x27; E0 and v_h.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_length, check\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import millimeter\n\nclass ExpiryCheckpoint:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given and guard for the expiry checkpoint&#x27;s estimate item.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    cw_e_max_new = Scenarios.CoworkerBesideArmBeliefTightTolerance.tolerance  # category: chosen (checkpoint given: tighter swept-volume tolerance)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    t_new = Scenarios.CoworkerBesideArmBeliefTightTolerance.result.horizon\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(\n        t_new &lt; LatchSlotBelief.t_exp &lt; MachineExpiryRoots.cw_t_exp, f&quot;Tighter tolerance must move the coworker row ahead of the latch row: {t_new}&quot;\n    )\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    cw_e_max_new_str = fmt_length(cw_e_max_new, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    cw_e_max_str = fmt_length(MachineExpiryRoots.cw_e_max, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n]```\n\n::: {#chk-mem-expiry-representation .callout-checkpoint title=&quot;Deadlines, frames, and maps&quot;}\n\nEvery deadline in @tbl-09-belief-decay-horizons comes from a growth law, a tolerance, and a map that preserved the evidence epochs behind them. These items test all three.\n\n- [ ] **Expiry estimate**: If the coworker&#x27;s tolerance tightened from `[ExpiryCheckpoint.cw_e_max_str]` to `[ExpiryCheckpoint.cw_e_max_new_str]`, with the same initial error and walking speed, what would its invalidation horizon be, and where would the row then fall relative to the latch-slot finger?\n- [ ] **Horizon vs. timeout**: Can you explain why a single staleness timeout for every belief must over-admit some rows of the table and under-admit others?\n- [ ] **Common-epoch composition**: Can you explain why the same finger misses the slot after one stale frame on a hand-composed chain yet stays within it for several frames when the slot is chained against the wrist pose of its own capture epoch?\n- [ ] **Representation choice**: Can you explain why the local snapshot is a Euclidean distance field, and which resource, rather than lookup time, then limits its voxel pitch?\n- [ ] **Map revisions**: Can you explain why a loop closure can misplace a remembered cell whose evidence epoch still passes every age check?\n\n:::\n\nEvery invalidation horizon is only as trustworthy as its error growth model. Such a model remains dependable only to the extent that the physical system can detect when its assumptions fail, whether through innovation monitoring upon reacquisition or systematic validation during sensor dropout trials. When unmodeled disturbances act along unobservable degrees of freedom, the system can drift far outside its tolerance without generating any detectable residual, which is why conservative geometric clearance and explicit physical bounds remain indispensable.\n\n## Observability Ceilings and Estimation Limits {#sec-memory-state-estimation-bounds}\n\nA force sensor can report the exact same reading when mechanical contact force increases as when its analog conditioning circuit drifts thermally. Sampling that stream repeatedly cannot distinguish the two physical causes. The underlying question is observability, whether a system&#x27;s available sensor outputs and known physical dynamics suffice to reconstruct the internal states required for control. Consider an instantaneous observation model $\\MeasurementVector = \\ObservationFunction(\\EstimatorStateVector)$. When distinct physical states $\\EstimatorStateVector_1 \\neq \\EstimatorStateVector_2$ yield identical measurements ($\\ObservationFunction(\\EstimatorStateVector_1) = \\ObservationFunction(\\EstimatorStateVector_2)$), the sensor mapping alone cannot separate them. In a combined force-and-bias state, equal and opposite shifts in contact force and sensor bias form an insensitive direction in the observation Jacobian $\\MeasurementMatrix = \\partial \\ObservationFunction / \\partial \\EstimatorStateVector$. Because this direction lies in the null space of $\\MeasurementMatrix$, local state variations along it produce zero change in the measurement vector. The measurement likelihood therefore has zero directional derivative along this unconstrained direction, although it may still vary in directions that change the measured sum. Instantaneous ambiguity does not automatically imply unobservability across time; if subsequent motion or control actions induce dynamics that differentiate the states, temporal filtering can resolve the ambiguity. However, if the ambiguity persists along the entire operating trajectory, no amount of signal processing can recover the lost degrees of freedom. Scaling up neural network parameters or adding recurrent layers expands representational capacity, but computation cannot synthesize physical information that no sensor ever transduced.\n\nObservability depends on both the sensor suite and the executed trajectory. When a physical disturbance drives a required state along an unobserved manifold, an estimator can fall into the **zero-innovation trap**. The innovation vanishes ($\\MeasurementInnovation = \\MeasurementVector - \\MeasurementMatrix\\StateEstimate = \\ZeroVector$), so no measurement ever contradicts the model. The covariance update depends on the modeled noise and measurement geometry rather than the observed residual. It can contract uncertainty in measured or model-coupled directions without revealing omitted drift along an unobservable direction. A misspecified filter that treats bias as known can therefore contract its reported force uncertainty while the true force and omitted bias drift apart without changing the measured sum.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ ILLUSTRATIVE WRIST STRAIN-GAUGE BIAS DRIFT ON THE MOBILE MANIPULATOR</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-memory-state-estimation-bounds (unobservable sensor bias).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Show a confident estimate drifting along an unobservable subspace.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    5 N reading; 0.2 N/min thermal drift over 15 min shifts the bias</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          by 3 N, so the true force is 2 N, 60 percent below nominal.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Illustrative reading, drift rate, and span; shift = rate x span.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          The sensor is sampled at the arm's joint-control rate (registry);</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          MLSysIM carries no wrist force-sensor drift, so the drift</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          values stay illustrative.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_force, fmt_int, check</p>\n<p>class MemoryBiasDrift:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>BIAS = Scenarios.WristForceBiasDrift  # category: illustrative (MLSysIM scenario) y_read = BIAS.reading  # category: illustrative (steady strain-gauge reading) sigma_claim = BIAS.reported_sigma  # category: illustrative (estimator's reported P99 bound) drift_rate = BIAS.drift_rate  # category: illustrative (thermoelastic bias drift) span = BIAS.span  # category: illustrative (drift interval) t_excite = BIAS.excitation  # category: illustrative (deliberate contact break) f_sample = ( Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator.arm.control_frequency )  # category: datasheet (sampled at the arm's joint-control rate)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>shift = BIAS.result.shift x_true = BIAS.result.true_value pct = BIAS.result.lost_share * 100</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(shift.magnitude - 3.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"Bias shift is 3 N.\") check(abs(x_true.magnitude - 2.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"True force falls to 2 N.\") check(abs(pct - 60.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"Drift is 60 percent of nominal.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">margin_bias_drift_confidence_sparkline.svg fig-alt hand-types 5 N, 0.05, 2 N, and 15 minutes</h1>\n<p>check(y_read.magnitude == 5.0 and sigma_claim.magnitude == 0.05 and span.magnitude == 15, \"Margin fig-alt values are stale.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>y_str = fmt_force(y_read, precision=0) zero_str = fmt_force(0 * newton, precision=0) sigma_str = fmt_force(sigma_claim, precision=2) rate_str = fmt(drift_rate.magnitude, precision=1) span_str = fmt_int(span.magnitude) shift_str = fmt_force(shift, precision=0) x_true_str = fmt_force(x_true, precision=0) pct_str = fmt_int(pct) t_excite_str = fmt_int(t_excite.to(millisecond).magnitude) f_sample_str = fmt_int(f_sample.to(hertz).magnitude) ]```</p>\n<p>Consider an illustrative strain-gauge force sensor mounted at the mobile manipulator's wrist, delivering a steady reading of <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.y_str]</code>. Once an unknown bias is introduced, this single reading is compatible with an infinite continuum of true contact forces. An estimator or learned policy that reports this contact force with narrow confidence bounds has not demonstrated that the state is <strong>observable</strong>. When two distinct physical state histories map to the identical sequence of sensor observations, no downstream algorithm, whether an extended Kalman filter or a deep recurrent network, can deduce which trajectory the physical system followed. The digital computing substrate receives identical bitstreams across both realities.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Observability</strong></em> is the property that the history of a machine's sensor outputs over a finite time window, combined with its known control inputs and system dynamics, uniquely determines its initial state, ensuring that distinct physical trajectories produce distinct measurement sequences.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Observability defines the hard physical ceiling on state reconstruction, regardless of model architecture or parameter count. In the wrist strain-gauge example, a <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.shift_str]</code> bias shift over <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.span_str]</code> minutes alters the true contact force by <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.pct_str]</code> percent while the innovation remains zero, because the sensor records only their scalar sum.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Observability is distinct from predictability. An estimator can project a future state with minimal covariance based entirely on prior dynamical assumptions; only measurements sensitive to that state can verify the prediction against reality.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A vanishing innovation does not validate state accuracy. Along an unobservable manifold, the innovation residual can remain zero while tracking error grows. A misspecified filter can still contract its reported covariance under its model assumptions; the zero residual does not justify that contraction.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>: <strong>Observability Rank Test</strong>: For the force-plus-bias state with $\\MeasurementMatrix = [1\\;\\,1]$ and static dynamics $\\StateTransitionMatrix = \\IdentityMatrix$, Kalman's observability matrix stacks $\\MeasurementMatrix$ and $\\MeasurementMatrix\\StateTransitionMatrix$. Because these two rows are identical, the matrix has rank one rather than two [@kalman1960general]. The unobservable direction corresponds to an equal increase in contact force and decrease in sensor bias, a mode that no number of scalar samples can resolve. Restoring full rank requires an independent sensing channel, an active excitation, or an external reference load.</p>\n<p>To trace the operational impact of this distinction, consider the wrist gripper regulating contact force against a rigid surface. Let $\\GripContactForce(\\TimeInstant)$ denote the true normal contact force, while the strain-gauge conditioning electronics introduce an unknown, slowly drifting bias $\\ForceSensorBias(\\TimeInstant)$. The raw measurement stream delivered to the estimator at each sample interval is $\\ForceSensorReading(\\TimeInstant) = \\GripContactForce(\\TimeInstant) + \\ForceSensorBias(\\TimeInstant)$. Suppose the sensor transmits a constant reading $\\ForceSensorReading(\\TimeInstant) =$ <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.y_str]</code>. This measurement is satisfied by a true force $\\GripContactForce(\\TimeInstant) =$ <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.y_str]</code> with zero bias $\\ForceSensorBias(\\TimeInstant) =$ <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.zero_str]</code>. However, it is equally satisfied by a true force $\\GripContactForce(\\TimeInstant) =$ <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.x_true_str]</code> with a sensor bias $\\ForceSensorBias(\\TimeInstant) =$ <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.shift_str]</code>, or even by $\\GripContactForce(\\TimeInstant) =$ <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.zero_str]</code> with a bias $\\ForceSensorBias(\\TimeInstant) =$ <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.y_str]</code>. For any arbitrary offset trajectory $\\UnobservableForceOffset(\\TimeInstant)$, the paired state trajectory $(\\GripContactForce(\\TimeInstant) - \\UnobservableForceOffset(\\TimeInstant), \\ForceSensorBias(\\TimeInstant) + \\UnobservableForceOffset(\\TimeInstant))$ generates an identical measurement sequence $\\ForceSensorReading(\\TimeInstant)$. The difference between physical contact force and sensor bias lies within the <strong>unobservable subspace</strong> of the measurement model, the null space in which the two variations cancel exactly in the digitized signal.</p>\n<p>Unable to distinguish physical load from electronic drift, an estimator must fall back on prior assumptions about state evolution. If a Kalman filter or recurrent estimator assumes that bias $\\ForceSensorBias(\\TimeInstant)$ remains static with near-zero process noise, it attributes all measurement variations in $\\ForceSensorReading(\\TimeInstant)$ directly to changes in contact force $\\GripContactForce(\\TimeInstant)$. As new samples arrive at <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.f_sample_str]</code> $\\text{Hz}$, the estimator repeatedly folds the measurements into its belief state. Because the incoming stream aligns with the forward state propagation, the computed covariance contracts monotonically. The estimator then publishes an aggressively tight uncertainty bound of <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.y_str]</code> $\\pm$ <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.sigma_str]</code> at p99. In reality, heat generated by nearby joint actuators warms the mechanical wrist bracket, inducing thermoelastic drift in the strain gauge. Sensor bias $\\ForceSensorBias(\\TimeInstant)$ creeps at <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.rate_str]</code> $\\text{N/min}$. Over <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.span_str]</code> $\\text{min}$, this thermal drift shifts $\\ForceSensorBias(\\TimeInstant)$ by <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.shift_str]</code>. True physical force on the workpiece drops from <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.y_str]</code> to <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.x_true_str]</code>, a <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.pct_str]</code> percent loss of grip force, even as the estimator reports high statistical certainty in its original value.</p>\n<p>The downstream controller responds to this false certainty by regulating joint torques to maintain what it believes is an optimal <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.y_str]</code> clamping force. If the workpiece requires substantial friction to resist gravity, the actual <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.x_true_str]</code> contact load allows it to slip from the jaws, even as telemetry shows the grip force sitting exactly on target. Standard filter monitoring cannot catch this failure. The estimator evaluates its innovation sequence (the residual formulation of @sec-perception-state-estimation), comparing each incoming measurement $\\ForceSensorReading(\\TimeInstant)$ against its predicted observation $\\PredictedForceReading(\\TimeInstant)$. Throughout the entire drift sequence, the estimator predicts $\\PredictedForceReading(\\TimeInstant) = \\EstimatedGripContactForce(\\TimeInstant) + \\EstimatedForceSensorBias(\\TimeInstant) =$ <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.y_str]</code>, which exactly matches the incoming sensor reading $\\ForceSensorReading(\\TimeInstant) =$ <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.y_str]</code>. The innovation $\\ForceReadingInnovation(\\TimeInstant)$ evaluates to zero at every sample step. An innovation check tests only whether new sensor data conflicts with the model's forward projection; it cannot expose errors aligned with an unobservable subspace where the sensor output remains entirely invariant to physical state drift.</p>\n<p>Restoring observability requires system designers to introduce an external constraint that breaks the symmetry along the unobservable subspace. An engineering architecture accomplishes this in one of three ways. First, independent sensing can introduce a secondary measurement channel governed by distinct failure physics, such as monitoring joint motor drive currents or optical deflection sensors that do not share the strain gauge's thermoelastic drift. Second, the machine can execute deliberate physical excitation. After securing the workpiece with an external fixture, the arm commands the gripper to break contact briefly for <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.t_excite_str]</code> $\\text{ms}$, establishing a known zero-force boundary condition ($\\GripContactForce(\\TimeInstant) =$ <code>[MemoryBiasDrift.zero_str]</code>) where the raw telemetry directly exposes the sensor bias $\\ForceSensorBias(\\TimeInstant)$. Third, the machine can rely on periodic external anchoring, bringing the end-effector into contact with a calibrated reference load cell during scheduled tool-change routines to zero the bias estimate. The mobile manipulator's base already depends on this third remedy. Wheel odometry leaves its longitudinal position along the aisle unobservable, and only the map-based corrections of @sec-memory-localization-slam supply the missing spatial constraint. When no admissible excitation separates two states that call for different responses before the machine must act, and no added channel is available, the states are observationally indistinguishable and no model on that channel can tell them apart. @Sec-frontier-observational-indistinguishability-sub develops this limit and the operating restrictions it forces.</p>\n<p>When a physical state cannot be constrained through complementary sensing or active excitation, the estimation layer must treat that state as unobservable and refuse to publish unwarranted precision. The software interface must either mark the affected state dimensions as invalid or inflate their uncertainty bounds based on the hardware's worst-case physical drift rate, overriding the artificially contracted covariance generated by the filter. Formal observability analysis provides the mathematical foundation for identifying these blind spots. Classical linear state-space formulations originate with @kalman1960general, while nonlinear extensions establish differential observability rank conditions on the Lie derivatives of the observation field [@hermann1977nonlinear]. Systems engineers do not recompute these differential rank conditions in real-time control loops. Instead, the design task is to identify during system modeling which operating regimes lose observability rank, ensuring that runtime state estimators never claim statistical convergence along an unconstrained physical dimension.</p>\n<p>↪ The Kalman and Hermann-Krener observability rank conditions are developed in @sec-appendix-control-covariance.</p>\n<p>Deterministic observability establishes whether system dynamics and sensor equations can distinguish trajectories over time, independent of stochastic noise assumptions. Nevertheless, practical estimator convergence and calibrated covariance bounds demand further conditions: accurately identified kinematic parameters, persistent trajectory excitation, and well-characterized sensor noise statistics. A manipulator trajectory can, for instance, leave a required state direction unconstrained by the available measurements; this estimator observability condition must be checked separately from the arm’s kinematic singularities. Robust physical AI systems therefore deploy runtime monitors that track the validity conditions of each estimator. These watchdogs cross-check redundant sensing channels, track kinematic condition numbers, and enforce re-zeroing schedules. While runtime monitors cannot guarantee the truth of every modeling assumption, any detected violation must immediately invalidate the dependent state estimate. When an estimator explicitly revokes its own confidence, downstream controllers can transition cleanly to a validated fallback policy. An estimator that invalidates its own output fails safely and visibly; the dangerous failures in physical AI occur when an estimator reports tight confidence along an unobservable manifold, silently blinding the controller to physical reality.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h2\">Spatial Belief Failure Modes and Map Corruption</h2>\n<p>Unobservable drift is one member of a broader class of spatial memory failure modes that rarely announce themselves with operating system exceptions or kernel panic logs. When a corrupted pointer or segmentation fault halts a process, the failure is loud and immediate, even if bringing the physical mechanism safely to rest requires dedicated fallback hardware. In contrast, spatial memory failures are quiet and deceptive. The memory subsystem continues publishing well-formed, syntactically valid data structures at high frequency, while the geometric coordinates inside those structures have parted ways with physical reality.</p>\n<p>This divergence exposes the <strong>middleware QoS mirage</strong>, the memory-side counterpart of a limit @sec-nervous-mailbox-exchanges established for transport, where no middleware setting could stand in for the chunk lease. DDS-based robotics middleware, including ROS 2, provides Quality of Service (QoS) policies such as <code>Deadline</code> and <code>Liveliness</code>. These policies monitor declared message intervals and publisher liveliness assertions, not the physical truth of the payload. If an upstream camera driver deadlocks or an object tracker loses its target while a republisher continues sending its cached matrix and satisfying those communication contracts, the policies need not report a violation. Physical evidence validation must therefore accompany transport monitoring; otherwise frozen transforms, stale state-store entries, and phantom tracks can reach motor controllers as apparently healthy data.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ ILLUSTRATIVE FROZEN-TRANSFORM TIMELINE\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-memory-failure-modes (loud and silent failures, frozen\n# │          transform).\n# │\n# │ Goal:    Show a republished stale transform passing a publish-time check\n# │          while tracking error grows.\n# │ Show:    1 ms republish period (1000 Hz); 5 ms freshness threshold; sensor\n# │          lost at 10 ms; the mug riding the pick-station conveyor at\n# │          AISLE.v_conveyor (0.20 m/s) gives 20 mm in 100 ms, past the 15 mm\n# │          grasp tolerance. Also the tool-tip offset of the frame-mismatch\n# │          example and the overconfident predictor&#x27;s prediction span.\n# │ How:     Illustrative timeline; error = speed x elapsed time. Conveyor\n# │          speed and grasp tolerance from the WarehouseAisle scenario.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_int, fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, check\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import meter\n\nclass MemoryFrozenTransform:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────\n    FROZEN = Scenarios.ConveyorMugFrozenTransform  # category: illustrative (MLSysIM scenario)\n    t_repub = FROZEN.republish_period  # category: illustrative (middleware republish period)\n    tau_max = FROZEN.freshness_threshold  # category: illustrative (publish-time freshness threshold)\n    t_loss = FROZEN.loss_time  # category: illustrative (sensor stops producing evidence)\n    v_obj = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle.v_conveyor  # category: illustrative (pick-station conveyor speed carrying the mug)\n    e_grasp = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle.grasp_tolerance  # category: illustrative (mug grasp tolerance)\n    t_window = FROZEN.window  # category: illustrative (elapsed time with static transform)\n    d_tool = 150 * millimeter  # category: illustrative (tool tip beyond the arm&#x27;s mounting flange; MLSysIM carries no TCP offset)\n    t_predict_long = 200 * millisecond  # category: illustrative (extended open-loop prediction span)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    f_repub = FROZEN.result.republish_rate\n    err = FROZEN.result.error\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(t_repub &lt; tau_max, &quot;A republished copy must pass the freshness threshold.&quot;)\n    check(abs(err.magnitude - 20.0) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Tracking error is 20 mm.&quot;)\n    check(err &gt; e_grasp, &quot;The frozen-transform error must exceed the grasp tolerance.&quot;)\n    # margin_frozen_transform_chain.svg fig-alt hand-types 10 ms, 1,000 Hz, 5 ms, 20 mm, and 100 ms\n    check(\n        t_loss.magnitude == 10 and abs(f_repub.magnitude - 1000) &lt; 1e-9 and tau_max.magnitude == 5 and t_window.magnitude == 100,\n        &quot;Margin fig-alt values are stale.&quot;,\n    )\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────\n    t_repub_str = fmt_int(t_repub.to(millisecond).magnitude)\n    tau_max_str = fmt_int(tau_max.to(millisecond).magnitude)\n    t_loss_str = fmt_int(t_loss.to(millisecond).magnitude)\n    f_repub_str = fmt_int(f_repub.magnitude)\n    v_obj_str = fmt_velocity(v_obj, unit=meter / second, precision=2)\n    e_grasp_str = fmt_length(e_grasp, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    d_tool_str = fmt_length(d_tool, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    t_predict_long_str = fmt_latency(t_predict_long, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_window_str = fmt_int(t_window.to(millisecond).magnitude)\n    err_str = fmt_int(err.magnitude)\n]```\n\nConsider a camera that abruptly stops delivering frames while an intermediate transport node continuously republishes the last calculated transform under fresh timestamps. A downstream controller with a `[MemoryFrozenTransform.tau_max_str]` $\\text{ms}$ publish-time freshness check accepts every message without protest. The scenario separates two kinds of failure in an embodied system. In a **loud failure**, a component crashes, halts execution, drops network packets, clears an output buffer, or asserts an explicit hardware error bit. Downstream consumers detect these faults through explicit validity flags or bounded stream timeouts. Avoiding harm then requires a qualified fallback (dynamic braking, position hold, or compliant impedance control) whose detection and response delays fit the remaining physical margin. In a **silent failure**, the spatial memory subsystem continues publishing structurally complete, numerically well-formed state records with valid status bits, low covariance flags, and monotonically increasing timestamps, even as the coordinates within those records drift away from the physical machine. Downstream control loops consume these corrupted records without raising an exception, closing feedback loops around phantom geometry. Physical actuators execute commands derived directly from these plausible yet false trajectories, such as driving an arm toward a target that now intersects a wall. Silent map corruption can therefore cross the causal boundary and inject destructive forces into the environment long before any software monitor registers an anomaly.\n\nWhen an upstream sensor halts entirely, the timestamp anti-pattern of @sec-memory-state-epoch manifests as the **frozen transform**. This represents one of the most deceptive silent failures in embodied robotics because republishing a stale spatial snapshot with updated transport timestamps masks a dead sensor. In an illustrative timeline on the mobile manipulator, the wrist camera&#x27;s tracking pipeline computes the target object&#x27;s transform $\\CameraObjectTransform$ at timestamp $\\EvidenceEpoch = 0\\text{ ms}$. If the optical sensor subsequently disconnects, drops frames, or suffers thread starvation at $\\TimeInstant =$ `[MemoryFrozenTransform.t_loss_str]` $\\text{ms}$, the tracking algorithm ceases to produce new spatial evidence. If the message-passing middleware or an intermediate transport node retains that last computed matrix in an unexpired buffer and republishes it at `[MemoryFrozenTransform.f_repub_str]` $\\text{Hz}$, generating a synthetic transmission timestamp $\\TimeInstant_k = \\EvidenceEpoch + \\RepublicationIndex\\,\\RepublicationPeriod$ with period $\\RepublicationPeriod =$ `[MemoryFrozenTransform.t_repub_str]` $\\text{ms}$, it erases the temporal provenance of the original measurement. A downstream tracking controller verifies freshness by computing data age as $\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant = \\CurrentWallClockTime - \\MessageTimestamp$. Because the republisher stamped the message with the current system clock, $\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$ evaluates to less than `[MemoryFrozenTransform.t_repub_str]` $\\text{ms}$, satisfying the freshness threshold of $\\ObservationAgeBudget =$ `[MemoryFrozenTransform.tau_max_str]` $\\text{ms}$. The controller consumes the transform as current evidence. If the mug rides the pick-station conveyor at `[MemoryFrozenTransform.v_obj_str]` while the reported transform remains static, the controller accumulates `[MemoryFrozenTransform.err_str]` $\\text{mm}$ of spatial tracking error in `[MemoryFrozenTransform.t_window_str]` $\\text{ms}$. This exceeds the `[MemoryFrozenTransform.e_grasp_str]` grasp tolerance, yet the estimator reports zero estimated tracking divergence.\n\n\n\nWhereas a frozen transform repeats a lost sensor&#x27;s last pose, phantom-object tracks arise when an estimator decouples the lifetime of spatial coordinates from the lifetime of existence evidence. Target tracking literature has long treated existence as an independent random variable alongside spatial kinematics: integrated probabilistic data association carries an explicit existence probability that updates with every sensor scan [@musicki1994integrated]. Modern learned trackers implement this separation in different ways. ByteTrack pairs object detection with a Kalman motion model, maintaining unassociated tracks in a grace buffer across a fixed window of missed frames [@zhang2022bytetrack]; query-based architectures like MOTR persist learned object queries across successive video frames [@zeng2022motr]. In either design, when an object is occluded or removed from the scene, the tracker enters the coasting state of @sec-perception-tracking-association and continues propagating its state vector $\\EstimatorStateVector = [\\CartesianPosition^\\top, \\Velocity^\\top]^\\top$ under an open-loop motion model. If retention rules are overly generous, the estimator maintains a persistent phantom entity in the state store. The path planner routes the manipulator around empty space, or an automated grasping routine closes its fingers on vacuum. Conversely, evicting an occluded obstacle prematurely creates a severe collision hazard when the arm sweeps through the physical space. Resolving phantom tracks requires moving beyond passive measurement absence or arbitrary heuristic timeouts to active negative evidence (@sec-memory-scene-attention-buffers). When a line-of-sight sensor ray sweeps the candidate volume and encounters zero density, raymarching actively evicts the phantom obstacle from the 3D state store as soon as line of sight confirms clearance. In manipulation, unfulfilled contact expectations (such as zero load at a predicted contact boundary) immediately trigger physical hypothesis invalidation.\n\nCoordinate frame misattributions divide along a similar boundary between syntax and semantics. A **schema-detectable frame mismatch** occurs when a publisher supplies a malformed frame identifier or a value outside a declared frame enumeration. Static schema enforcement can reject that packet at the serialization boundary. However, a well-formed identifier can still name an undefined link in the transformation tree graph. Resolving an otherwise well-formed identifier against the current transform tree requires a frame-aware validator or consumer; the transport schema alone does not establish that the named parent link exists. In contrast, a **semantically wrong frame assignment** occurs when an engineer or configuration file assigns a valid, type-correct frame identifier that represents the wrong physical link. For example, commanding the mobile manipulator&#x27;s fingertip trajectory using the transform of the arm&#x27;s bare mounting flange $\\FlangePose$ instead of the gripper&#x27;s tool tip $\\ToolTipPose$ satisfies every schema validation check, type assertion, and tree traversal test. In an illustrative setup with a tool tip `[MemoryFrozenTransform.d_tool_str]` beyond the flange along the tool $\\Glyph{z}$-axis, a flange-as-tip error shifts the intended contact point by that offset. Whether contact occurs depends on the fixture geometry and admitted path. Detecting a semantic frame error requires physical consistency checks, such as verifying measured motor torques against an analytical dynamic model of the arm, or cross-referencing multi-camera optical tracker observations against joint encoder kinematics.\n\nEven when the coordinate frame is assigned and verified, a fourth silent failure appears when sensor updates drop out and an estimator relies entirely on its predictive forward model to advance state estimates across the blind interval. A well-formulated filter propagates the mean state estimate $\\StateEstimate(\\TimeInstant)$ and covariance $\\EstimationErrorCovariance(\\TimeInstant)$ under its stated dynamics and process-noise model. The worked drift model grows position uncertainty during the gap; general covariance propagation need not be monotonic in every direction. An **overconfident predictor** understates process variance $\\ProcessNoiseCovariance$, holding $\\EstimationErrorCovariance(\\TimeInstant)$ artificially compact over an extended prediction duration, such as beyond an illustrative `[MemoryFrozenTransform.t_predict_long_str]`. When fresh sensor measurements eventually arrive, the artificially small covariance causes the filter to discount incoming observations; it weights its internal dead-reckoning so heavily that it classifies valid measurements as statistical outliers and rejects them. Engineers identify overconfidence during system qualification by executing staged synthetic occlusions, dropping sensor frames for a range of intervals and evaluating the innovation $\\MeasurementInnovation$ at reacquisition against the covariance $\\InnovationCovariance$ the filter predicted for it. These are the NIS and NEES tests of @sec-memory-estimator-consistency applied at the end of a gap rather than in steady state. Averaged over the staged trials, the NIS at reacquisition, and the NEES against ground truth, must fall inside their chi-squared acceptance intervals [@barshalom2001estimation], and an average above the interval indicates an overly optimistic process noise model. Drift along an unobservable degree of freedom, such as workpiece slippage inside compliant gripper pads, produces no reacquisition residual at all (@sec-memory-state-estimation-bounds). At vehicle scale, a terrain belief stands until the wheels themselves contradict it.\n\n::: {#ws-memory-spirit-rover-troy-entrapment .callout-war-story title=&quot;A rover trapped in sand (2009)&quot;}\n**Context**: By 2009 the *Spirit* rover had been driving for three years with an inoperative right-front wheel drive, dragging the dead wheel behind it as it drove backward on drives planned by operators on Earth. In the valley west of Home Plate, at a site the team named Troy, it drove onto soil that looked like the ground it had already crossed [@arvidson2010spirit].\n\n**Mechanism**: A thin sulfate-rich crust covered loose sulfate and basaltic sand. The surface&#x27;s appearance, which is all that orbital and rover cameras observe, carried no information about the shear strength underneath. Climbing soil-covered slopes on five working wheels already produced high slip, and at Troy the wheels broke through the crust and excavated the sand beneath instead of advancing the chassis [@arvidson2010spirit].\n\n**Impact**: *Spirit* became embedded in late April and early May 2009. Months of extrication attempts, rehearsed on an Earth testbed, failed, and in January 2010 the mission redesignated the rover a stationary research platform.\n\n**Response**: Slip detection already existed. Visual odometry, which compares the rover&#x27;s tracked motion with its commanded wheel rotation, had been used on both rovers to measure slip and stop drives when it exceeded a limit [@maimone2007two]. That measurement bounded how deep each drive could dig once slip appeared, but it could not warn before the wheels broke through.\n\n**Systems lesson**: Traversability inferred from appearance does not observe the property that matters, the soil&#x27;s strength, so a stored traversability belief can be well formed and wrong. Slip is the physical residual that exposes it, and the belief must expire the moment that residual departs from its bound.\n:::\n\nFrozen transforms, phantom tracks, misassigned frames, and overconfident predictors each demand distinct validation checks. The belief record of @sec-memory-handoff makes their provenance, age, frame, and growth assumptions available to consumers; independent physical checks still have to establish whether those claims hold. The filled schema in the next section collects those obligations for the latch-slot finger and the moving mug.\n\n## The Belief Record Schema {#sec-memory-spatial-state-schema}\n\nThe belief record introduced in @sec-memory-handoff, the seventh record in the record chain, binds each state estimate to its capture epoch, coordinate frame, and error-growth law so that downstream controllers can catch a frozen transform, a chimeric frame chain, or a prediction that has outlived its slot tolerance. This schema unifies discrete tracked entities and continuous volumetric representations (@tbl-09-schema-volumetric-belief) under one set of time and error rules, serving both trajectory planning on the host and bounded local permission checks on real-time microcontrollers. From the observation contracts established in @sec-perception-observation-contract, the record inherits the raw sensor capture epoch, clock identifier, and hardware conversion bound. For differently constrained state components, it preserves separate evidence epochs or a conservative shared epoch and growth bound, as required by @sec-memory-state-epoch. With these fields, the safety permission controller translates the sensor&#x27;s capture timestamp into its own local monotonic clock with bounded synchronization error before evaluating evidence age. Persistent memory contributes five fields of its own. An estimate epoch dates the propagated state without renewing physical evidence. An uncertainty-growth bound projects error expansion across blind intervals. A validity region defines the operating envelope where that bound holds. The record also carries a physical invalidation deadline. Finally, a query-work allocation is set to at most `[RepresentationQuery.t_query_str]` per local collision query in the reference design, and it becomes a justified execution bound only after qualification on the chosen hardware. Across its lifetime, each record moves through the four-state lifecycle of @fig-09-spatial-belief-state-machine, whose guards enforce two rules. Only fresh evidence returns a record to `VALID`, and leaving `UNOBSERVABLE` also requires restoring the missing physical constraint.\n\n::: {#fig-09-spatial-belief-state-machine fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;tb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Spatial belief state machine and transition invariants**: Each state carries its invariant inside the box and each edge its guard. Occlusion moves a record from VALID to DEGRADED, where the error bound grows under the declared law, and the bound reaching $\\\\PositionalTaskTolerance$ or a breach of the validity region moves it to EXPIRED. Every edge back to VALID passes the same evidence guard, so freshness alone cannot restore observability and extrapolation cannot renew validity; leaving UNOBSERVABLE also needs the missing constraint restored.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;State diagram with four boxes. VALID, labeled measured, E at most e max, leads by an occlusion arrow to DEGRADED, labeled predicted, E of delta t at most e max, which leads by an arrow labeled E of delta t equals e max or region breach to EXPIRED, labeled supports no motion. Two rails above return from DEGRADED and EXPIRED to VALID under the guard fresh evidence and innovation gate and model revalidated. A bus from all three states, labeled observability lost from any state, leads to UNOBSERVABLE, labeled rank-deficient state, below VALID, and an arrow labeled constraint restored plus same guard returns from UNOBSERVABLE to VALID.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nThe transitions enforce strict physical invariants across the spatial cache:\n\n1. `VALID` $\\to$ `DEGRADED`: Occurs upon sensor occlusion or field-of-view exit when direct measurement ceases, but the declared growth law still supports the task. Downstream consumers expand safety margins according to that law: stochastic models require calibrated coverage percentiles, whereas bounded-disturbance models enforce worst-case physical limits.\n2. `DEGRADED` $\\to$ `EXPIRED`: Occurs deterministically when projected uncertainty breaches task tolerance ($\\BeliefErrorBound(\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant) \\ge \\PositionalTaskTolerance$) or operating conditions leave the declared validity region. The entry immediately ceases to support dependent motion commands. Safety controllers must always reserve a feasible fallback trajectory in advance, because invalidating a software record cannot arrest physical momentum.\n3. `ANY` $\\to$ `UNOBSERVABLE`: Occurs immediately when physical observability rank collapses, such as unconstrained sensor bias drift under thermal changes or mechanical slippage along unmonitored degrees of freedom.\n4. `ANY` $\\to$ `VALID`: Requires fresh physical evidence ($\\EvidenceEpoch = \\FreshEvidenceTime$), innovation residuals passing gating checks, and revalidation of the dynamic model within the operating regime. Returning from `UNOBSERVABLE` additionally requires a restored physical constraint that resolves the rank deficiency; acquiring another ambiguous sample from the unconstrained sensor is insufficient. A belief record can never transition from `DEGRADED` or `EXPIRED` back to `VALID` through predictive extrapolation, autoregressive rollout, or software republishing.\n\n@Tbl-09-invalidation-epochs instantiates this schema for the latch-slot finger as a concrete bounded-disturbance belief record, and the prose that follows pairs it with the linear-drift record for the moving mug handle. Together, the two records show how the same fourteen fields accommodate both accelerating manipulation errors and constant-velocity transport bounds.\n\n\\Needspace{28\\baselineskip}\n\n| Field                              | Example record value                                                                                                                                           | Consumer check                                                                                                                   |\n|:-----------------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Entity and quantity                | Gripper finger relative to the latch-release slot, lateral offset from the slot centerline                                                                     | Match the target entity and task quantity                                                                                        |\n| Value and SI unit                  | Lateral offset, stored in meters; the bounds below are shown in millimeters for reading                                                                        | Decode as meters, not millimeters or force                                                                                       |\n| Frame                              | `latch_slot` frame, with the transform revision of the wrist-camera calibration                                                                                | Resolve a time-valid transform chain                                                                                             |\n| Evidence epoch                     | $\\EvidenceEpoch$, the last wrist-camera capture before the finger occludes the slot, on the wrist camera&#x27;s capture clock                                       | Preserve across prediction and republishing                                                                                      |\n| Estimate epoch                     | Latest propagation of the offset, on the same capture clock                                                                                                    | Date the propagated estimate; do not renew evidence                                                                              |\n| Clock conversion                   | Versioned $\\MicrocontrollerClockTime=\\ClockConversionSlope \\CaptureTimestamp+\\ClockConversionIntercept$, error at most `[LatchSlotBelief.eps_sync_str]` | Refuse if conversion expires; add error to upper age                                                                             |\n| Initial error                      | $\\InitialTrackingError$ = `[LatchSlotBelief.e0_str]`                                                                                                    | Check the initial bound against calibration                                                                                      |\n| Growth law                         | `[LatchSlotBelief.growth_law_math]`                                                                                                                     | Compare at evidence age with the slot tolerance                                                                                  |\n| Validity region                    | Arm acceleration and contact mode unchanged since $\\EvidenceEpoch$; drift and disturbance bounds above validated within this region                            | Expire immediately on a mode or residual-bound violation                                                                         |\n| Expiry                             | $\\EvidenceEpoch$ + `[LatchSlotBelief.t_exp_str]`, where the growth law reaches the `[LatchSlotBelief.e_max_str]` slot tolerance                  | Refuse dependent proposals at expiry; reserve time for an earlier physical response                                              |\n| Provenance                         | Wrist-camera observation ID, calibration revision, estimator build hash                                                                                        | Match approved sources; hash is version provenance, not payload integrity                                                        |\n| Lifecycle                          | `VALID` at capture, `DEGRADED` once the finger occludes the slot, `EXPIRED` at $\\EvidenceEpoch$ + `[LatchSlotBelief.t_exp_str]`                         | Never restore `VALID` without fresh physical evidence                                                                            |\n| Map cell evidence, when applicable | Not used by this record; a map belief such as the rack-end cross aisle carries a per-cell capture epoch and free/occupied/unknown state                        | Check a validated conservative snapshot certificate covering every supporting cell; unknown stays unknown                        |\n| Collision query budget             | $\\ClearanceQueryTime \\le$ `[RepresentationQuery.t_query_str]` (qualified local clearance and required gradient lookup)                                  | Qualify at most `[RepresentationQuery.t_query_str]` per local query and the full collision workload within its allocation |\n\n: **Belief record**: Value, unit, entity, frame, two epochs, clock conversion, error model, validity region, expiry, query latency budget, provenance, and lifecycle travel together as the seventh record in the record chain. A later estimate epoch dates the represented state, not a new measurement, and cannot extend expiry past $\\EvidenceEpoch + \\BeliefInvalidationHorizon$. {#tbl-09-invalidation-epochs tbl-colwidths=&quot;[23,39,38]&quot;}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ MUG-HANDLE BELIEF ON THE CONVEYOR</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-memory-spatial-state-schema (second filled belief, the one</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @sec-intent's lease consumes).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    Fill the belief record for the mug's handle on the takeaway</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          conveyor beside the latch-slot finger.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    initial error, conveyor drift bound, grasp tolerance, expiry.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     E(t) = E0 + v_conveyor t (the belt speed bounds drift, so a = 0);</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          expiry is the root of E(t) = grasp tolerance.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.core.units import meter, millimeter, second</p>\n<p>AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class MugHandleBelief:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>e0 = AISLE.grasp_initial_error  # category: illustrative (handle-pose error at the wrist capture) v_conv = AISLE.v_conveyor  # category: illustrative (takeaway-conveyor speed bound) e_max = AISLE.grasp_tolerance  # category: chosen (task-profile grasp tolerance) t_use = Scenarios.ConveyorMugHandleBelief.use_age  # category: illustrative (capture-to-use age on the consumer's clock) eps_sync = LatchSlotBelief.eps_sync  # category: illustrative (clock-conversion bound, as in the latch record)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>R = Scenarios.ConveyorMugHandleBelief.result t_exp = R.horizon age_upper = R.use_age_upper e_use = R.error_at_use e_margin = R.margin_at_use t_left = R.time_left t_latest = R.latest_use_age</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(e0.to(millimeter).magnitude - 3.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Handle initial error should be 3 mm, got {e0}.\") check(abs(e_max.to(millimeter).magnitude - 15.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Grasp tolerance should be 15 mm, got {e_max}.\") check(abs(v_conv.to(meter / second).magnitude - 0.20) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Conveyor speed should be 0.20 m/s, got {v_conv}.\") check(abs(t_exp.magnitude - 60.0) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Mug-handle expiry should be 60 ms, got {t_exp}.\") check(LatchSlotBelief.t_exp &gt; t_exp, \"The mug handle must expire before the latch-slot finger.\") check(e_use &lt; e_max and t_left.magnitude &gt; 0, \"The belief must pass admission at the stated use instant.\") check(abs(e_use.magnitude - 10.04) &lt; 1e-9, f\"Error at use should be 10.04 mm, got {e_use}.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>e0_str = fmt_length(e0, unit=millimeter, precision=0) v_conv_str = fmt_velocity(v_conv, unit=meter / second, precision=2) e_max_str = fmt_length(e_max, unit=millimeter, precision=0) t_exp_str = fmt_latency(t_exp, unit=millisecond, precision=0) t_use_str = fmt_latency(t_use, unit=millisecond, precision=0) e_margin_str = fmt_length(e_margin, unit=millimeter, precision=2) t_left_str = fmt_latency(t_left, unit=millisecond, precision=1) t_latest_str = fmt_latency(t_latest, unit=millisecond, precision=1) _ms = lambda q, p=1: fmt_magnitude(q, millisecond, precision=p) age_math = fmt_display_math(rf\"\\Delta t_{{\\text{{upper}}}} = {_ms(t_use, 0)} + {_ms(eps_sync)} = {_ms(age_upper)}\\ \\text{{ms}}\") use_math = fmt_display_math( rf\"E = {fmt_magnitude(e0, millimeter, precision=0)}\\ \\text{{mm}} + {fmt_magnitude(v_conv, millimeter / millisecond, precision=1)}\\ \\text{{mm/ms}} \\times {_ms(age_upper)}\\ \\text{{ms}} = {fmt_magnitude(e_use, millimeter, precision=2)}\\ \\text{{mm}} \\le {fmt_magnitude(e_max, millimeter, precision=0)}\\ \\text{{mm}}\" ) left_math = fmt_display_math(rf\"{_ms(t_exp, 0)} - {_ms(age_upper)} = {_ms(t_left)}\\ \\text{{ms}}\") ]```</p>\n<p>The same schema governs the target belief consumed during grasping, the six-degree-of-freedom pose of a mug handle as the container advances along a takeaway conveyor toward the robotic manipulator. This record anchors its pose in the robot base frame, registers its evidence epoch to the wrist camera's final pre-occlusion capture, and synchronizes clocks under the same <code>[LatchSlotBelief.eps_sync_str]</code> conversion bound. At capture, calibration limits initial pose error to $\\InitialTrackingError =$ <code>[MugHandleBelief.e0_str]</code>. Its error-growth law differs from that of the latch-slot finger. Because the conveyor belt advances at a bounded velocity of at most <code>[MugHandleBelief.v_conv_str]</code> without independent acceleration, unobserved positional drift accumulates linearly over time according to @eq-mem-linear-horizon, reaching the <code>[MugHandleBelief.e_max_str]</code> grasp tolerance at $\\EvidenceEpoch +$ <code>[MugHandleBelief.t_exp_str]</code>. That is the freshness deadline @sec-body-measurement-freshness derived for this same mug, from the same numbers; what memory adds is that the deadline now travels inside the record, beside the evidence epoch it is measured from. The validity region requires that the belt run forward within this rated speed and that the mug remain upright and seated. Any operational violation (a conveyor stall, a speed surge, or a shock that tips the mug) expires the record immediately regardless of elapsed time. In the worked tracked-evidence model, an active visual tracker compensates the nominal conveyor motion, leaving residual belt slip as the declared growth term. The tighter horizon requires qualification of the tracker’s initial error, capture-to-use age, and residual dynamics; frame rate alone cannot establish it. This is the tracked evidence horizon met in @sec-brain-two-proposers and carried into the intent lease in @sec-intent-goals-expire.</p>\n<p>Downstream consumers evaluate either record through an invariant admission procedure. The controller converts the sensor capture epoch to its local clock, adds the synchronization uncertainty margin to the elapsed age, verifies the lifecycle status and validity region, and evaluates the growth law against the clearance budget of the proposed trajectory. Continuous-field representations follow an equivalent procedure, evaluating local signed-distance or occupancy snapshots against spatial clearance bounds within their qualified query allocations. Passing this admission check admits the belief to the <strong>separate permission decision</strong>; it does not command actuator motion. When an updated record fails admission or expires, an active motion command may continue only until the expiry of the record on which it was admitted. If that expiry arrives before a refreshed record does, the real-time controller must commit to a verified fallback maneuver before physical margins are exhausted. Notebook \\ref{nbk-memory-admission-margin} walks through this admission evaluation for the mug-handle belief.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A trajectory proposal requests closing the gripper on the mug handle <code>[MugHandleBelief.t_use_str]</code> after the evidence epoch, measured on the consumer's local clock. Does the spatial belief remain admissible for the grasp at that instant, and how much validity margin remains?</p>\n<p><strong>Variables</strong>:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Initial error $\\InitialTrackingError =$ <code>[MugHandleBelief.e0_str]</code> and conveyor speed bound $\\ConveyorSpeedBound =$ <code>[MugHandleBelief.v_conv_str]</code> (both illustrative); grasp tolerance $\\PositionalTaskTolerance =$ <code>[MugHandleBelief.e_max_str]</code> (chosen); expiry $\\BeliefInvalidationHorizon =$ <code>[MugHandleBelief.t_exp_str]</code> (derived from @eq-mem-linear-horizon).</li>\n<li>Clock-conversion bound $\\ClockConversionBound =$ <code>[LatchSlotBelief.eps_sync_str]</code> (illustrative) and execution age <code>[MugHandleBelief.t_use_str]</code> (illustrative).</li>\n<li>Lifecycle <code>DEGRADED</code>, with validity region intact: the belt advances forward within nominal limits and the mug remains seated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: The consumer charges the clock synchronization bound to the elapsed age, evaluates the growth law at this upper bound, and computes the remaining belief lifetime: <code>[MugHandleBelief.age_math]</code> <code>[MugHandleBelief.use_math]</code> <code>[MugHandleBelief.left_math]</code></p>\n<p><strong>Result</strong>: The belief passes admission at the stated instant, retaining <code>[MugHandleBelief.e_margin_str]</code> of clearance margin and <code>[MugHandleBelief.t_left_str]</code> of belief lifetime; the limiting evidence-use age is <code>[MugHandleBelief.t_latest_str]</code> after the evidence epoch.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Belief admission supplies a time-bounded input to the separate motion-permission decision. No grasp duration has been supplied, so the limiting evidence-use age is not a guaranteed completion time. The motion controller must establish a feasible completion or fallback before physical margins are exhausted within the remaining <code>[MugHandleBelief.t_left_str]</code>. If an external disturbance knocks the mug loose, the record expires immediately despite valid timestamps, because the physical system has breached the validity region for which the growth bound was derived.</p>\n<p>These items close the chapter's body: the epochs a record carries, the deadline that ends it, the states no estimator can recover, and the lifecycle and admission check that govern all three.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Epoch classification</strong>: Distinguish the evidence epoch ($\\EvidenceEpoch$) from the estimate epoch ($\\EstimateEpoch$) and publication epoch ($\\PublicationEpoch$). Why does stamping an estimate with its publication time conceal the physical error accumulated since capture?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Admission margin</strong>: For the mug-handle record, why does the consumer add the clock-conversion bound to the elapsed age before evaluating the growth law, and what must the controller do with the remaining lifetime once admission passes?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Unobservable manifold hazards</strong>: Explain why vanishing innovation residuals ($\\ForceSensorReading - \\PredictedForceReading = 0$) fail to reveal sensor bias drift (such as thermal expansion from nearby actuators) along unobservable degrees of freedom.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>State lifecycle state machine</strong>: Trace the deterministic transitions between <code>VALID</code>, <code>DEGRADED</code>, <code>EXPIRED</code>, and <code>UNOBSERVABLE</code>. Why does renewing a record require fresh physical evidence, and why does recovering from <code>UNOBSERVABLE</code> require restoring an underlying physical constraint?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Belief record</strong>: Enumerate the required fields of the belief record (the seventh record in the book's architecture), including hardware capture epochs, qualified collision-query allocations, and task-space validity regions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h2\">Fallacies and Pitfalls</h2>\n<p>Evaluating a spatial memory architecture requires auditing how separate state beliefs interact across time, coordinate frames, and physical occupancy. In physical AI, planning failures frequently occur when systems treat software records as static facts rather than time-decaying physical hypotheses. Two records can each be internally valid and fresh according to local timestamps, yet combining them without temporal reconciliation yields an inconsistent or hazardous model of the workspace. Before authorizing mechanical motion, a real-time controller must reconcile disparate sensor epochs, velocity bounds, and physical occlusion boundaries.</p>", "quizzes": []};
